extension Parser
	// ⭐⭐ **`extension <Interface> … end` — MONOMORPHIZED BY RE-PARSE, ONE PASS PER CONFORMING TYPE.**
	//
	// An extension method has ONE source body and must become a CONCRETE method of every type whose
	// `implements` clause names the interface, because the compiler dispatches statically: `list.count()` is an
	// ordinary direct call to `IntList.count`, and `IntList.count` has to exist as a function. So the body's
	// TOKENS are parsed again for each conformer, with `enclosingType` set to that conformer — which is
	// exactly the state `type IntList`'s own body would have been parsed in. Nothing downstream learns that
	// extensions exist: `declaredMethodName` names the method `IntList.count`, `enclosingSelfType` types the
	// receiver `structRef(IntList)`, and the call site resolves through the ordinary method door.
	//
	// ⭐ **RE-PARSE RATHER THAN IR-CLONE.** Capturing the body once and cloning it per substitution
	// means hunting down every construct inside the clone whose identity is per-substitution — a
	// closure-specialization drain, and a panic on *"closure '_$closure_N' referenced from a
	// monomorphized extension body"* when one is missed. Re-parsing has NO clone and therefore no such
	// hunt: every ValueId, every lifted closure and every temporary is MINTED FRESH by the parser, in
	// the conformer's own scope. A closure inside `callTag` lifts to `Three.callTag$closure_0` on one
	// pass and `Seven.callTag$closure_0` on the next — two distinct functions, each with its own
	// `tag()` resolved against its own receiver — because the lifted name is derived from the
	// ENCLOSING FUNCTION's name, which is already per-conformer. The substitution-dependent-closure
	// miscompile is unrepresentable rather than cured. It costs one parse of the body per conformer,
	// which is exactly the code that has to be generated.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BODY IS NOT PARSED AT ALL WHEN NOTHING CONFORMS**, so an extension on an interface no type
	// implements is unchecked. That is the same bargain a generic body strikes in a monomorphizing compiler,
	// and the alternative — checking it against a `Self` that has no type — is not available.
	//
	// ⚠ The cursor is re-seated from the body start each pass and, at the end, walked to the declaration's own
	// `end` through `advanceToOutermostBlockEnd` — the walk the sweep's `skipPastBlockEnd` takes, so the real
	// parse and the sweep cannot disagree about where this declaration stops — and the label behind it is
	// judged there. That also makes the zero-conformer case need no arm of its own.
	//
	// An `export`/`public` modifier on the `extension` keyword is consumed by `dispatchTopLevel` and means
	// nothing here: an extension declares no name to make visible, and each of its METHODS carries its own
	// visibility exactly as a `type`'s members do (`parseTypeMember`).
	function parseExtensionDeclaration() throws SourceParseError
		let header = try self.readExtensionHeader()

		// ⛔⛔ **`extension byte` IS REFUSED HERE, AND THE REFUSAL IS DERIVED RATHER THAN SPELLED.**
		// `namesAPrimitiveType` admits FOUR names because that is the set with STATICS (`byte.fromString`);
		// `primitiveTagOfTypeName` admits THREE because that is the set with a VALUE TAG, and only the second
		// set can be extended — `byte` names no type in the compiler at all, and a byte VALUE is an ordinary `int`
		// range (`typealias Byte = int(0 to u8.max)`, `stdlib/Character.maxon:12`) which reaches every receiver
		// door tagged `integer`. So an `extension byte` body would publish `byte.<method>` symbols nothing can
		// ever dispatch.
		//
		// ⚠ **WITHOUT THIS `byte` FALLS TO `undeclaredTarget` AND IS REFUSED AS AN UNKNOWN TYPE NAME (E3011),
		// which denies a name the compiler DOES recognize as a primitive.** An unknown name or a typealias is
		// refused below; this refusal says why the one primitive name that cannot be extended cannot.
		//
		// ⇒ Written as the DIFFERENCE between the two tables, not as `b"byte"`: the day a fifth primitive
		// joins `namesAPrimitiveType` without a value representation it is refused here automatically, and the
		// day `byte` acquires one it is admitted by `extensionTargetOf` and never reaches this line.
		if namesAPrimitiveType(header.nameTok.value) and primitiveTagOfTypeName(header.nameTok.value) == ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'aPrimitiveWithNoValuesOfItsOwn'
			throw SourceParseError.unsupported("`extension {String.from(header.nameTok.value)}` — `{String.from(header.nameTok.value)}` is the qualifier of a static (`{String.from(header.nameTok.value)}.fromString`) and names no type of its own, so it has no values for these methods to be dispatched on; a byte-sized value is an `int` range (`typealias Byte = int(0 to u8.max)`), so extend `int`", line: header.nameTok.line, column: header.nameTok.column)
		end 'aPrimitiveWithNoValuesOfItsOwn'

		match header.target 'target'
			undeclaredTarget then throw self.undeclaredExtensionTargetRefusal(header.nameTok)
			interfaceExtension or
			typeExtension then break
		end 'target'

		let bodyStart = self.pos
		let conformers = self.admittedExtensionConformers(header)
		try self.parseExtensionBodies(header, conformers: conformers, bodyStart: bodyStart)

		self.publishPendingBareAliases(self.artifact.functionTypeAliases)
		self.pos = bodyStart
		self.advanceToOutermostBlockEnd()
		try self.consumeBlockEnd(header.nameTok.value, labelIsRequired: false)
	end 'parseExtensionDeclaration'

	// The conformer list and the per-conformer verdict are `readExtensionHeader`'s and
	// `extensionConformerVerdict`'s — the SAME answers, in the SAME order, that
	// `foldExtensionDeclarationInto` published a signature for. A body emitted here with no signature
	// published there is a call that cannot be typed; a signature with no body is a link failure — and a
	// CONDITIONAL extension adds a third way for the two to disagree, so the filter is one function both
	// call rather than a rule each applies.
	function admittedExtensionConformers(header ExtensionHeader) returns NameRoster
		let admitted = NameRoster.create(0)

		for conformer in extensionTargetConformers(header.target) 'eachConformer'
			match self.extensionConformerVerdict(header, conformer: conformer, index: self.signatures) 'verdict'
				// A withheld method has no signature, so a body emitted for it would be a function nothing can
				// call — and its own statements would not type, which is the whole reason the clause exists.
				unmet then continue
				satisfied then admitted.push(conformer)
			end 'verdict'
		end 'eachConformer'

		return admitted
	end 'admittedExtensionConformers'

	// Every conformer reads each method body under ONE region key, and a refusal raised by any
	// conformer abandons the method for every conformer, whatever their order. A body first refused
	// under a later conformer has already been emitted for the earlier ones, so it sets
	// `extensionReplayOwed` and the conformer loop replays from the extension's mark with every
	// abandoned method skipped.
	function parseExtensionBodies(header ExtensionHeader, conformers NameRoster, bodyStart ElementIndex) throws SourceParseError
		if not self.recoversRegions() or conformers.count() < 2 'eachBodyRecoversOnItsOwn'
			try self.parseEachExtensionConformer(header, conformers: conformers, bodyStart: bodyStart)
			return
		end 'eachBodyRecoversOnItsOwn'

		let mark = self.takeRewindMark()

		while true 'attempt'
			self.extensionReplayOwed = false

			try self.parseEachExtensionConformer(header, conformers: conformers, bodyStart: bodyStart)

			if not self.extensionReplayOwed 'everyConformerReadTheSameBodies'
				break
			end 'everyConformerReadTheSameBodies'

			self.rewindTo(mark)
		end 'attempt'
	end 'parseExtensionBodies'

	function parseEachExtensionConformer(header ExtensionHeader, conformers NameRoster, bodyStart ElementIndex) throws SourceParseError
		for conformer in conformers 'eachConformer'
			// ⭐ **THE EXTENSION'S OWN `implements` IS RECORDED HERE, PAST THE VERDICT, AND THAT POSITION IS THE
			// WHOLE MEANING OF A CONDITIONAL CONFORMANCE.** `extension Array implements Hashable where Element
			// is Hashable` says the conformance holds for the conformers that satisfy the clause and for no
			// others; recorded before the verdict it would claim `Hashable` for every one of them, which is the
			// same sentence with the condition deleted. The `unmet` arm above has already `continue`d, so a
			// conformer reaching this line is one the clause admits.
			//
			// It is the REAL PARSE that records it and not the fold, for the reason the fold publishes only
			// signatures: `recordConformance` writes the per-file artifact `checkConformance` later reads, and
			// the fold runs once per declaration over a shared index that has no file to attribute it to.
			self.recordExtensionConformance(header, conformer: conformer)

			self.pos = bodyStart
			self.enterTypeScopeOf(conformer)
			self.openExtensionBodyScope(header, conformer: conformer, index: self.signatures, pass: ExtensionBodyPass.parse)

			// Where the sibling-receiver walk begins for a bare call in these bodies — the EXTENSION's member
			// list, not the conformer's, because a bare call here may name a sibling extension method. What
			// the conformer itself declares is NOT on this walk and comes from the whole-program index
			// (`isImplicitSelfMethod`); the extended interface's own requirements are added to the map by
			// `ensureSiblingReceivers`, which is what makes a bare `tag()` resolve against a REQUIREMENT the
			// index files under no conformer. See `extensionInterfaceName`.
			self.enclosingTypeBodyStart = bodyStart
			self.enclosingBodyIsWalkable = true
			self.extensionInterfaceName = self.extendedInterfaceName(header)

			while true 'members'
				let atEnd = try self.atDeclarationBodyEnd()

				if atEnd 'closes'
					break
				end 'closes'

				// ⭐ **A SHADOWED METHOD IS STEPPED OVER WHOLE** — the conformer declares its own, so the
				// fold published no signature for this one and a body emitted here would be a second definition
				// under the same symbol. The DECISION is the fold's, read back rather than re-derived: by now the
				// index also holds the extension's OWN published names, so `conformerDeclaresOwnMethod` can no
				// longer tell them apart. See `ProgramSignatures.extensionPublishedMethods`.
				// ⭐⭐ **AND A STDLIB MEMBER A USER FILE DECLARES TOO IS STEPPED OVER FOR THE SAME REASON** —
				// the user's `extension int` wears the name (`ProgramSignatures.noteExtensionPublishedMethod`),
				// so a body emitted here is a second definition under one symbol, which is `E3006` at the merge.
				// The provenance test is THIS parser's own: the set is keyed by name alone, and the file that
				// WON must go on emitting its body.
				if let memberName = try self.extensionMemberMethodNameAt(self.pos) 'aMethod'
					let extensionMemberName = qualifiedMethodName(conformer, methodName: memberName)
					let memberTakesReceiver = self.extensionMemberTakesReceiverAt(self.pos)
					let shadowed = self.signatures.extensionMethodIsShadowed(extensionMemberName, takesReceiver: memberTakesReceiver)

					if not shadowed and not extensionTargetIsAnInterface(header.target) and self.signatures.typeDeclaresField(conformer, fieldName: memberName) 'nameTheTypeGivesAField'
						let fieldMemberTok = self.tokenAt(pastDeclarationModifiers(self.tokens, index: self.pos) + 1)
						throw SourceParseError.duplicateDeclaration(fieldAndMethodMessage(memberName, typeName: conformer), line: fieldMemberTok.line, column: fieldMemberTok.column)
					end 'nameTheTypeGivesAField'

					if shadowed and not extensionTargetIsAnInterface(header.target) 'hidesTheTypesOwnMember'
						let memberNameTok = self.tokenAt(pastDeclarationModifiers(self.tokens, index: self.pos) + 1)
						throw SourceParseError.duplicateDeclaration(duplicateDeclarationMessage(extensionMemberName), line: memberNameTok.line, column: memberNameTok.column)
					end 'hidesTheTypesOwnMember'

					if shadowed 'anotherDeclarationWearsTheName'
						self.skipPastExtensionMember()
						continue
					end 'anotherDeclarationWearsTheName'
				end 'aMethod'

				// An extension member is a TYPE member — same visibility, same `static`, same `function`.
				// A `var`/`let` is refused by that reader (`requireMemberListDeclaresStorage`), which is the
				// right answer: an extension adds behaviour, never storage, so there is no layout for a field
				// to join and no declaration for a `static` binding's slot to belong to.
				try self.parseTypeMember(MemberList.extensionBody)
			end 'members'

			self.closeTypeParamScope()
			self.extensionInterfaceName = InterfaceName.none
			self.readsALaterConformer = true
		end 'eachConformer'

		self.readsALaterConformer = false
	end 'parseEachExtensionConformer'

	function undeclaredExtensionTargetRefusal(nameTok Token) returns SourceParseError
		let written = self.signatures.aliasIdentityDisplay(nameTok.value)

		match self.signatures.declaredTypeNameIn(nameTok.value, readerFilePath: self.job.path) 'declaration'
			undeclared then return SourceParseError.unknownTypeName(written, line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			declared then return SourceParseError.unsupported("`extension {written}` — '{written}' is a typealias, which declares no methods of its own; extend the `type`, `enum`, `union`, `interface` or primitive it names", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'declaration'
	end 'undeclaredExtensionTargetRefusal'

	// ⭐ **THE METHOD NAME AN EXTENSION MEMBER STARTING AT `index` DECLARES, as a PEEK** —
	// `AbsentError` for a nested typealias, for a malformed member, and for anything that is not a
	// method, so the one caller can ask unconditionally. It mirrors the header order the fold and
	// `parseTypeMember` both read — an optional visibility modifier, an optional `static`, then
	// `function <name>` — because the shadow decision has to be taken BEFORE the member is consumed,
	// and a member the decision skips is stepped over whole rather than parsed.
	function extensionMemberMethodNameAt(index ElementIndex) returns immutable ByteArray throws AbsentError
		let at = pastDeclarationModifiers(self.tokens, index: index)

		if not self.functionDeclarationAt(at) 'notAMethod'
			throw AbsentError.absent
		end 'notAMethod'

		return self.tokenAt(at + 1).value
	end 'extensionMemberMethodNameAt'

	function extensionInnerAliasSiteAt(nameTok Token) returns ExtensionInnerAliasSite
		return ExtensionInnerAliasSite.create(self.job.path, nameLine: nameTok.line, nameColumn: nameTok.column)
	end 'extensionInnerAliasSiteAt'

	function requireExtensionInnerAliasKept(nameTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		if self.job.isStdlibSource 'theLibraryDeclaresMembersTwice'
			return
		end 'theLibraryDeclaresMembersTwice'

		let qualified = joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingTypeName(), member: nameTok.value)

		if let keeper = try self.signatures.extensionInnerAliasKeeper(qualified) 'someExtensionDeclaresIt'
			if self.extensionInnerAliasSiteAt(nameTok).compare(keeper) != Ordering.equalTo 'aLaterDeclaration'
				throw self.duplicateNestedAliasRefusal(nameTok, qualified: qualified, declaredBy: "by an `extension` in {keeper.declFilePath.filename()}")
			end 'aLaterDeclaration'
		end 'someExtensionDeclaresIt'
	end 'requireExtensionInnerAliasKept'

	// True for an interface extension's member that the conformer's own body also declares: the member is
	// read and discarded, so the conformer's declaration is the one every body compiled for it resolves.
	function extensionAliasYieldsToTheTypesOwn(nameTok Token) returns bool throws SourceParseError
		let typeName = self.enclosingTypeName()
		let qualified = joinQualifiedName(typeName, member: nameTok.value)

		if not self.signatures.typeBodyDeclaresInnerAlias(qualified) 'noBodyDeclaresIt'
			try self.requireExtensionInnerAliasKept(nameTok)
			return false
		end 'noBodyDeclaresIt'

		if not self.extensionInterfaceName.isNamed() 'aTypeExtension'
			throw self.duplicateNestedAliasRefusal(nameTok, qualified: qualified, declaredBy: "in the body of `{String.from(typeName)}`")
		end 'aTypeExtension'

		return true
	end 'extensionAliasYieldsToTheTypesOwn'

	// Every bare member name of the enclosing body becomes a registry key through `innerAliasKey`, so the
	// program's member and a library member of the same name stay two keys; `innerAliasMemberFor` owns the rule.
	function authorsMemberName(member ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
		return self.signatures.innerAliasMemberFor(self.enclosingTypeName(), member: member, inExtensionBody: self.innerAliasesFromIndex, readerPath: self.job.path)
	end 'authorsMemberName'

	function innerAliasKey(member ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
		return joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingTypeName(), member: self.authorsMemberName(member))
	end 'innerAliasKey'

	function nestedRangedAlias(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns RangedTypeAlias
		let member = self.authorsMemberName(alias.name)

		if member.equals(alias.name) 'filedUnderItsOwnName'
			return alias
		end 'filedUnderItsOwnName'

		return alias.underName(member)
	end 'nestedRangedAlias'

	function duplicateNestedAliasRefusal(nameTok Token, qualified ByteArray, declaredBy String) returns SourceParseError
		return SourceParseError.registryRefused(ErrorCode.semanticDuplicateTypeAlias, explanation: "Duplicate typealias '{String.from(nameTok.value)}': `{String.from(qualified)}` is already declared {declaredBy}", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column, note: DiagnosticNote.none)
	end 'duplicateNestedAliasRefusal'

	function requireNestedAliasDeclaredOnce(nameTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		let declared = self.nestedAliasesDeclaredForWrite()

		if declared.contains(nameTok.value) 'aRedeclaration'
			let typeName = self.enclosingTypeName()
			throw self.duplicateNestedAliasRefusal(nameTok, qualified: joinQualifiedName(typeName, member: nameTok.value), declaredBy: "earlier in the body of `{String.from(typeName)}`")
		end 'aRedeclaration'

		declared.insert(nameTok.value)
	end 'requireNestedAliasDeclaredOnce'

	function nestedAliasesDeclaredForWrite() returns InnerAliasNameSet
		match self.declaredNestedAliases 'state'
			unwritten then break
			entries(table) then return table
		end 'state'

		let fresh = InnerAliasNameSet.create()
		self.declaredNestedAliases = InnerAliasRoster.entries(fresh)
		return fresh
	end 'nestedAliasesDeclaredForWrite'

	// The one test of an extension member's kind, past its visibility modifier. The fold's shadow note
	// and the real parse's query are both keyed by kind, so a second spelling of this test would file
	// them apart.
	function extensionMemberTakesReceiverAt(index ElementIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(pastVisibilityModifiers(self.tokens, index: index)) != TokenKind.static
	end 'extensionMemberTakesReceiverAt'

	// Step the cursor past a whole extension MEMBER, leaving it exactly where `parseTypeMember` would have.
	// The member's own `end` is found by `skipPastBlockEnd`, which needs the cursor INSIDE the one block that
	// `end` closes — so the `function` keyword is consumed first, exactly as every other walk over a method
	// enters it.
	function skipPastExtensionMember()
		while not self.isEof() and self.kindAt(self.pos) != TokenKind.function 'toTheKeyword'
			self.advance()
		end 'toTheKeyword'

		self.advance()
		self.skipPastBlockEnd()
	end 'skipPastExtensionMember'

	// The interface whose REQUIREMENTS are siblings of this body's methods — see `ensureSiblingReceivers`.
	// Empty for a TYPE extension, and that is the point: a type has no requirement list to add.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT EMPTY BECAUSE THE WALK ALREADY FINDS A TYPE'S OWN MEMBERS — IT DOES NOT, AND THIS COMMENT
	// SAID IT DID.** The walk starts at the EXTENSION's member list (`enclosingTypeBodyStart` is set to the
	// extension's body, one line above the assignment of this field), so `extension Box`'s bare `tag()` never
	// saw `type Box`'s `tag` at all. The conformer's own methods come from the whole-program index instead,
	// which is where they are for an INTERFACE extension's conformer too — see `isImplicitSelfMethod`. What a
	// requirement list adds on top is the one thing the index cannot: an interface's requirement is not filed
	// under the conformer, so a conformer that has not yet been reached by any declaration of it still
	// resolves `tag()` here.
	function extendedInterfaceName(header ExtensionHeader) returns InterfaceName
		match header.target 'target'
			interfaceExtension then return InterfaceName.named(header.nameTok.value)
			typeExtension or
			undeclaredTarget then return InterfaceName.none
		end 'target'
	end 'extendedInterfaceName'

	// ⭐ **`interface Name [extends …] [uses …] … end`** — the FRONT-END half of interfaces,
	// as the REAL parse reaches it. Its whole effect is the `IrInterface` it puts on this file's artifact, which
	// `mergeArtifact` folds into `project.interfaces` for `checkConformance` to verify every `implements`-ing
	// type against. It EMITS NOTHING: an interface declares a contract, not code — there are no witness tables,
	// no dispatch, no lowering.
	//
	// The READING is `readInterfaceDeclaration`'s, shared with the declaration sweep's second pass; what this
	// arm owns is the artifact, which the sweep has none of. See that function for why the two are split.
	function parseInterfaceDeclaration() throws SourceParseError
		let iface = try self.readInterfaceDeclaration(true)
		try self.requireNoUnstorableRequirementType()
		self.artifact.recordInterface(iface)
		self.contributeInterfaceSendErrors(iface.name)
	end 'parseInterfaceDeclaration'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE REFUSAL IS RECORDED BY THE SHARED READER AND THROWN ONLY HERE, AND THAT SPLIT IS FORCED.**
	// `readInterfaceDeclaration` has TWO callers: this one, the real parse, and `foldInterfaceDeclarationInto`,
	// which is TOLERANT — it swallows a `SourceParseError` and records nothing. Throwing from inside the reader would
	// therefore drop the interface out of the whole-program index whenever the fold reached it, and the fold
	// runs with `allFilesFolded` already set, so `parseTypeReference` CAN mint an `interfaceRef` there for any
	// interface folded earlier. The result would be order-dependent: the same program would lose a declaration
	// or not depending on which interface was declared first. So the reader notes and this throws.
	//
	// The message is `requireStorableDeclaredType`'s, through the same `DeclaredStoragePosition` door the five
	// other interface-typed positions use — see `interfaceStoragePositionMessage`.
	function requireNoUnstorableRequirementType() throws SourceParseError
		let refusal = self.statement.pendingRequirementParam.refusal()
		self.clearPendingRequirementParam()

		match refusal 'noted'
			none then return
			noted(interfaceName, position, line, column) then throw SourceParseError.unsupported(interfaceStoragePositionMessage(position, interfaceName: interfaceName), line: line, column: column)
		end 'noted'
	end 'requireNoUnstorableRequirementType'

	// Note an interface-typed slot of a requirement — a PARAMETER or the RETURN — for the real parse to
	// refuse. Only the FIRST: one refusal per declaration is what the author acts on, and the sibling
	// positions report one too.
	function noteUnstorableRequirementType(declaredType MaxonType, position DeclaredStoragePosition, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
		if self.statement.pendingRequirementParam.isNoted() 'alreadyNoted'
			return
		end 'alreadyNoted'

		if maxonTypeTag(declaredType) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
			return
		end 'notAnExistential'

		self.statement.pendingRequirementParam = PendingUnstorableRequirement.noted(self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(declaredType)), position: position, line: line, column: column)
	end 'noteUnstorableRequirementType'

	// The fold's counterpart to the throw above: it must leave no record behind for the NEXT declaration this
	// same reader parses to inherit.
	function clearPendingRequirementParam()
		self.statement.pendingRequirementParam = PendingUnstorableRequirement.none
	end 'clearPendingRequirementParam'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE READER OF AN `interface` DECLARATION** — cursor AT the `interface` keyword, landing past
	// the closing `end`. TWO parties build an `IrInterface` and they must build the SAME one: the real parse,
	// which records it on the file's artifact for `checkConformance` to read whole-program after every merge;
	// and `foldInterfaceDeclarationInto`, which records it in the declaration index for the PARSE-time witness
	// dispatch and `with`-arity readers. A second builder would be free to render a requirement's types
	// differently from this one, and a rendered type name is an IDENTITY here, not a caption — the failure mode
	// is a silent false accept on a conformance (`renderDeclaredTypeName`'s header carries the measurement).
	//
	// Method signatures have an implicit `self` (never declared), so `parseFunctionParameters` reads the USER
	// parameters only — exactly what the conformance check compares against an impl's parameters once it drops
	// the impl's prepended `__self`. `Self` in a signature resolves against `enclosingType` (set to the
	// interface name here), so the check can substitute the conforming type for it.
	function readInterfaceDeclaration(checksEndLabel bool) returns IrInterface throws SourceParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.interface)
		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
		try self.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned(nameTok, declares: DeclaredTypeMeaning.nominalIdentity)

		// An interface is neither a struct nor an enum, and `isEnum: false` is the right answer for the one
		// thing the kind decides: a `Self` in an interface method's signature is a `structRef`, which is what
		// `checkConformance` substitutes the CONFORMING type into.
		self.enterTypeScope(nameTok.value, isEnum: false)

		// Per DECLARATION, so the entries a previous interface in this file left behind cannot be folded under
		// this one's name. It points back at the shared empty rather than allocating, so an interface that
		// declares no generic-instance alias — every interface in the corpus but a handful — costs nothing.
		self.interfaceAliasScratch = self.anchors.interfaceAliases

		var extendsInterfaces = NameRoster.create(0) as isolated NameRoster
		var associatedTypeNames = NameRoster.create(0) as isolated NameRoster
		try self.parseInterfaceHeaderClauses(extendsInterfaces, associatedTypeNames: associatedTypeNames)
		try requireMemberCount(associatedTypeNames.count(), limit: MaxDeclaredMembers, members: "associated types", anchor: nameTok)

		// ⭐ **AN INTERFACE'S `uses` NAMES ARE ITS TYPE-PARAMETER SCOPE** — the same field a `type`'s
		// `uses` sets (`openTypeParamScope`), because the body asks the same question of it: inside
		// `interface InitableFromArrayLiteral uses Element`, a written `Element` denotes the associated type
		// and nothing else. Without it a `typealias ElementArray = Array with Element` below reads `Element`
		// as an ordinary `named` type, and `checkGenericArgType`'s rule 3 refuses it with **E3011 Unknown
		// type 'Element'** — a diagnostic against a name the interface's own header declares.
		// A `uses`-less interface keeps the SHARED empty list `enterTypeScope` just installed, so it
		// allocates nothing (`openTypeParamScope`'s discipline).
		let usesNames = freeze associatedTypeNames

		if usesNames.count() != 0 'hasAssociatedTypes'
			self.enclosingTypeParams = usesNames
		end 'hasAssociatedTypes'

		let methods = InterfaceMethodSigArray.create(0) as isolated InterfaceMethodSigArray
		let claimedRequirementNames = RequirementNameSet.create()

		while true 'members'
			self.skipNewlines()

			if self.at(TokenKind.end) 'closes'
				break
			end 'closes'

			if self.isEof() 'eof'
				let tok = self.current()
				throw SourceParseError.unexpectedEof(tok.line, column: tok.column)
			end 'eof'

			// ⭐ **AN ASSOCIATED-TYPE ALIAS** — `typealias ElementArray = Array with Element`, the shape
			// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:56` opens with. It is ACCEPTED AND RECORDED, never resolved or
			// substituted: `readNestedTypeAlias` enters the bare member into `enclosingInnerAliases`, so the
			// very next line's `static function init(value ElementArray)` reads a name this interface actually
			// DECLARES. Nothing SUBSTITUTES the instance for it, and nothing here needs to — an interface EMITS
			// NOTHING, and a requirement's parameter types exist only as the SOURCE STRINGS `checkConformance`
			// compares (`renderInterfaceTypeName`). Binding `Element := <arg>` at an `implements … with …` is
			// `checkConformance`'s, and `specs/associated-types.md` carries its cases.
			//
			// ⚠ **A SKIP TO END OF LINE WOULD PARSE HERE TOO, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED**: an
			// interface requirement's declared types are never RESOLVED, only rendered, so `static function
			// init(value Nonesuch)` — a requirement naming a type no file declares — compiles and runs clean
			// today, no diagnostic. The reason not to skip is therefore not that it fails; it is that it
			// works BY LEANING ON THAT HOLE. A skipped alias leaves `ElementArray` denoting nothing at all,
			// matching the conformer only because the string happens to be spelled the same in both files
			// (`stdlib/List.maxon:13`, `Set.maxon:14`), and the substitution has to resolve exactly
			// these types.
			//
			// Recording it costs one set insert and buys the alias a real meaning: `Array with Element` is
			// checked like any other instantiation, so `typealias A = Array with Nonesuch` inside an interface
			// is **E3011 Unknown type 'Nonesuch'** where a skip reports nothing. That check is also what forces
			// the `uses` scope installed above — without it the interface's own `Element` is the undeclared
			// name E3011 fires on.
			//
			// The whole-program shape is DISCARDED here for the reason `parseTypeMember` discards it: an
			// inner alias is keyed whole-program by the tolerant sweep, and the sweep records nothing of an
			// interface's BODY (`recordScannedInterface`) because an interface contributes no layout, no
			// signature and no emitted symbol for a later pass to look one up from. Its header's `uses`
			// arity is the one exception, because a parse reads it back.
			//
			// ⚠ A function alias is refused here though a `type` body takes one, and the asymmetry is the
			// sweep's, not the grammar's: a function alias is resolved through the whole-program
			// `signatures.functionTypeAliases`, which `foldFile` fills from the declaration sweep — and the
			// sweep reads no interface BODY at all (`recordScannedInterface` consumes the declaration whole).
			// Recorded from the real parse alone the name would be a function alias to `project` and an
			// unknown type to `parseTypeReference`, which is a wrong answer rather than a missing feature.
			if self.atTypeAliasDeclaration() 'associatedTypeAlias'
				let aliasTok = self.current()

				// ⚠ `unpublished`, for the reason the function form is refused (see `TupleAliasOwner`): the
				// declaration sweep reads no interface body, so a tuple alias keyed from the real parse alone
				// would resolve by which file the walk reached first. Read and validated here and entered into
				// `enclosingInnerAliases`, which is all a requirement's rendered signature needs.
				//
				// The generic-instance form's right-hand side is kept: discarded, a requirement would render
				// the unresolved `Holder.ElementArray` while every conformer's own alias renders a resolved
				// `Array_…`, and every conformer would be refused. A ranged inner alias erases to `integer` and
				// a tuple one resolves to the interned tuple's own `structRef`, so for those two the
				// requirement's rendered type is already the type.
				let aliasVisibility = try self.consumeOptionalVisibility()

				if checksEndLabel 'aRealParseRefusesARedeclaration'
					try self.requireNestedAliasDeclaredOnce(self.tokenAt(self.pos + 1))
				end 'aRealParseRefusesARedeclaration'

				match try self.readNestedTypeAlias(aliasVisibility, recordFunctionSignature: false, tupleOwner: TupleAliasOwner.unpublished) 'nested'
					ranged or
					tupleAlias then break
					genericInstance(entry) then self.noteInterfaceAlias(entry)
					functionAlias then throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a function typealias inside an `interface` body is not supported — the declaration sweep does not read an interface's members, so the alias would name a type only its own file could resolve; declare it at file scope", line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column)
				end 'nested'

				continue
			end 'associatedTypeAlias'

			let methodCount = methods.count()
			try requireMemberCount(methodCount + usesNames.count() + 1, limit: MaxDeclaredMembers, members: "methods and associated types", anchor: nameTok)
			let method = try self.parseInterfaceMethod(nameTok.value, claimed: claimedRequirementNames)
			methods.push(method)
		end 'members'

		// The fold reads the declaration tolerantly and must not drop an interface for its label; the real parse
		// of the declaring file is the one that refuses it.
		if checksEndLabel 'theRealParse'
			try self.consumeBlockEnd(nameTok.value, labelIsRequired: false)
		end 'theRealParse' else 'theFold'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.end)
			self.skipOptionalLabel()
		end 'theFold'

		self.closeTypeParamScope()
		let declared = IrInterface.create(nameTok.value, methods: freeze methods, extendsInterfaces: freeze extendsInterfaces, associatedTypeNames: usesNames, declFilePath: self.job.path)

		if checksEndLabel and declared.extendsInterfaces.count() > 0 'inheritsSlots'
			try requireMemberCount(interfaceWitnessSlots(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), iface: declared).count() + declared.associatedTypeNames.count(), limit: MaxDeclaredMembers, members: "witness slots (its own and its parents')", anchor: nameTok)
		end 'inheritsSlots'

		return declared
	end 'readInterfaceDeclaration'

	// The optional `extends P1, P2` and `uses A, B` clauses of an interface header, in either order. A
	// parent interface's methods a conforming type must ALSO supply (`checkConformance` projects them at
	// decl order); `uses` names associated types.
	// Stops at the first token that opens neither clause (a newline into the body).
	function parseInterfaceHeaderClauses(extendsInterfaces mutable isolated NameRoster, associatedTypeNames mutable isolated NameRoster) throws SourceParseError
		while true 'clauses'
			if self.at(TokenKind.extends) 'extendsClause'
				try self.consume(TokenKind.extends)
				try self.parseCommaSeparatedNames(extendsInterfaces, namesTypes: true)
				continue
			end 'extendsClause'

			if self.at(TokenKind.uses) 'usesClause'
				try self.consume(TokenKind.uses)
				try self.parseCommaSeparatedNames(associatedTypeNames, namesTypes: false)
				continue
			end 'usesClause'

			break
		end 'clauses'
	end 'parseInterfaceHeaderClauses'

	// A comma-separated list of identifiers (`A, B, C`) into `out` — the shared reader behind an
	// interface's `extends`/`uses` lists and a `type`'s `implements` list.
	function parseCommaSeparatedNames(out mutable isolated NameRoster, namesTypes bool) throws SourceParseError
		out.push(try self.consumeListedName(namesTypes))

		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'more'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			out.push(try self.consumeListedName(namesTypes))
		end 'more'
	end 'parseCommaSeparatedNames'

	function consumeListedName(namesTypes bool) returns immutable ByteArray throws SourceParseError
		if namesTypes 'aReferenceToADeclaredType'
			return (try self.consumeConformedInterfaceName()).value
		end 'aReferenceToADeclaredType'

		return (try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)).value
	end 'consumeListedName'

	// One `[static] function name(params) [returns Type] [throws E]` interface method SIGNATURE (no body).
	// Its param/return types are rendered to SOURCE strings (see IrInterface) via `renderInterfaceTypeName`
	// — `Self` for the enclosing interface, the alias name for a `named` type, `void` for no return.
	// `claimed` is what the enclosing interface has already read, for the duplicate-name refusal below:
	// that check needs the NAME TOKEN's position, which exists only here.
	function parseInterfaceMethod(interfaceName ByteArray, claimed RequirementNameSet) returns InterfaceMethodSig throws SourceParseError
		let isStatic = self.at(TokenKind.static)

		if isStatic 'staticMethod'
			self.advance()
		end 'staticMethod'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.function)

		// An interface method's name is a DECLARED name exactly as `parseFunction`'s is, so it admits a keyword
		// too — a conforming type may declare `function to()`, and a requirement it could not spell would
		// be a requirement no type could satisfy.
		let nameTok = try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.declaration)
		try requireUnclaimedInterfaceRequirementName(nameTok, interfaceName: interfaceName, claimed: claimed)

		let paramNames = NameRoster.create(0)
		let paramTypes = MaxonTypeArray.create(0)
		let paramRanges = SourceRangeArray.create(0)

		// An interface REQUIREMENT binds no value — it declares a signature and has no body — so the
		// declared-buffer-alias column and the declared-type-position column are filled and
		// dropped here. Neither is omitted from the call, because `parseFunctionParameters` fills every column
		// in lockstep and a caller that could opt out would be a second way for the columns to come apart.
		let paramSurfaces = DeclaredSurfaceArray.create(0)
		let paramTypeStarts = TokenIndexArray.create(0)
		let paramCapabilities = ParamCapabilityArray.create(0)
		let paramMarkers = BoolArray.create(0)
		let markerTokens = TokenIndexArray.create(0)

		if self.receiverCapabilityAt(self.pos) == ParamCapability.consuming 'aConsumingRequirement'
			throw notATypeError(self.tokenAt(self.pos + 1))
		end 'aConsumingRequirement'

		let selfCapability = try self.consumeParameterListOpen(not isStatic)
		try self.parseFunctionParameters(paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges, paramSurfaces: paramSurfaces, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts, paramCapabilities: paramCapabilities, paramMarkers: paramMarkers, markerTokens: markerTokens, owner: ParamDefaultOwner.interfaceRequirement, paramTypeOffer: noClosureParamOffer)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// NOTED, not thrown — see `requireNoUnstorableRequirementType` for why this reader may not refuse.
		if let existential = try firstExistentialParam(paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges) 'existentialParam'
			self.noteUnstorableRequirementType(existential.declaredType, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.interfaceRequirementParameter, line: existential.anchor.line, column: existential.anchor.column)
		end 'existentialParam'

		let returnTok = self.tokenAt(self.pos)
		let returnTypeStart = self.returnTypeTokenIndex()
		let hasReturnType = self.at(TokenKind.returns)
		let qualifiedReturn = try self.parseOptionalReturnType()
		let returnType = qualifiedReturn.returnType

		// The RETURN half, noted the same way and refused through the SAME `returnType` arm a declared
		// function's return uses. Unguarded, an `interface Maker function make() returns Shape end` compiled
		// and LINKED, and the first dispatch on the result reported `a member access on a 'int' value` — the
		// declared type having quietly degraded to the machine word.
		match returnType 'declaredReturn'
			void then break
			value(inner) then self.noteUnstorableRequirementType(inner, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.interfaceRequirementReturnType, line: returnTok.line, column: returnTok.column)
		end 'declaredReturn'

		// ⭐ **THE `throws E` CLAUSE IS RECORDED, NOT DISCARDED.** It decides which ABI a
		// witness dispatch of this method uses — the dual-register `witnessTryCall` or the single-register
		// `witnessCall` — and it is the ONLY place that fact exists: a witness method has no callee name for
		// `ProgramSignatures.throwsOf` to answer about. Discarded, a throwing interface method
		// dispatched through a witness silently DROPPED its error flag: the caller's `otherwise` never ran and
		// the impl's throw-path primary (0) came back as a real answer.
		let throwsClause = try self.parseOptionalThrowsClause()

		let written = self.writtenSignatureTypeNames(true, paramNames: paramNames, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts, returnTypeStart: returnTypeStart, hasReturnType: hasReturnType, ownTypeParams: NameRoster.create(0))
		let paramRequirements = self.interfaceParamRequirements(paramTypes, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts, interfaceName: interfaceName)
		let returnRequirement = self.interfaceReturnRequirement(returnType, typeStart: returnTypeStart, interfaceName: interfaceName)
		return InterfaceMethodSig.create(nameTok.value, paramNames: frozenNamesOf(paramNames), paramTypeNames: self.renderInterfaceParamTypes(paramTypes, requirements: paramRequirements, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts, interfaceName: interfaceName), paramTypes: paramRequirements, returnTypeName: self.renderInterfaceReturnType(returnType, requirement: returnRequirement, typeStart: returnTypeStart, interfaceName: interfaceName), returnType: returnRequirement, isStatic: isStatic, throwsClause: throwsClause, paramIsBuffer: interfaceParamBufferSpellings(paramSurfaces), paramCapabilities: frozenCapabilitiesOf(paramCapabilities), returnCapability: qualifiedReturn.capability, byRefParams: markerMaskOf(paramMarkers), nameLine: nameTok.line, nameColumn: nameTok.column, written: written, selfCapability: selfCapability)
	end 'parseInterfaceMethod'

	// Render each of an interface method's parameter types to its source string, parallel to `paramTypes`.
	// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedMemory` SPELLING, LIFTED OUT OF THE SURFACE COLUMN BEFORE IT IS DROPPED.** Bit 0 of
	// a position's root node is `SurfaceBitIsBuffer` — set when the source wrote the buffer's own name
	// rather than `ByteArray` or an `Array` alias. `parseInterfaceMethod` computes this column for every
	// requirement already (`parseFunctionParameters` fills every column in lockstep) and, until now, threw
	// it away; `InterfaceMethodSig.paramIsBuffer` says why it must survive.
	//
	// A position whose surface tree is the shared EMPTY one is plainly declared, which is every parameter in
	// the corpus but the two `BuiltinDictionaryLiteral` writes — so the common path reads one count and
	// pushes `false`.
	function interfaceParamBufferSpellings(paramSurfaces DeclaredSurfaceArray) returns immutable BoolArray
		let out = BoolArray.create(paramSurfaces.count(), zeroed: false) as isolated BoolArray

		for i in 0 upto paramSurfaces.count() 'each'
			let surface = try paramSurfaces.get(i) otherwise panic("interfaceParamBufferSpellings: paramSurfaces.get OOB at {i} of {paramSurfaces.count()} — parseFunctionParameters fills this column in lockstep with the names")
			out.push(surfaceIsBuffer(surface))
		end 'each'

		return freeze out
	end 'interfaceParamBufferSpellings'

	// A parameter declared through a generic-instance ALIAS renders as that alias, the requirement's brand.
	function renderInterfaceParamTypes(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, requirements RequirementTypeArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexArray, interfaceName ByteArray) returns immutable NameRoster
		let out = NameRoster.create(paramTypes.count(), zeroed: false) as isolated NameRoster

		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'each'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("renderInterfaceParamTypes: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			let requirement = try requirements.get(i) otherwise panic("renderInterfaceParamTypes: requirements.get OOB at {i} — interfaceParamRequirements builds one per parameter")
			let rendered = self.renderInterfaceRequirement(requirement, declared: paramType, typeStart: paramTypeStartAt(paramTypeStarts, position: i), interfaceName: interfaceName)
			out.push(rendered)
		end 'each'

		return freeze out
	end 'renderInterfaceParamTypes'

	function interfaceParamRequirements(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexArray, interfaceName ByteArray) returns immutable RequirementTypeArray
		let out = RequirementTypeArray.create(paramTypes.count(), zeroed: false) as isolated RequirementTypeArray

		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'each'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("interfaceParamRequirements: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			let requirement = self.interfaceRequirementType(paramType, typeStart: paramTypeStartAt(paramTypeStarts, position: i), interfaceName: interfaceName)
			out.push(requirement)
		end 'each'

		return freeze out
	end 'interfaceParamRequirements'

	function frozenCapabilitiesOf(capabilities ParamCapabilityArray) returns immutable ParamCapabilityArray
		let out = ParamCapabilityArray.create(capabilities.count(), zeroed: false) as isolated ParamCapabilityArray

		for capability in capabilities 'eachCapability'
			out.push(capability)
		end 'eachCapability'

		return freeze out
	end 'frozenCapabilitiesOf'

	function interfaceReturnRequirement(returnType MaxonReturnType, typeStart ElementIndex, interfaceName ByteArray) returns RequirementType
		return match returnType 'r'
			void gives RequirementType.create(RequirementTypeIdentity.void, brand: EmptySourceText)
			value(inner) gives self.interfaceRequirementType(inner, typeStart: typeStart, interfaceName: interfaceName)
		end 'r'
	end 'interfaceReturnRequirement'

	function interfaceRequirementType(declared MaxonType, typeStart ElementIndex, interfaceName ByteArray) returns RequirementType
		if self.namesInterfaceSelf(declared, interfaceName: interfaceName) 'theInterfacesOwnType'
			return RequirementType.ofSelf()
		end 'theInterfacesOwnType'

		return self.declaredRequirementType(declared, typeStart: typeStart)
	end 'interfaceRequirementType'

	function declaredRequirementType(declared MaxonType, typeStart ElementIndex) returns RequirementType
		let requirement = requirementTypeOfDeclared(declared, names: self.artifact.typeNames, brand: self.declaredBrandAt(typeStart, resolved: declared))

		if not self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'theSweepSettlesItsRequirementsAfterTheFold'
			return requirement
		end 'theSweepSettlesItsRequirementsAfterTheFold'

		return self.signatures.fileCompleteRequirement(requirement, declFilePath: self.job.path)
	end 'declaredRequirementType'

	function renderInterfaceRequirement(requirement RequirementType, declared MaxonType, typeStart ElementIndex, interfaceName ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
		if not requirement.brand.isEmpty() 'aBrandedInstance'
			return requirement.brand
		end 'aBrandedInstance'

		return self.renderInterfaceParamType(declared, typeStart: typeStart, interfaceName: interfaceName)
	end 'renderInterfaceRequirement'

	function namesInterfaceSelf(declared MaxonType, interfaceName ByteArray) returns bool
		return maxonTypeTag(declared) == ValueTypeTag.structRef and self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(declared)).equals(interfaceName)
	end 'namesInterfaceSelf'

	function renderInterfaceParamType(paramType MaxonType, typeStart ElementIndex, interfaceName ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
		if maxonTypeTag(paramType) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAnInstance'
			return self.renderInterfaceTypeName(paramType, interfaceName: interfaceName)
		end 'notAnInstance'

		match self.writtenTypeNameAt(typeStart) 'qualified'
			named(name) then return name if containsQualifier(name) else self.renderInterfaceTypeName(paramType, interfaceName: interfaceName)
			unnamed then return self.renderInterfaceTypeName(paramType, interfaceName: interfaceName)
		end 'qualified'
	end 'renderInterfaceParamType'

	// Render an interface method's RETURN to its source string — `void` for no `returns`, else the type.
	function renderInterfaceReturnType(returnType MaxonReturnType, requirement RequirementType, typeStart ElementIndex, interfaceName ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
		return match returnType 'r'
			void gives InterfaceVoidTypeName
			value(inner) gives self.renderInterfaceRequirement(requirement, declared: inner, typeStart: typeStart, interfaceName: interfaceName)
		end 'r'
	end 'renderInterfaceReturnType'

	// Render a parsed interface-signature `MaxonType` to the SOURCE string E3016 prints. `Self` (a `structRef`
	// whose id is the interface's own name) renders as `Self`; every other type defers to the SHARED
	// `renderDeclaredTypeName`, so it reads as the impl side does.
	//
	// ⚠ The `Self` arm is the WHOLE of what this adds, and it is deliberately NOT in the shared renderer: a
	// `Self` written in a conformance ARGUMENT means the conforming type's own name, not the string `Self`.
	function renderInterfaceTypeName(t MaxonType, interfaceName ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
		if self.namesInterfaceSelf(t, interfaceName: interfaceName) 'selfRef'
			return InterfaceSelfTypeName
		end 'selfRef'

		// Every other type goes through the ONE shared renderer (`IrInterface.renderDeclaredTypeName`) — the
		// same function the IMPL side of the conformance uses, so an associated type, a type PARAMETER and a
		// generic INSTANCE cannot be spelled one way here and another way there. `enclosingTypeParams` is the
		// interface's own `uses` list inside an interface body, which is what a `typeParameter(token)` in one of
		// its signatures indexes.
		return renderDeclaredTypeName(t, declarationName: interfaceName, typeParamNames: self.enclosingTypeParams, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, signatures: self.signatures)
	end 'renderInterfaceTypeName'

	// The `implements I1, I2 [with …]` conformance clause of a `type`, `enum` or `union` declaration.
	// Records a ConformanceDecl anchored on the declaration's NAME token, or does nothing when
	// there is no clause. Each interface carries its own `with` bindings, which `checkConformance`
	// substitutes into that interface's requirements before it compares them.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE PRODUCER FOR ALL THREE DECLARATION KINDS.** Every consumer of a conformance — the widening
	// verdict, E3015/E3016, the witness-slot filing, the type-cycle edges, the mutation summary — reads
	// `project.conformances` and nothing else, so a kind filed anywhere else is a kind those checks do not
	// see at all.
	function parseImplementsClause(nameTok Token) returns NameRoster throws SourceParseError
		let interfaces = try self.readImplementsList(ConformanceOwner.declaration(nameTok.value))

		if not interfaces.isEmpty() 'declaresAConformance'
			self.artifact.recordConformance(ConformanceDecl.create(nameTok.value, interfaces: interfaces, nameLine: nameTok.line, nameColumn: nameTok.column, filePath: self.job.path))
		end 'declaresAConformance'

		return conformanceInterfaceNames(interfaces)
	end 'parseImplementsClause'

	// The conformance an `extension <T> implements …` declares on behalf of ONE conformer the `where` clause
	// admits. Anchored on the extension's own name token, so a requirement the body fails to satisfy is
	// blamed at the `extension` line the author wrote rather than at the type's distant declaration.
	//
	// A plain `extension X` records nothing, which is the empty-list fast path and the whole corpus bar two
	// files.
	function recordExtensionConformance(header ExtensionHeader, conformer immutable ByteArray)
		if header.interfaces.isEmpty() 'addsNoConformance'
			return
		end 'addsNoConformance'

		self.artifact.recordConformance(ConformanceDecl.create(conformer, interfaces: header.interfaces, nameLine: header.nameTok.line, nameColumn: header.nameTok.column, filePath: self.job.path))
	end 'recordExtensionConformance'

	// ⭐ **THE `implements I1, I2 [with …]` LIST ITSELF, WITHOUT DECIDING WHO CONFORMS.** Two headers carry
	// this clause — a `type` declaration's and an `extension`'s — and they differ only in what they do with
	// the answer: a type records the conformance against its own name unconditionally, while an extension
	// records it per CONFORMER and only where the `where` clause is satisfied. Reading it in one place is
	// what keeps `implements Iterable with (Element, Iter)` one grammar rather than two that agree by hand.
	//
	// An absent clause answers an empty list.
	function readImplementsList(owner ConformanceOwner) returns InterfaceConformanceArray throws SourceParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.implements) 'noImplements'
			return InterfaceConformanceArray.create(0)
		end 'noImplements'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.implements)

		let interfaces = InterfaceConformanceArray.create(0)
		interfaces.push(try self.parseOneConformance(owner))

		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreInterfaces'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			interfaces.push(try self.parseOneConformance(owner))
		end 'moreInterfaces'

		return interfaces
	end 'readImplementsList'

	// One `I [with …]` entry of an `implements` list — the interface name and the arguments bound to
	// its `uses` names, which are only meaningful as a pair (`InterfaceConformance`).
	function parseOneConformance(owner ConformanceOwner) returns InterfaceConformance throws SourceParseError
		let ifaceTok = try self.consumeConformedInterfaceName()

		if not self.at(TokenKind.with) 'bindsNothing'
			return InterfaceConformance.withoutBindings(ifaceTok.value)
		end 'bindsNothing'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.with)
		let spellings = NameRoster.create(0)
		let types = RequirementTypeArray.create(0)
		let withArgs = try self.parseConformanceWithArgs(ifaceTok.value, spellings: spellings, types: types, owner: owner)
		return InterfaceConformance.create(ifaceTok.value, withArgs: withArgs, withArgSpellings: spellings, withArgTypes: types)
	end 'parseOneConformance'

	// ⭐ **THE `with <TypeArg>` / `with (<TypeArgs>)` ASSOCIATED-TYPE BINDING, IN BOTH SPELLINGS.**
	// Every argument is READ as a type reference and RENDERED to the source string `checkConformance`
	// substitutes into a requirement's signature — the conformance's whole contribution.
	//
	// ⚠ **HOW MANY ARGUMENTS THE UNPARENTHESIZED FORM TAKES IS THE INTERFACE'S `uses` ARITY, and reading
	// exactly one is a wrong answer.** `implements Pair with Integer, Float` against `interface Pair uses
	// First, Second` would leave `, Float` to `parseImplementsClause`'s comma loop, which files `Float` as a
	// second INTERFACE and reports a spurious `E3015 … implements unknown interface 'Float'` on a legal
	// program. The arity is consulted here rather than at the clause loop.
	//
	// The arity is asked ONLY after a first argument is followed by a comma, so no program that parses
	// today pays the lookup at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH ARMS GO THROUGH `parseTypeReference`, AND SKIPPING BALANCED TOKENS IN THE PARENTHESIZED ONE
	// WOULD BE A TYPE POSITION THE E3062 USE CHECK CANNOT SEE.** That reader is where a written type name is
	// recorded as USED (`recordTypeNameUse`), so a file whose only mention of an alias is
	// `implements Container with (Score)` would have that alias reported `unused typealias` — a legal program
	// refused, and refused in only ONE of the two spellings. The two arms are one rule, so a use may not be
	// visible in one spelling of it and invisible in the other.
	//
	// It reads a LEAF reference per argument rather than reaching for `parseGenericArgNodes`: that
	// reader also VALIDATES what it reads (`checkGenericArgType`, and `checkGenericSpellings` over the
	// `with` sites it notes), and a
	// conformance argument is checked whole-program by `checkConformance` instead, which is the only
	// place the interface it binds against is reachable.
	//
	// ⚖ **THE PARENTHESIZED ARM IS ALSO WHERE A SURPLUS ARGUMENT IS REFUSED, AND ONLY THERE** —
	// see `checkParenthesizedConformanceArgs` for the rule and `ConformanceUsesArity` for the false
	// reject it must not commit. The asymmetry with the unparenthesized arm below is deliberate and it
	// is why the check lives HERE rather than in `ConformanceCheck`: whether the list was written in
	// parentheses is a fact only the token stream holds. `InterfaceConformance` carries each argument and
	// its written spelling, neither of which records the parentheses, so the two list forms are already
	// indistinguishable one line later — recording a flag on it purely so a later pass could re-derive what
	// this function is looking straight at would be plumbing a parse fact through the whole tier for no gain.
	function parseConformanceWithArgs(interfaceName ByteArray, spellings NameRoster, types RequirementTypeArray, owner ConformanceOwner) returns NameRoster throws SourceParseError
		let args = NameRoster.create(0)

		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) and self.parenthesizedWithBindsOneTuple(interfaceName) 'oneTupleArgument'
			// ONE argument, read by the ordinary reference reader — `parseTypeReference` meets the `(` and
			// routes to `parseTupleTypeReference`, which is what mints and interns the tuple's layout. So the
			// binding this records is the same tuple instance `WithIterIterator.current`'s declared
			// `returns (Source, Element)` resolves to, which is what `ConformanceCheck` compares.
			try self.readConformanceWithArgInto(args, spellings: spellings, types: types, owner: owner, interfaceName: interfaceName)
			return args
		end 'oneTupleArgument'

		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'parenthesized'
			// The `(` is the anchor for a surplus: it is the token that opens the list whose LENGTH is
			// the assertion being refused, and it is on the `implements` line the author must edit.
			let openTok = self.current()
			try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)

			while true 'eachArg'
				try self.readConformanceWithArgInto(args, spellings: spellings, types: types, owner: owner, interfaceName: interfaceName)

				if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'lastArg'
					break
				end 'lastArg'

				try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			end 'eachArg'

			try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
			self.checkParenthesizedConformanceArgs(interfaceName, written: args.count(), openTok: openTok)
			return args
		end 'parenthesized'

		try self.readConformanceWithArgInto(args, spellings: spellings, types: types, owner: owner, interfaceName: interfaceName)

		if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'singleArg'
			return args
		end 'singleArg'

		let arity = self.interfaceUsesArity(interfaceName)

		while args.count() < arity and self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreArgs'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			try self.readConformanceWithArgInto(args, spellings: spellings, types: types, owner: owner, interfaceName: interfaceName)
		end 'moreArgs'

		return args
	end 'parseConformanceWithArgs'

	function readConformanceWithArgInto(args NameRoster, spellings NameRoster, types RequirementTypeArray, owner ConformanceOwner, interfaceName ByteArray) throws SourceParseError
		let bindingTok = self.current()
		spellings.push(self.typeSpellingAt(self.pos))
		let bound = try self.readConformanceWithArg(types)

		match owner 'owner'
			unchecked then break
			declaration(conformer) then self.refuseBindingAnExtensionStoresAsAnArgument(conformer, interfaceName: interfaceName, position: args.count(), bound: bound, bindingTok: bindingTok)
		end 'owner'

		args.push(bound)
	end 'readConformanceWithArgInto'

	function refuseBindingAnExtensionStoresAsAnArgument(conformer ByteArray, interfaceName ByteArray, position ElementIndex, bound ByteArray, bindingTok Token)
		if not self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'indexStillBuilding'
			return
		end 'indexStillBuilding'

		for held in self.signatures.extensionHeldArguments(conformer, interfaceName: interfaceName, associatedPosition: position) 'eachHeldArgument'
			let slot = self.signatures.genericArgSlotOf(held.baseName)

			if self.signatures.genericArgumentIsRefused(slot, argumentIsAnInterface: true, position: held.position as ElementIndex) 'refused'
				self.recordArtifactDiagnostic(ErrorCode.parserUnsupportedFeature, message: unsupportedFeatureMessage(interfaceStoragePositionMessage(slot.storagePosition(), interfaceName: bound)), range: SourceRange.ofToken(bindingTok))
				return
			end 'refused'
		end 'eachHeldArgument'
	end 'refuseBindingAnExtensionStoresAsAnArgument'

	// One conformance argument: read as a type reference (so it records the alias USE), its identity pushed
	// onto `types`, and its display string returned. Spelled once because both arms above read an argument
	// the same way, and a second spelling would be free to record the use in only one of them — which is
	// exactly the bug the parenthesized arm already had.
	//
	// It renders through the SHARED renderer and NOT through `renderInterfaceTypeName`, which is the one
	// difference between the two conformance-argument positions: a written `Self` here has already resolved
	// to the enclosing type's own `structRef` (`enclosingSelfType`), and the conforming type's NAME is
	// precisely what the binding must carry. `enclosingTypeParams` is the CONFORMING type's own `uses` list,
	// which is what makes `type Box uses T implements Holder with T` bind `Element := T`.
	function readConformanceWithArg(types RequirementTypeArray) returns immutable ByteArray throws SourceParseError
		let start = self.pos
		let declared = try self.parseTypeReference(BarePrimitiveVoice.admitted)
		types.push(self.declaredRequirementType(declared, typeStart: start))
		return self.renderScopedTypeName(declared)
	end 'readConformanceWithArg'

	// ⭐ **`with (A, B)` AGAINST A ONE-ASSOCIATED-TYPE INTERFACE IS ONE TUPLE ARGUMENT, NOT TWO
	// BINDINGS.** A pure LIST reading, with `((A, B))` as the tuple spelling, collides with
	// `stdlib/helpers/itertools/withIterator.maxon`: it writes
	// `implements Iterator with (Source, Element)` against a one-`uses` `Iterator` and declares
	// `current() returns (Source, Element)`, so NO list reading can make it conform. The corpus is the
	// reference, so the arity discrimination is: where a single type argument is expected, `(A, B)` is
	// a tuple type rather than two separate type arguments.
	//
	// ⚠ **A COMMA IS REQUIRED, WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS THE TWO SPELLINGS THE SURPLUS-ARGUMENT RULE PINS ALIVE.** `with (T)`
	// holds no top-level comma and stays ONE ordinary binding — `parseTupleTypeReference` would refuse
	// it as a one-element tuple (`E2003`), which this deliberately does not. `with ((A, B))` holds its
	// comma one level DOWN, so it too stays one binding, whose single item is the tuple. Only `with
	// (A, B)` moves, and only at arity one.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ANSWERS `false` IN THE FIRST DECLARATION SWEEP, BECAUSE THE ARITY IS NOT KNOWN THERE**
	// — `Queries.foldInterfaceDeclarations` runs after the last file is folded, so
	// `conformanceUsesArity` reads `unresolvable` for every interface while the sweep is walking, and
	// the sweep records the ITEM LIST. Every clause that writes a `with` is re-read by
	// `foldConformanceClauseInto` once the arity resolves, and that record replaces the sweep's. The
	// re-read is a parse rather than a repair of the sweep's record: the binding a tuple carries is
	// the INTERNED tuple layout's name, whose elements are canonicalized through `mangleTypeArg` (a
	// type parameter's is a digest of its declaration and name), so it cannot be rebuilt from the
	// SPELLINGS.
	function parenthesizedWithBindsOneTuple(interfaceName ByteArray) returns bool
		match self.conformanceUsesArity(interfaceName) 'arity'
			declared(count) then return count == SingleAssociatedType and self.parenGroups.holdsTopLevelComma(self.tokens, open: self.pos)
			unresolvable then return false
		end 'arity'
	end 'parenthesizedWithBindsOneTuple'

	// ⚖ **A PARENTHESIZED `with (…)` LIST MAY NOT BE LONGER THAN THE INTERFACE'S `uses` ARITY.**
	// Without this, `implements One with (Integer, Float)` against `interface One uses A` binds
	// `A := Integer`, drops `Float` and compiles clean (measured, exit 42) — a typo nothing reported.
	//
	// ⚖ **THE UNPARENTHESIZED SPELLING KEEPS IGNORING ITS SURPLUS, AND THAT IS THE RULE RATHER THAN AN
	// EXEMPTION FROM IT.** The two spellings are not the same claim. Parentheses make the list
	// explicit, so its LENGTH is something the author ASSERTED and can be wrong about; without them
	// the length is decided by the interface's own arity and a trailing comma legitimately belongs to
	// the outer `implements` list — which is why precisely `arity` items are consumed there, and why
	// `associated-types.md`'s `surplus-conformance-argument-is-ignored` pins that half. Refusing here
	// therefore diverges from nothing the unparenthesized rule pinned.
	//
	// ⚠ **A DEFICIT IS NOT THIS DIAGNOSTIC AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE.** Too FEW arguments is already
	// reported whole-program, by NAME rather than by count — `ConformanceCheck.checkOneInterfaceConformance`
	// says which associated type is left unbound (E3016), which is the sentence the author can act on in
	// one edit. Adding a count-shaped complaint beside it would be two wordings for one mistake.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT DOES NOT FIRE ON AN INTERFACE THAT RESOLVES TO NOTHING.** A name no file declares has no
	// arity to be surplus OF, so the `unresolvable` arm is silent: the program's real error is the
	// whole-program `E3015 … implements unknown interface`, which names the fault precisely, and an arity
	// sentence about an interface the compiler never found would blame the wrong thing. That case is carried
	// by `ConformanceUsesArity` rather than by the count-shaped default the unparenthesized arm takes, which
	// is the whole reason the two arms do not share one number.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT RUNS ONLY ON THE FAR SIDE OF THE DECLARATION FOLD**, the same gate `requireFieldDefaultType`
	// sits behind: during the sweep this index is still being BUILT, so an
	// interface declared in a not-yet-swept file reads as unresolvable, and a diagnostic built there is
	// allocation spent on a verdict the throwaway artifact discards.
	function checkParenthesizedConformanceArgs(interfaceName ByteArray, written ElementIndex, openTok Token)
		if not self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'indexStillBuilding'
			return
		end 'indexStillBuilding'

		match self.conformanceUsesArity(interfaceName) 'arity'
			declared(count) then self.checkConformanceArgsAgainstArity(interfaceName, declared: count, written: written, openTok: openTok)
			unresolvable then break
		end 'arity'
	end 'checkParenthesizedConformanceArgs'

	// The comparison and the sentence, once. It names BOTH counts because a reader fixing this needs to
	// know which one to change, and the interface, because the arity is the interface's fact and not the
	// conformance's — the `uses` clause the author has to look at may be in another file entirely.
	function checkConformanceArgsAgainstArity(interfaceName ByteArray, declared ElementIndex, written ElementIndex, openTok Token)
		if written <= declared 'withinArity'
			return
		end 'withinArity'

		self.recordArtifactDiagnostic(ErrorCode.conformanceWithArgSurplus, message: "interface '{String.from(interfaceName)}' declares {declared} associated type(s), but this parenthesized 'with' clause binds {written}", range: SourceRange.ofToken(openTok))
	end 'checkConformanceArgsAgainstArity'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE THE NUMBER COMES FROM** — how many associated types conforming to
	// `interfaceName` binds, off the ONE interface-lookup rule (`IrInterface.lookupInterface`, asked of this
	// phase's store — `signatures.interfaceMap()`). Its two
	// readers ask the same question and need different things from the miss, so the miss is a CASE and
	// not a default: see `ConformanceUsesArity` for why folding them into one count is precisely the
	// shape that hides a false reject.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT COUNTS THE INTERFACE'S OWN `uses` NAMES AND NOT ITS `extends`-INHERITED ONES — a SCOPE
	// statement, not an incrementality one.** Both the count and `extendsInterfaces` ride the signature
	// index's hash (`recordInterfaceDeclaration`), which keys every parse memo, so walking the chain here
	// would depend on nothing unhashed. What stops the widening is that the compiler does not inherit
	// associated-type BINDINGS at all — see below. Widening the count
	// without also inheriting the binding would accept an argument nothing then substitutes.
	//
	// The consequence today is bounded and is a MESSAGE change, never an accept becoming a reject:
	// `interface Sub extends Base` (where `Base uses Element`) leaves `Sub` with an empty own list, so
	// `implements Sub with (Integer)` is refused here — a program the compiler ALREADY rejects, with E3016's
	// wrong-signature sentence, because the compiler does not inherit the binding either
	// (`ConformanceCheck.checkOneInterfaceConformance`). The day inherited bindings land, this count and
	// that hash move together.
	function conformanceUsesArity(interfaceName ByteArray) returns ConformanceUsesArity
		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: interfaceName) 'lookup'
			found(iface) then return ConformanceUsesArity.declared(iface.associatedTypeNames.count())
			notFound then return ConformanceUsesArity.unresolvable
		end 'lookup'
	end 'conformanceUsesArity'

	// ⭐ **HOW MANY ARGUMENTS AN UNPARENTHESIZED `with` CLAUSE AFTER `interfaceName` TAKES** — the count of
	// that interface's `uses` names, off the ONE arity door (`conformanceUsesArity`, which asks the ONE
	// parse-time interface door). It is not a separate lookup and it is not a separate STORE either:
	// the arity IS `associatedTypeNames.count()` on the declaration that door hands back, so the number cannot
	// disagree with the interface a witness dispatch resolves under the same name — nor with the
	// count the parenthesized arm refuses a surplus against.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHOLE-PROGRAM, AND NOT THIS FILE'S `artifact.interfaces`.** That array is filled by
	// `recordInterface` as the linear parse REACHES each declaration, so it would answer for an interface
	// declared above the conformance and for no other — an interface written lower in the same file,
	// or in any other file, would silently take the default of 1 and `implements Duo with Score, Weight`
	// truncate after `Score`. Both shapes then surface as `E3016 … type 'Both' does not define
	// required associated type 'Q' from interface 'Duo'` and `E3015 … type 'Both' implements unknown
	// interface 'Weight'`, on a legal program — the whole-program conformance check reporting the
	// CONSEQUENCE of a truncated parse, where the parse itself could not see the cause.
	// `specs/associated-types.md` carries all four cases.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN INTERFACE NO FILE DECLARES DEFAULTS TO ONE, RATHER THAN BEING REFUSED AS UNRESOLVABLE.**
	// That set is the TYPOS plus the interfaces `foldInterfaceDeclarations` COULD NOT READ, and
	// nothing else: it visits every `interface` in every source path of the program, stdlib included,
	// before any file is parsed, and it is TOLERANT — a malformed declaration records nothing, which
	// is the one non-typo way to land here and is a program the real parse rejects on its own line
	// anyway. Refusing instead would turn a misspelled interface into a parse error where the
	// whole-program `E3015 … implements unknown interface` already names it precisely.
	function interfaceUsesArity(interfaceName ByteArray) returns ElementIndex
		match self.conformanceUsesArity(interfaceName) 'arity'
			declared(count) then return count
			unresolvable then return UnresolvableInterfaceUsesArity
		end 'arity'
	end 'interfaceUsesArity'

	// Parse an `enum`/`union` declaration:
	//
	//     enum Color
	//         red
	//         green = 5
	//         blue
	//     end 'Color'
	//
	// ⭐ **THE DECLARATION EMITS NOTHING** — like a `type`, its whole effect is the `EnumLayout`
	// recorded below, which `Color.red` reads a case's TAG out of and an enum `match` reads the case
	// SET out of. `union` and `enum` share this routine entirely: only the diagnostic wording later
	// differs, carried by `layout.isUnion`.
	//
	// A CASE MAY CARRY A PAYLOAD (`value(n Integer)` — see `readEnumCaseInto`), and its raw
	// value may be an int, a float, a string or a character, in the explicit (`North = 'n'`) and the implicit
	// (`"North"` standing where the name goes) spellings alike. A STRUCT or FUNCTION backing is read too
	// and checked per case (`checkStructBackedCase`, `checkFunctionBackedCase`).
	//
	// ⭐ **A MEMBER MAY BE AN INSTANCE METHOD**, and it is built by the very same `parseFunction` a
	// struct's method is: the receiver is `__self` at parameter 0, the method is declared under
	// `Direction.isNorth` (`declaredMethodName`), and a call is an ordinary direct call with the receiver
	// prepended. What differs from a `type` is only the RECEIVER'S TYPE — `named`, not `structRef`
	// (`enclosingSelfType`) — and that there are no FIELDS, hence no self-field aliases. A `static
	// function` stays refused (`parseEnumMethod`).
	function parseEnumDeclaration(isUnion bool, visibility Visibility) throws SourceParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.union if isUnion else TokenKind.enum)
		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
		try self.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned(nameTok, declares: DeclaredTypeMeaning.nominalIdentity)

		// ⚠ **THE REAL PARSE READS THE CLAUSE THROUGH `readImplementsList`, NEVER THROUGH THE SWEEP'S
		// `readOptionalImplementsClause`.** That reader stops at an unparenthesized `with` whose interface
		// arity is not yet knowable, because the sweep is what builds the arity index; here the index is
		// whole, so an interface named after a `with` reaches the layout instead of being dropped. It is also
		// the only reader that yields the `with` bindings `checkOneConformance` substitutes.
		self.openConformanceClauseAliasScope()
		let conformsTo = try self.parseImplementsClause(nameTok)
		self.closeConformanceClauseAliasScope()
		try self.requireDeclarationHeaderLineEnd()
		self.enterTypeScope(nameTok.value, isEnum: true)

		let layout = EnumLayout.create(nameTok.value, isUnion: isUnion, conformsTo: conformsTo, visibility: visibility, declFilePath: self.job.path, backing: EnumBacking.unresolved)
		layout.declLine = nameTok.line
		layout.declColumn = nameTok.column
		let ledger = EnumCaseLedger.create()

		// The cursor sits at the enum body start; remember it so the lazy sibling-receiver walk (#65) can
		// begin here once a method body's bare call needs it — exactly as `parseTypeDeclaration` does. It is
		// not optional bookkeeping: left at the PREVIOUS declaration's body, a bare `isStop()` inside an enum
		// method would resolve against another type's method list.
		self.enclosingTypeBodyStart = self.pos
		self.enclosingBodyIsWalkable = true

		while true 'members'
			self.skipNewlines()

			if self.enumBodyEndsAt(self.pos) 'closes'
				break
			end 'closes'

			if self.isEof() 'eof'
				let tok = self.current()
				throw SourceParseError.unexpectedEof(tok.line, column: tok.column)
			end 'eof'

			// A method — `[export] function <name>(`. `function` ALONE is a legal CASE name, so only
			// `atEnumMethodStart`'s full shape is a method; everything else is a case.
			if self.atEnumMethodStart() 'method'
				try self.parseEnumMethod(layout)
				continue
			end 'method'

			// The member's first token is the case's NAME token, which anchors every per-case diagnostic.
			// Read before the case rather than after, because reading it moves the cursor.
			let caseTok = self.current()
			try self.readEnumCaseInto(layout, ledger: ledger)
			self.checkFunctionBackedCase(layout, caseTok: caseTok)
			try self.checkStructBackedCase(layout, caseTok: caseTok)
		end 'members'

		try self.consumeBlockEnd(nameTok.value, labelIsRequired: false)

		self.artifact.recordEnumType(layout)
		self.closeTypeParamScope()
	end 'parseEnumDeclaration'

	// ⛔ **ONE MEMBER METHOD OF AN `enum`/`union`, AND THE ONE THING IT REFUSES.** The cursor sits on
	// the optional `export`. An INSTANCE method goes straight to the shared `parseFunction`, which is the
	// whole implementation: a method IS a function, plus a receiver at parameter 0.
	//
	// ⭐ **A `static function` STAYS REFUSED.** Accepting one and silently dropping the modifier makes
	// the call die at `E3036 missing argument for parameter 'self'`, blaming the call site for a
	// declaration the compiler mis-parsed. A positioned refusal at the declaration is strictly better,
	// and it costs nothing: no `union`/`enum` in `stdlib/` declares one.
	//
	// It is not `parseTypeMember` widened, because an enum body's grammar is genuinely a different one: it
	// has no FIELDS and no nested `typealias` (the two other members that routine reads), and `static` is
	// an error here rather than the receiver switch it is there.
	function parseEnumMethod(layout EnumLayout) throws SourceParseError
		let visibility = try self.consumeOptionalVisibility()

		if self.at(TokenKind.static) 'staticMethod'
			throw self.staticFunctionOnEnumRefusal(layout, tok: self.current())
		end 'staticMethod'

		try self.parseFunction(visibility, takesReceiver: true, form: DeclaredHeadForm.declaredFunction)
	end 'parseEnumMethod'

	function staticFunctionOnEnumRefusal(layout EnumLayout, tok Token) returns SourceParseError
		return SourceParseError.unsupported("a `static function` on `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` (an INSTANCE method is supported — a static one has no receiver to name the enum through, and no `enum`/`union` in the corpus declares one)", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'staticFunctionOnEnumRefusal'

	function enumBeingExtended() returns immutable EnumLayout
		match self.signatures.enumInAnyFile(self.enclosingTypeName()) 'extendedEnum'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			undeclared then panic("Parser.enumBeingExtended: '{String.from(self.enclosingTypeName())}' is marked an enum by `enterTypeScope` and no enum registry holds it")
		end 'extendedEnum'
	end 'enumBeingExtended'

	// Is the token at `index` the `end` that CLOSES an enum/union body — as opposed to a case NAMED `end`?
	//
	// ⚠ **`end` IS A LEGAL CASE NAME** (`keyword-as-case-name.md` declares one), so the terminator
	// cannot be recognized by the `end` keyword alone. The rule: a terminating `end` is followed by
	// the block LABEL (a character literal) or by end-of-file; an `end` followed by anything else — a
	// newline, another case — is the case named `end`. Every corpus enum closes with a labelled `end
	// 'Name'`, which this recognizes; a bare unlabelled `end` mid-body is read as a case.
	//
	// ⚠ It takes an INDEX rather than reading the cursor because THREE walks ask it and only two of them
	// are cursor-driven: the real parse and the tolerant sweep sit ON the token, while the sibling-receiver
	// scan (`ensureSiblingReceivers`) walks an index of its own. Written twice, the third walk would read a
	// case named `end` as the body's terminator, and a bare sibling call to a method declared after such a
	// case would report `E3004 call to undefined function`.
	function enumBodyEndsAt(index ElementIndex) returns bool
		return labelledBlockCloserAt(self.tokens, index: index) or (self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.end and self.kindAt(index + 1) == TokenKind.eof)
	end 'enumBodyEndsAt'

	// Does the cursor open a METHOD inside an enum/union body — `[export] [static] function <name>(`?
	//
	// ⚠ **`function` IS ALSO A LEGAL CASE NAME** (`keyword-as-case-name.md` declares one), so a bare
	// `function` keyword does NOT open a method: a method is `function` followed by a NAME and a `(` —
	// the shared `functionDeclarationAt`, which the declaration sweep reads for the same reason. The
	// modifiers an enum body may carry before the keyword are spanned too, BOTH of them in order
	// (`parseTypeMember` accepts `export static` on a struct, so `export static` inside an enum must
	// reach the refusal in `parseEnumMethod` rather than fall through to `readEnumCaseInto` and be
	// blamed as a malformed case).
	//
	// ⚠ It is the shape gate for BOTH readers of an enum body — the real parse and the tolerant sweep — so
	// the two cannot disagree about which members are cases, which is what a case's TAG depends on.
	function atEnumMethodStart() returns bool
		return self.functionDeclarationAt(pastDeclarationModifiers(self.tokens, index: self.pos))
	end 'atEnumMethodStart'

	// Read ONE enum/union case into `layout`, threading the auto-increment tag through `counter` and
	// fixing the layout's BACKING. Reports the three declaration diagnostics: E3030 (duplicate case
	// name), E3031 (duplicate raw value), E3032 (raw value type mismatch). SHARED by the real parse and
	// the tolerant sweep (`recordScannedEnum`), which wraps it in `otherwise` — so the two cannot drift
	// on how a tag is assigned OR on how it is to be READ, the two facts they MUST agree on (the sweep's
	// layout is what the parser reads both from at a use site).
	function readEnumCaseInto(layout EnumLayout, ledger EnumCaseLedger) throws SourceParseError
		let nameTok = try self.readEnumCaseNameToken()
		let caseName = try self.enumCaseNameOf(nameTok)

		// A `__`-prefixed case is reserved. Checked before the duplicate/backing rules so the
		// reservation is reported at the name, and — as with the field reader — the tolerant sweep
		// (`recordScannedEnum`) swallows the throw and leaves the diagnostic to the real parse.
		//
		// ⚠ It is asked about `caseName` — the DECODED name, the one this case is actually declared under
		// everywhere else in this function — and not about the token's text. This is THE site that makes those
		// two different things (`"\x5f\x5fx"` declares `__x`); see `requireUnreservedName`.
		// ⚠ `bindsInto` is stated HERE and nowhere else: a case name is reachable only as `Marker.self`, so it
		// is the one declaration in the language that cannot shadow the implicit receiver. See
		// `DeclaredNameNamespace` — the default is the refusing side, so a new binding site added tomorrow
		// gets the reservation without having to remember it.
		try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: caseName, bindsInto: DeclaredNameNamespace.qualifiedByItsType)

		// E3030 — checked at the second declaration.
		if ledger.nameIsClaimed(caseName) 'duplicateCase'
			throw SourceParseError.enumDuplicateCase(String.from(caseName), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'duplicateCase'

		// ⭐ **AN IMPLICITLY BACKED CASE — the literal stood where the NAME goes, so it is both.** `"North"`
		// declares the case `North` with the raw value `"North"`, and `'N'` declares `N` with the raw value
		// `'N'`. Asked before everything below because such a case has no payload list to read (a literal is
		// not a callable head) and never writes an `=`: its raw value is already in hand, and an `=` after it
		// would be a second raw value for one case.
		if tokenKindIsEnumLiteralBacking(nameTok.kind) 'implicitBacking'
			try self.appendLiteralBackedEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, literalTok: nameTok, ledger: ledger, caseTok: nameTok, payloads: PayloadFieldArray.create(0))
			return
		end 'implicitBacking'

		// ⭐⭐ **A CASE'S PAYLOAD LIST AND ITS RAW VALUE ARE INDEPENDENT, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS READ HERE
		// RATHER THAN IN AN ARM THAT RETURNS.** `add(dest ID, src ID) = OpMeta.create(1)` writes BOTH — a
		// runtime payload and compile-time metadata (`/specs/union-struct-backing.md`) — and a payload
		// list that ENDED the case would kill every one of those at the `=` as *"Expected 'an enum case
		// name'"*. The read position is right after the case name for an enum, or after the optional
		// payload list for a union — at the optional `=`: `add(dest ID, src ID) = 5` beside `nop = 7`
		// gives `op.rawValue` 5 and `op.ordinal` 0.
		//
		// ⇒ so every append site below takes the payload list, and no arm may re-mint an empty one: which
		// raw value a case wrote cannot change which payload slots it declared.
		//
		// No payload type is refused in the reader — a refusal would `throw` in the tolerant sweep
		// (`recordScannedEnum`'s `otherwise break`), truncating the layout and misassigning every later case's
		// tag.
		let payloads = try self.readOptionalPayloadFields()

		if not self.at(TokenKind.equals) 'noRawValue'
			// A bare or payload-only case takes the auto-increment tag and requires integer backing.
			try self.appendAutoTaggedEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, ledger: ledger, caseTok: nameTok, payloads: payloads)
			return
		end 'noRawValue'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)

		// A FLOAT raw value, positive or NEGATED (`= 1.1`, `= -2.2`): its tag is the IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN
		// (matched by an i64 compare, exactly as an int tag is), and its display is the float's source
		// text. A leading `-` folds into the sign bit via `negatedFloatBits` — the same spelling
		// `parseNegatedNumericLiteral` uses in expression position — because a negated float is still a
		// FLOAT, not the int path `readEnumIntRawValue` would send it down (where the float literal after
		// the minus is a type mismatch, not the negation the source wrote).
		let floatNegated = self.at(TokenKind.minus) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.floatLiteral

		if floatNegated or self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'floatValue'
			if floatNegated 'consumeMinus'
				try self.consume(TokenKind.minus)
			end 'consumeMinus'

			let floatTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.floatLiteral)
			let magnitude = try self.floatLiteralBits(floatTok)
			let bits = negatedFloatBits(magnitude) if floatNegated else magnitude
			let rawDisplay = "-{String.from(floatTok.value)}" if floatNegated else String.from(floatTok.value)
			try self.requireEnumBacking(layout, kind: EnumBacking.float, backingStruct: EnumBackingStruct.none, caseTok: nameTok)
			try self.appendEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, tag: bits, rawValue: rawDisplay, declaresRawValue: true, caseTok: nameTok, payloads: payloads, backingFields: freeze EnumBackingFieldArray.create(0), ledger: ledger)
			return
		end 'floatValue'

		// An EXPLICIT string or character raw value (`json = "application/json"`, `North = 'n'`) — the same
		// two backings the implicit form above fixes, and the same body reads them.
		if tokenKindIsEnumLiteralBacking(self.current().kind) 'literalRawValue'
			let literalTok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			try self.appendLiteralBackedEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, literalTok: literalTok, ledger: ledger, caseTok: nameTok, payloads: payloads)
			return
		end 'literalRawValue'

		// A STRUCT raw value (`add = Meta{value: 1}`, `add = Meta.create(1)`) — the case is backed by a
		// compile-time constant struct, and `op.rawValue.value` selects one of its fields. Asked BEFORE the
		// function arm because both open with a bare identifier; the two lookaheads are complementary, so the
		// order is a reading convenience rather than a precedence.
		if self.atEnumStructRawValue() 'structValue'
			try self.appendStructBackedEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, ledger: ledger, caseTok: nameTok, payloads: payloads)
			return
		end 'structValue'

		// A FUNCTION raw value (`doubleOp = doubleFn`) — the case is backed by a reference to a declared
		// function, and `Op.doubleOp.rawValue` hands that function back as a callable value.
		if self.atEnumFunctionRawValue() 'functionValue'
			try self.appendFunctionBackedEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, ledger: ledger, caseTok: nameTok, payloads: payloads)
			return
		end 'functionValue'

		// An INTEGER raw value (possibly negated). It resets the auto-increment counter to value + 1,
		// which is what makes `enum E { a = 5  b }` give `b` the tag 6.
		let intValue = try self.readEnumIntRawValue()
		try self.requireEnumBacking(layout, kind: EnumBacking.integer, backingStruct: EnumBackingStruct.none, caseTok: nameTok)
		ledger.nextTag = intValue + 1
		try self.appendEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, tag: intValue, rawValue: "{intValue}", declaresRawValue: true, caseTok: nameTok, payloads: payloads, backingFields: freeze EnumBackingFieldArray.create(0), ledger: ledger)
	end 'readEnumCaseInto'

	// The payload list a case opens with (`circle(radius Real)`), or an empty one where it writes none.
	// Written as its own reader because "does this case declare payload slots" is now a question ASKED
	// rather than a route TAKEN — see `readEnumCaseInto`, where the answer no longer decides whether a raw
	// value may follow.
	function readOptionalPayloadFields() returns PayloadFieldArray throws SourceParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'noPayloadList'
			return PayloadFieldArray.create(0)
		end 'noPayloadList'

		return try self.readPayloadFieldsInto()
	end 'readOptionalPayloadFields'

	// The auto-increment half of a case that writes no raw value of its own — a bare case (`green`) and a
	// payload case (`circle(r Real)`), which differ only in the payload list they carry. ONE body, because
	// the two arms had one between them: the tag rule and the backing it fixes written twice is a tag rule
	// free to drift, and a case's tag is the fact every `Color.red` and every `match` arm reads back.
	function appendAutoTaggedEnumCase(layout EnumLayout, caseName ByteArray, ledger EnumCaseLedger, caseTok Token, payloads PayloadFieldArray) throws SourceParseError
		try self.requireEnumBacking(layout, kind: EnumBacking.integer, backingStruct: EnumBackingStruct.none, caseTok: caseTok)
		let tag = self.nextAutoEnumTag(ledger)
		try self.appendEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, tag: tag, rawValue: "{tag}", declaresRawValue: false, caseTok: caseTok, payloads: payloads, backingFields: freeze EnumBackingFieldArray.create(0), ledger: ledger)
	end 'appendAutoTaggedEnumCase'

	// Take the counter's next tag and step it — the tag every case gets that does not write a NUMBER of its
	// own: a bare case, a payload case, and a STRING-backed one, whose raw value is not a number and so
	// cannot be its tag.
	function nextAutoEnumTag(ledger EnumCaseLedger) returns FullRangeInt
		let tag = ledger.nextTag
		ledger.nextTag = ledger.nextTag + 1
		return tag
	end 'nextAutoEnumTag'

	// ⭐⭐ **A STRING- OR CHARACTER-BACKED CASE, IN EITHER OF THE TWO SPELLINGS THAT REACH IT** — the literal
	// standing in the NAME position (`"North"`, `'N'`) or written after an `=` (`json = "application/json"`,
	// `North = 'n'`). One body, because the two differ only in WHERE the literal was written: the backing it
	// fixes, the tag it takes and the raw value it records are identical, and `/specs/enum-full.md` declares
	// both spellings of both backings.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TWO BACKINGS TAG DIFFERENTLY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT SEPARATES THEM.** A character raw
	// value IS a number — its Unicode codepoint — so the codepoint IS the tag, and every tag-keyed rule
	// (E3031, `.ordinal`, a `match`'s i64 compare) reads it unchanged. A STRING raw value is not a number, so
	// the case takes the ordinary auto-increment tag and its bytes ride `EnumLayout.caseRawValues`, which is
	// what E3031 compares for that backing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CHARACTER ARM DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TOUCH `counter`, UNLIKE THE INT RAW-VALUE ARM.** An
	// int raw value resets it because a BARE case may legally follow one (`a = 5 b` gives `b` the tag
	// 6); no case of any kind may follow a character one without agreeing on the backing, and every
	// kind that could continue the count — bare, payload, int — fixes `integer` and so raises E3032 at
	// `requireEnumBacking` first. A reset here would therefore be a store nothing can read.
	function appendLiteralBackedEnumCase(layout EnumLayout, caseName ByteArray, literalTok Token, ledger EnumCaseLedger, caseTok Token, payloads PayloadFieldArray) throws SourceParseError
		if literalTok.kind == TokenKind.charLiteral 'characterBacking'
			let codepoint = try self.enumCharacterRawValue(literalTok)
			try self.requireEnumBacking(layout, kind: EnumBacking.character, backingStruct: EnumBackingStruct.none, caseTok: caseTok)
			try self.appendEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, tag: codepoint, rawValue: String.from(try self.charLiteralBytes(literalTok)), declaresRawValue: true, caseTok: caseTok, payloads: payloads, backingFields: freeze EnumBackingFieldArray.create(0), ledger: ledger)
			return
		end 'characterBacking'

		let bytes = try self.decodedStringLiteralBytes(literalTok)
		try self.requireEnumBacking(layout, kind: EnumBacking.string, backingStruct: EnumBackingStruct.none, caseTok: caseTok)
		let tag = self.nextAutoEnumTag(ledger)
		try self.appendEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, tag: tag, rawValue: String.from(bytes), declaresRawValue: true, caseTok: caseTok, payloads: payloads, backingFields: freeze EnumBackingFieldArray.create(0), ledger: ledger)
	end 'appendLiteralBackedEnumCase'

	// ⭐ **IS THE CURSOR ON A FUNCTION RAW VALUE — `= doubleFn` — RATHER THAN ON A STRUCT ONE?** Both are
	// written as a bare identifier after the `=`, and the ONE token behind it is what separates them: a
	// struct backing continues into a construction (`Point{…}`) or a static call (`Point.create(…)`), a
	// function reference stops at the name. That one-token lookahead is what lets the two readers partition
	// the identifier-headed raw values between them so neither can claim the other's.
	//
	// ⚠ **STATED POSITIVELY — WHAT MAY FOLLOW — AND NOT AS A BLACKLIST OF `{` AND `.`.** A function raw value
	// IS the whole raw value, so the next token must end the case: the newline before the next member, or the
	// `end` that closes the body. Written as "anything but `{` or `.`", `= someFn(3)` was admitted, the name
	// taken as the backing, and the leftover `(3)` surfaced from the members loop as a malformed CASE NAME —
	// a diagnostic at the wrong token saying nothing about raw values. A blacklist also has to be re-audited
	// by every construct that ever puts a new token after a name; this cannot be widened wrongly.
	//
	// A directory-qualified name (`= beta.doubleFn`) is the same raw value one route longer, so the name ends
	// where its dotted chain does.
	function atEnumFunctionRawValue() returns bool
		if not self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'notAName'
			return false
		end 'notAName'

		return self.statementEndsAt(self.pos + self.dottedChainLengthAt(self.pos) * TokensPerQualifierSegment + 1)
	end 'atEnumFunctionRawValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **A FUNCTION-BACKED CASE (`doubleOp = doubleFn`) — the DECLARATION half, which records a NAME and
	// nothing else.** The address is not knowable here and deliberately is not looked for: the function may
	// be declared later in the file or in another file entirely (`function-backing.forward-reference`,
	// `.cross-file`), and this same reader runs inside the tolerant declaration SWEEP, which has no
	// whole-program index to ask. So the case carries the function's NAME on `caseRawValues` — resolved in the
	// declaring file (`caseFunctionName`) — and the callable value is materialized per read
	// (`Parser.emitEnumCaseConstant`), which is also what makes the binding whole-program rather than file-ordered.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TAG IS THE AUTO-INCREMENT DECLARATION INDEX**, for the STRING backing's reason exactly:
	// a function reference is not a number, so it cannot be one. E3031 then compares the recorded
	// NAMES (`EnumCaseLedger.rawValueIsClaimed` past `rawValueIdentity`), which is what refuses `a = f
	// b = f`.
	function appendFunctionBackedEnumCase(layout EnumLayout, caseName ByteArray, ledger EnumCaseLedger, caseTok Token, payloads PayloadFieldArray) throws SourceParseError
		let functionTok = self.current()
		let qualifierDots = self.dottedChainLengthAt(self.pos)
		let writtenName = functionTok.value if qualifierDots == 0 else self.assembleDottedChainAt(self.pos, dots: qualifierDots)
		self.pos = self.pos + qualifierDots * TokensPerQualifierSegment + 1

		try self.requireEnumBacking(layout, kind: EnumBacking.functionRef, backingStruct: EnumBackingStruct.none, caseTok: caseTok)
		let tag = self.nextAutoEnumTag(ledger)
		try self.appendEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, tag: tag, rawValue: String.from(try self.caseFunctionName(writtenName, qualifierDots: qualifierDots, functionTok: functionTok, caseTok: caseTok)), declaresRawValue: true, caseTok: caseTok, payloads: payloads, backingFields: freeze EnumBackingFieldArray.create(0), ledger: ledger)
	end 'appendFunctionBackedEnumCase'

	// ⭐ **THE DECLARATION A CASE'S FUNCTION NAME MEANS, DECIDED IN THE FILE THAT DECLARES THE ENUM.** Every read
	// of the case materializes the address from the recorded name in whatever file reads it, so resolving there
	// would let one case call different functions in files that see different competitors. Recorded resolved
	// before `appendEnumCase` so E3031 compares two cases' names on the same footing.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE NAME MUST STAND AS A FUNCTION VALUE, AND THIS IS WHERE THAT IS ASKED** — not the compiler's own,
	// declared, non-throwing, and returning something a single result register can carry — through the gate
	// `emitFunctionRef` applies to `let f = doubleFn`, since the case IS a function reference written in a
	// declaration. Asked once, at the declaration, whether or not any program reads the enum, and quoting the name
	// as written. A read of the case therefore emits its reference without asking again.
	//
	// ⚠ The sweep records the name as written, because no contest is known while it reads, and swallows throws,
	// which would truncate the layout; `ProgramSignatures.settleFunctionBackedEnumCases` resolves the layouts it
	// filed once the contest is known.
	//
	// A dotted name backs a case only as the value door reads one
	// (`parseNamespaceQualifiedFunctionValue`): a directory-qualified function, never a type's method.
	function caseFunctionName(writtenName immutable ByteArray, qualifierDots ElementIndex, functionTok Token, caseTok Token) returns immutable ByteArray throws SourceParseError
		if not self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'theContestIsNotKnownYet'
			return writtenName
		end 'theContestIsNotKnownYet'

		let resolved = try self.caseFunctionDeclaration(writtenName, qualifierDots: qualifierDots, functionTok: functionTok)
		try self.requireNameIsUsableAsFunctionValue(Token.create(TokenKind.identifier, line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column, value: writtenName), funcName: resolved)
		return resolved
	end 'caseFunctionName'

	function caseFunctionDeclaration(writtenName ByteArray, qualifierDots ElementIndex, functionTok Token) returns immutable ByteArray throws SourceParseError
		if qualifierDots == 0 'aBareName'
			return try self.resolvedFunctionValueName(writtenName, anchor: functionTok, use: BareNameUse.enumCaseBacking)
		end 'aBareName'

		let headIndex = (self.pos - (qualifierDots * TokensPerQualifierSegment + 1))

		match resolveChain(self.tokens, at: headIndex, signatures: self.signatures, readerFilePath: self.job.path, headIsAValue: false, use: ChainUse.memberReference) 'chain'
			declaredFunction(callee, _) then return callee
			unqualified or
			value or
			declaredType or
			ambiguous or
			nothing then throw SourceParseError.undefinedVariable(String.from(writtenName), line: functionTok.line, column: functionTok.column)
		end 'chain'
	end 'caseFunctionDeclaration'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME OF CASE `index`'S BACKING FUNCTION — the ONE reader of the convention that, for this
	// backing, `caseRawValues` holds a function's name.** The declaration check's two sides, the read's reference
	// and `enumFunctionBackingGoverningName` all want it, and a convention restated at each is one nobody can change.
	//
	// ⚠ **`FirstCaseIndex` IS A LEGAL ARGUMENT FOR ANY FUNCTION-BACKED LAYOUT, and that is a property of the
	// backing rather than of the caller.** The backing is fixed BY a case (`requireEnumBacking` runs from
	// inside the case reader), so `EnumBacking.functionRef` implies at least one case exists — which is what
	// lets `enumFunctionBackingGoverningName` index case 0 without asking, and what the panic below says if
	// the implication is ever broken.
	function enumCaseFunctionName(layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex) returns immutable ByteArray
		if index >= layout.caseCount() 'noSuchCase'
			panic("Parser.enumCaseFunctionName: `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` is function-backed but has no case {index} — a backing is fixed BY a case, so a function-backed layout has at least one and `FirstCaseIndex` is always in range")
		end 'noSuchCase'

		return freeze layout.rawValueAt(index).toByteArray()
	end 'enumCaseFunctionName'

	// ⭐⭐ **A FUNCTION-BACKED CASE'S SIGNATURE MUST BE THE ONE THE ENUM ALREADY FIXED, ASKED AT THE DECLARATION.**
	// `/specs/enum-function-backing.md` states it — *"All cases must share the same function signature"* — and the
	// compiler depends on it: `.rawValue` hands back ONE value whose governing name is the FIRST case's function, so
	// a second case with a different shape would be called through a promise it does not keep. The parser cannot
	// decide it (a shape lives in the whole-program `funcSignatures` and is compared RESOLVED), so it is recorded as
	// a `PendingFunctionTypeDoor` and `SemanticCheck.checkFunctionTypeDoors` answers it.
	//
	// ⚠ **REAL PARSE ONLY, and that is why it is HERE and not in `readEnumCaseInto`.** The tolerant sweep shares
	// that reader, and it runs before the whole-program index this asks exists.
	function checkFunctionBackedCase(layout EnumLayout, caseTok Token)
		if layout.backing != EnumBacking.functionRef 'notFunctionBacked'
			return
		end 'notFunctionBacked'

		let index = layout.caseCount() - 1

		// The FIRST case fixes the signature, so it has nothing to be compared against.
		if index == FirstCaseIndex 'fixesTheSignature'
			return
		end 'fixesTheSignature'

		self.recordFunctionTypeDoorByName(self.enumCaseFunctionName(layout, index: FirstCaseIndex), gotName: self.enumCaseFunctionName(layout, index: index), door: FunctionTypeDoor.enumCaseBacking, place: FunctionTypeDoorPlace.named(layout.caseNameAt(index)), anchor: caseTok, handedAt: caseTok)
	end 'checkFunctionBackedCase'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERYTHING A STRUCT-BACKED CASE OWES, ASKED AT THE DECLARATION — once per case, whether or not any
	// program ever reads a field of it.** `checkFunctionBackedCase`'s twin, called from the same per-case loop
	// for the same reason, and REAL PARSE ONLY for the same reason: the tolerant sweep shares the case reader
	// and swallows its throws, which would truncate the layout and misassign every later case's tag.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHY IT CAN ASK WHAT THE READER COULD NOT: the whole-program signature index is COMPLETE by the
	// time any file is parsed.** So `structOf` answers here, where inside the sweep it could not — which is
	// what lets the four rules below be facts about the DECLARATION rather than about whichever field some
	// expression happened to read. That is the whole point of the position: **a field nobody reads was never
	// validated at all** while these lived at the read site.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE TYPE RULE IS THE ONE THAT KEEPS OUT A COMPILER PANIC AND A WILD POINTER.** Without it,
	// `readEnumBackingFieldValue` admits any of its four value forms into any field: a `FullRangeFloat` field written
	// `1.5` panics the x64 emitter (*"a register-to-register move from rax to xmm0 crosses register
	// files"*) and a `String` field written `1` compiles clean and ACCESS-VIOLATES at run time, dereferencing
	// a String record address of 1. Both are MEASURED; see `EnumBackingFieldValue.literal`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT ASKS ALL FOUR AT EVERY DEPTH, BECAUSE IT RECURSES INTO THE NESTED GROUPS**
	// (`/specs/enum-nested-struct-backing.md`). `OpMeta.create(4, isMemory: true)` short of `isCall` is the
	// same defect one level down as `X64OpMeta.create(…)` short of `setsFlags`, and a rule that stopped at
	// the outermost group would be a door with a hole exactly the shape of the nested-group feature.
	function checkStructBackedCase(layout EnumLayout, caseTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		if layout.backing != EnumBacking.structRecord 'notStructBacked'
			return
		end 'notStructBacked'

		let index = layout.caseCount() - 1

		try self.checkEnumBackingGroup(try self.requireEnumBackingStruct(layout, anchor: caseTok), entries: layout.backingFieldsAt(index), group: RootBackingGroup, layout: layout, index: index, caseTok: caseTok)
	end 'checkStructBackedCase'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE FOUR RULES, ASKED OF ONE GROUP** — the entries filling one constant struct, which is the
	// case's own backing at `RootBackingGroup` and a nested constant at every deeper call. Every rule below
	// is about ONE struct and the entries that fill it, so recursing is the whole of what nesting costs.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RECURSION IS DRIVEN BY WHAT WAS *WRITTEN*, NOT BY THE TYPE GRAPH, AND THAT IS WHY IT
	// TERMINATES WITHOUT A DEPTH GUARD.** It descends only into an entry that wrote a nested constant, and
	// the source text holding those entries is finite. It could NOT rest on types being acyclic: E4014 is
	// checked by `TypeCycleCheck` after every file is parsed (`Compiler.maxon`), so a `type A  export let a
	// as A end` reaches this door FIRST — and here it is simply a case that fails the missing-field rule at
	// whatever depth the author stopped writing.
	function checkEnumBackingGroup(struct StructLayout, entries EnumBackingFieldArray, group EnumBackingGroupId, layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex, caseTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		let filled = FixedBoolArray.create(struct.fieldCount())

		for entry in 0 upto entries.count() 'eachWrittenField'
			let recorded = enumBackingEntryAt(entries, entry: entry as EnumBackingEntryIndex)

			if recorded.group != group 'fillsAnotherStruct'
				continue
			end 'fillsAnotherStruct'

			let field = try self.requireEnumBackingFieldPosition(struct, entries: entries, recorded: recorded, caseTok: caseTok)

			// ⚠ **A DUPLICATE IS CAUGHT BY THE FILLED COLUMN, WHICH IS WHY THERE IS ONE.** The resolution scan in
			// `enumBackingFieldConstant` returns on its first hit and visits none of the rest, so `Meta{value: 1,
			// value: 2}` would otherwise keep the FIRST and silently drop the second.
			//
			// It reports through the LITERAL's own voice rather than a second sentence for one defect: such a
			// literal is "initialized twice" wherever it is written, and a factory call in this position IS
			// that literal (see `readEnumBackingFields`). Anchored at the CASE, the token every per-case
			// declaration diagnostic names and the position the oracle uses.
			let alreadyFilled = try filled.get(field) otherwise panic("checkEnumBackingGroup: filled.get OOB at {field} below count {filled.count()}")

			if alreadyFilled 'writtenTwice'
				throw SourceParseError.duplicateFieldInitializer(String.from(struct.name), fieldName: String.from(struct.fieldNameAt(field)), line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
			end 'writtenTwice'

			try filled.set(field, value: true) otherwise panic("checkEnumBackingGroup: filled.set OOB at {field} below count {filled.count()}")

			// The type rule ANSWERS with the domain it decided on, so the descent below reads the one
			// classification this field was admitted under rather than asking a second time — the shape that
			// keeps "what the door admitted" and "where the door recurses" from ever being two answers.
			let domain = try self.requireEnumBackingFieldValueSuitsField(struct, field: field, value: recorded.value, caseTok: caseTok)

			if not domainIsEnumBackingNestedRecord(domain) 'aScalarLeaf'
				continue
			end 'aScalarLeaf'

			try self.checkEnumBackingGroup(self.enumBackingNestedStruct(struct, field: field), entries: entries, group: nestedBackingGroupOf(entry as EnumBackingEntryIndex), layout: layout, index: index, caseTok: caseTok)
		end 'eachWrittenField'

		// ⛔⛔ EVERY DECLARED FIELD MUST BE WRITTEN, AND A DEFAULT DOES NOT EXCUSE ONE. The answer is
		// `E3018: missing field 'value' in struct raw value for 'Meta'`, at the case, for BOTH `Meta{}`
		// on a one-field struct AND a struct whose omitted field declares `= 9`. Asked here rather than
		// at the read for this door's whole reason: the read only ever visits the field some expression
		// named, so a case short of a field nobody reads was admitted silently.
		for field in 0 upto struct.fieldCount() 'eachDeclaredField'
			let wasWritten = try filled.get(field) otherwise panic("checkEnumBackingGroup: filled.get OOB at {field} below count {filled.count()}")

			if not wasWritten 'neverWritten'
				throw self.enumBackingMissingFieldError(struct.name, fieldName: struct.fieldNameAt(field), layout: layout, index: index, anchor: caseTok)
			end 'neverWritten'
		end 'eachDeclaredField'
	end 'checkEnumBackingGroup'

	// ⭐ THE ONE SENTENCE FOR "this case supplies no constant for that field", rendered here because it has
	// TWO throwers and must not become two rules: this door (which asks it of every declared field, at the
	// declaration) and `enumBackingFieldConstant` (which meets it at a read).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE READ'S COPY IS NOT DEAD, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A DIAGNOSTIC RATHER THAN A PANIC.** The door
	// runs during the DECLARING file's real parse, and a file that READS the enum may be parsed first — so a
	// read can reach the gap before the door has reported it. A panic there would beat a diagnostic to the
	// user for a program that has a perfectly good one waiting.
	//
	// It is deliberately NOT `fieldNotInitialized`, whose sentence ends "and it has no default value": the
	// rule here is stricter than a literal's, since a defaulted field is refused too, so that clause would be
	// false exactly where this differs from a literal.
	function enumBackingMissingFieldError(structName ByteArray, fieldName ByteArray, layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex, anchor Token) returns SourceParseError
		return SourceParseError.unsupported("field '{String.from(fieldName)}' of the `{String.from(structName)}` backing, which case '{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(index))}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` writes no value for — a struct backing supplies one constant PER CASE for every field, defaults included, because reading one field selects it from every case", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'enumBackingMissingFieldError'

	// WHICH declared field an entry fills — its LABEL's field, or the field at its own POSITION. Both ways of
	// being wrong are reported here: a label naming no declared field, and a positional entry past the last
	// one (`Meta.create(1, 2, 3)` on a one-field struct, whose surplus entries nothing ever visited).
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS THE INVERSE OF `enumBackingEntryFills`, AND THE MATCHING CLAUSES ARE ITS.** The door needs
	// ENTRY→FIELD (only that direction can name the field a surplus positional claims) where the read needs
	// FIELD→ENTRY, so the two cannot be one function — but the RULE they share is written once, there.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COUNT IN THE SENTENCE IS THE GROUP'S, NOT THIS ENTRY'S POSITION.** It read
	// `recorded.position + 1`, which is where the FIRST offender sits: `Meta.create(1, 2, 3)` on a one-field
	// struct announced *"2 argument(s)"* about a three-argument call. A diagnostic that states a wrong number
	// is worse than one that states none.
	function requireEnumBackingFieldPosition(struct StructLayout, entries EnumBackingFieldArray, recorded EnumBackingField, caseTok Token) returns FieldIndex throws SourceParseError
		if recorded.name.isEmpty() 'positional'
			if recorded.position >= struct.fieldCount() 'pastTheLastField'
				throw SourceParseError.unsupported("{enumBackingGroupEntryCount(entries, group: recorded.group)} argument(s) to the `{String.from(struct.name)}` constant, which declares {struct.fieldCount()} field(s) — a factory call in this position is read as the struct LITERAL its arguments fill, so an argument past the last field names nothing", line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
			end 'pastTheLastField'

			return recorded.position
		end 'positional'

		return try self.requireFieldIndex(struct, name: recorded.name, anchor: caseTok)
	end 'requireEnumBackingFieldPosition'

	// ⭐ **THE FIELD `name` DENOTES, OR E3018 — one door for the two askers**, because a name the DOOR
	// admits and one the READ admits must be the same set and nothing else made them: the declaration's label
	// check (`requireEnumBackingFieldPosition`) and `emitEnumBackingFieldSelect` were the identical
	// `declaresField`-then-`indexOfField` pair under the identical constructor, which is also two linear scans
	// of one column where one answers both.
	function requireFieldIndex(struct StructLayout, name ByteArray, anchor Token) returns FieldIndex throws SourceParseError
		return try struct.indexOfField(name) otherwise throw SourceParseError.unknownField(String.from(struct.name), fieldName: String.from(name), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireFieldIndex'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE WRITTEN CONSTANT SUIT THE FIELD'S DECLARED TYPE?** — the rule whose absence was the
	// panic and the wild pointer. It is asked against `enumBackingFieldDomainOf`, the SAME classifier the
	// read's decode is exhaustive over, so the door admits exactly what the read can produce a value for.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT HANDS BACK THE DOMAIN IT DECIDED ON**, which is what the door's descent into a nested constant
	// reads. Re-asking the classifier there would be the same question answered twice, free to disagree the
	// day either half changes — and "the field this recursed into is the field the rule admitted" is the
	// property that keeps a nested group being checked against the struct it actually fills.
	function requireEnumBackingFieldValueSuitsField(struct StructLayout, field FieldIndex, value EnumBackingFieldValue, caseTok Token) returns EnumBackingFieldDomain throws SourceParseError
		let fieldType = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(struct, index: field, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
		let fieldName = String.from(struct.fieldNameAt(field))
		let domain = self.enumBackingFieldDomainOf(fieldType)

		// The FIELD's type and the CONSTANT written into it are two different faults, and they get two
		// sentences. This one is shared with the READ (see `enumBackingUnsupportedFieldError`).
		try self.requireEnumBackingFieldTypeIsSupported(struct.name, fieldName: struct.fieldNameAt(field), fieldType: fieldType, domain: domain, anchor: caseTok)

		if enumBackingValueSuitsDomain(value, domain: domain, declaredName: self.enumBackingFieldTypeIdentity(fieldType, domain: domain)) 'suits'
			return domain
		end 'suits'

		throw SourceParseError.unsupported("the constant written for field '{fieldName}' of the `{String.from(struct.name)}` backing, which is declared `{maxonTypeDisplayName(fieldType, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, signatures: self.signatures)}` — a backing field takes an INT literal for an integer field, a `true`/`false` for a `bool` field, an int or float literal for a float field, `<ThatEnum>.<case>` of THAT enum for a field of a declared enum, and a nested constant of THAT type (a literal or a factory call) for a field of a declared `type`", line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
	end 'requireEnumBackingFieldValueSuitsField'

	// The domain gate, spelled as a `throws` so both askers can use it — the door below (once per declared
	// case) and the READ (`emitEnumBackingFieldSelect`).
	function requireEnumBackingFieldTypeIsSupported(structName ByteArray, fieldName ByteArray, fieldType MaxonType, domain EnumBackingFieldDomain, anchor Token) throws SourceParseError
		if not domainIsEnumBackingUnsupported(domain) 'holdable'
			return
		end 'holdable'

		throw self.enumBackingUnsupportedFieldError(structName, fieldName: fieldName, fieldType: fieldType, anchor: anchor)
	end 'requireEnumBackingFieldTypeIsSupported'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE SENTENCE FOR "that field's declared type has no constant this column can hold"** —
	// `enumBackingMissingFieldError`'s twin, and it has two throwers for the identical reason, which THIS DIFF
	// established by reading the pipeline rather than by assuming: `queryProgramSignatures` sweeps every file
	// before any real parse, but `parseModule` is one flat source-order loop and `parseFunction` parses and
	// lowers bodies inline — so a read in a function declared ABOVE the `enum` in the same file reaches the
	// read's gate before the declaration door ever runs, and across files `Queries.maxon` walks
	// `project.db.sourcePaths` with nothing sorting it. A panic on that path would beat a perfectly good
	// diagnostic to the user.
	//
	// ⚠ It names the FIELD's type, never the constant written into it: a `String`-typed backing field is
	// wrong whatever is written there, and the author's fix is the field's type or the backing's shape.
	function enumBackingUnsupportedFieldError(structName ByteArray, fieldName ByteArray, fieldType MaxonType, anchor Token) returns SourceParseError
		return SourceParseError.unsupported("field '{String.from(fieldName)}' of the `{String.from(structName)}` backing is declared `{maxonTypeName(fieldType, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames)}`, which has no constant a struct backing can hold — a backing field is an integer, a `bool`, a float, a PAYLOAD-FREE enum, or a declared `type` whose own fields are again those, because the select that reads one field produces a single i64 per case and a nested record is DESCENDED INTO rather than selected. A `{String.from(StringConformerName)}`, a `{String.from(CharacterConformerName)}`, an array, an interface, a function, or a payload-bearing union is a heap value that i64 would only be an address of", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'enumBackingUnsupportedFieldError'

	// The TYPE a field of one of the two NAMED domains denotes — the identity a written `<Type>.<member>`
	// must match, so `Config{mode: Priority.low}` on a `Mode`-typed field is refused and so is
	// `X64OpMeta.create(Other.create(1), …)` on an `OpMeta`-typed one. Empty for every other domain, where no
	// value naming a type is admitted at all and the name is never compared.
	function enumBackingFieldTypeIdentity(fieldType MaxonType, domain EnumBackingFieldDomain) returns immutable ByteArray
		match domain 'd'
			enumWord or
			nestedRecord then return self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
			integerWord or
			booleanWord or
			floatBits or
			unsupported then return EmptySourceText
		end 'd'
	end 'enumBackingFieldTypeIdentity'

	// ⭐ **THE STRUCT A `nestedRecord` FIELD NAMES — the group's own declaration**, asked by the door as it
	// recurses and by the read as it descends, so both check a nested group against the same layout.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PANIC'S GUARD IS `enumBackingRecordFieldDomain`, WHICH PROBED THIS EXACT NAME.** A field is
	// classified `nestedRecord` only because `structOf` answered `declared` for it, so an `undeclared` here
	// means one name was read two ways — not a program the author could write.
	function enumBackingNestedStruct(struct StructLayout, field FieldIndex) returns StructLayout
		let fieldName = self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(struct, index: field, into: self.artifact.typeNames)))

		match self.signatures.structFor(fieldName, readerFilePath: self.job.path) 'declaring'
			declared(nested) then return nested
			undeclared then panic("enumBackingNestedStruct: field '{String.from(struct.fieldNameAt(field))}' of `type {String.from(struct.name)}` is declared `{String.from(fieldName)}`, which was classified a NESTED RECORD and yet no file declares a `type` of that name — `enumBackingRecordFieldDomain` reaches that classification only through `structOf` answering `declared` for this very name")
		end 'declaring'
	end 'enumBackingNestedStruct'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A FIELD'S DECLARED TYPE DOES TO THE i64 THE SELECT PRODUCES — THE ONE CLASSIFIER**, read by
	// the door above and by `emitEnumBackingFieldSelect`'s decode. See `EnumBackingFieldDomain`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE NAME IS RESOLVED, NOT APPROXIMATED, AND `rangedAliasResolvedTag` IS WHY.** A field declared
	// `Latency` (a ranged INT alias) and one declared `Ratio` (a ranged FLOAT alias) are both `named`, and
	// `tagIsIntegral` answers true for BOTH — which is exactly the approximation that function's own header
	// warns produces "a compiler crash on a legal-looking program". A `named` that reduces to no primitive is
	// a declared ENUM (or an undeclared name, which is being rejected for the missing type anyway), whose
	// value IS its i64 tag — including a FLOAT-backed enum, whose tag is a bit pattern in a GPR.
	function enumBackingFieldDomainOf(fieldType MaxonType) returns EnumBackingFieldDomain
		let resolved = self.rangedAliasResolvedTag(maxonTypeTag(fieldType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(fieldType))

		if resolved == ValueTypeTag.float 'floatDomain'
			return EnumBackingFieldDomain.floatBits
		end 'floatDomain'

		if resolved == ValueTypeTag.boolean 'boolDomain'
			return EnumBackingFieldDomain.booleanWord
		end 'boolDomain'

		if tagIsPlainInteger(resolved) 'integerDomain'
			return EnumBackingFieldDomain.integerWord
		end 'integerDomain'

		if resolved == ValueTypeTag.named 'aNamedType'
			return self.enumBackingNamedFieldDomain(self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)))
		end 'aNamedType'

		// ⭐ A DECLARED `type` is `structRef` and never `named` — the tag has its own arm precisely so the
		// rules that read `named` cannot absorb a record (`MaxonType.structRef`). It is the one tag with a
		// nested-record domain, and it is asked of the REGISTRY rather than assumed from the tag: a name no
		// file declares is refused rather than descended into.
		if resolved == ValueTypeTag.structRef 'aDeclaredRecord'
			return self.enumBackingRecordFieldDomain(self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)))
		end 'aDeclaredRecord'

		return EnumBackingFieldDomain.unsupported
	end 'enumBackingFieldDomainOf'

	// ⛔⛔ **A `named` FIELD IS A WORD ONLY IF ITS DECLARATION IS UNBOXED — AND `enumOf` HOLDS UNIONS
	// TOO.** `ProgramSignatures.enumTypes` is one registry for `enum` and `union` alike (`isUnion` is
	// a bit on the layout), and a PAYLOAD-BEARING union is a box or a tagged word, with EVERY case
	// taking that form, payload-free ones included (`EnumLayout.carriesPayloads`, and
	// `parseEnumLiteral` says so outright). So its value is never the declaration's bare tag.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED, AND IT IS THIS RUNG'S OWN DEFECT CLASS ONE TYPE OVER.** `union Shape circle(r Num)
	// blank end` + `type Meta export let s as Shape end` + `a = Meta{s: Shape.blank}` COMPILED CLEAN and
	// exited `0xC0000005`: the constant column stored `blank`'s tag, this classifier called it `enumWord`, the
	// select claimed the raw i64 was a `Shape`, and the `match` loaded through it as a box pointer at address
	// 1. That is the *"a String record address of 1"* failure `EnumBackingFieldValue.literal` records, reached
	// through the one arm whose header had asserted *"the i64 is that enum's TAG, which is what an enum value
	// IS"* — true for an enum and for a payload-free union, false for a boxed one.
	function enumBackingNamedFieldDomain(name ByteArray) returns EnumBackingFieldDomain
		let declared = self.signatures.enumFor(name, readerFilePath: self.job.path)

		if not enumMatchIsDeclared(declared) 'notADeclaredEnum'
			return EnumBackingFieldDomain.unsupported
		end 'notADeclaredEnum'

		if requireEnumLayout(declared).carriesPayloads() 'boxedUnion'
			return EnumBackingFieldDomain.unsupported
		end 'boxedUnion'

		return EnumBackingFieldDomain.enumWord
	end 'enumBackingNamedFieldDomain'

	// ⭐⭐ **A FIELD DECLARED AS ANOTHER `type` IS A NESTED RECORD** — a domain of its own
	// (`/specs/enum-nested-struct-backing.md`), and it is admitted on a DIFFERENT premise from the three word
	// domains, which is the whole reason it is its own arm: **nothing is ever SELECTED at this domain.** A
	// record IS a box pointer at run time — the trap `enumBackingNamedFieldDomain` records, one type over —
	// so a select that produced one would be the same wild pointer. Instead the read keeps descending
	// (`resolveEnumBackingLeaf` never returns this domain, and refuses a chain that stops on it) and the door
	// recurses (`checkEnumBackingGroup`), until a SCALAR LEAF is reached, which is a word like any other.
	//
	// ⚠ Every leaf of such a record is classified by `enumBackingFieldDomainOf` itself, so a `String`, an
	// array or a boxed union INSIDE a nested record is refused exactly as one at the top level is.
	function enumBackingRecordFieldDomain(name ByteArray) returns EnumBackingFieldDomain
		match self.signatures.structFor(name, readerFilePath: self.job.path) 'declaring'
			declared then return EnumBackingFieldDomain.nestedRecord
			undeclared then return EnumBackingFieldDomain.unsupported
		end 'declaring'
	end 'enumBackingRecordFieldDomain'

	// The read's gate on the remaining domains — an exhaustive match, so a domain added to
	// `EnumBackingFieldDomain` cannot silently take the word route here.
	//
	// ⚠ One arm asserts and one throws, and the difference is whether the guard is local. `floatBits`
	// is impossible: the caller returns on `domainIsEnumBackingFloatBits` one branch above, so
	// reaching it means one classifier read two ways. `unsupported` rests on the declaration door
	// having already run, an ordering premise that does not hold (see
	// `enumBackingUnsupportedFieldError`).
	function requireEnumBackingFieldDomainIsAWord(step EnumBackingFieldStep, fieldType MaxonType, domain EnumBackingFieldDomain, anchor Token) throws SourceParseError
		match domain 'd'
			integerWord or
			booleanWord or
			enumWord then return
			floatBits then panic("requireEnumBackingFieldDomainIsAWord: field '{String.from(step.fieldName)}' of `type {String.from(step.ownerName)}` is float-domained and reached the word route — the caller returns on `domainIsEnumBackingFloatBits` one branch above, so the two readings of one classifier have come apart")

			// A NESTED RECORD is the domain that is never an answer: `resolveEnumBackingLeaf` either descends
			// through it or refuses the read outright, so the leaf it hands this gate cannot be one — a local
			// guard, in the same descent, which is what makes this an assertion rather than a diagnostic.
			nestedRecord then panic("requireEnumBackingFieldDomainIsAWord: field '{String.from(step.fieldName)}' of `type {String.from(step.ownerName)}` is a NESTED RECORD and reached the word route — `resolveEnumBackingLeaf` loops while `domainIsEnumBackingNestedRecord` holds and refuses a chain that stops on one, so no leaf it returns can carry this domain")
			unsupported then throw self.enumBackingUnsupportedFieldError(step.ownerName, fieldName: step.fieldName, fieldType: fieldType, anchor: anchor)
		end 'd'
	end 'requireEnumBackingFieldDomainIsAWord'

	// ⭐ **IS THE CURSOR ON A STRUCT RAW VALUE — `= Meta{value: 1}` or `= Meta.create(1)`?** The twin of
	// `atEnumFunctionRawValue`, and it must be POSITIVE ABOUT THE WHOLE FORM for that function's exact reason.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A BARE `identifier .` IS NOT ENOUGH, AND CLAIMING IT MISROUTES A DIAGNOSTIC ONE CONSTRUCT OVER.**
	// It read `leftBrace or dot` for one edit, so `= Priority.low` — an author reaching for an enum member —
	// was claimed as a struct raw value: the reader ate `Priority`, `.`, `low`, then demanded the factory's
	// `(` and reported **E2010 `Expected '(' but got 'newline'`**, the wrong token, saying nothing about raw
	// values. MEASURED. That is verbatim the failure `atEnumFunctionRawValue`'s header was written to prevent
	// (*"a blacklist also has to be re-audited by every construct that ever puts a new token after a name"*),
	// reintroduced beside it.
	//
	// ⇒ the factory arm names all four of its tokens, so anything that is not a whole `<Type>.<member>(`
	// falls through to `readEnumIntRawValue`, whose refusal now enumerates the six legal raw values at the
	// token the author actually wrote.
	function atEnumStructRawValue() returns bool
		if not self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'notATypeName'
			return false
		end 'notATypeName'

		if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftBrace 'structLiteral'
			return true
		end 'structLiteral'

		return self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot and tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2)) and self.kindAt(self.pos + 3) == TokenKind.leftParen
	end 'atEnumStructRawValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **A STRUCT-BACKED CASE (`add = Meta{value: 1}`, `add = Meta.create(1)`) — the DECLARATION half,
	// which records the struct's NAME and the fields' CONSTANTS and resolves neither.**
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TAG IS THE AUTO-INCREMENT DECLARATION INDEX**, for the STRING and FUNCTION backings' reason
	// exactly: a struct value is not a number, so it cannot be one. What is different is E3031 — those two
	// backings compare their recorded column and this one compares NOTHING, because two cases may legally
	// carry equal struct backings (`EnumRawValueIdentity.incomparable`, which carries the measurement).
	//
	// ⚠ **WHY NOTHING IS RESOLVED HERE: this reader runs inside the tolerant declaration SWEEP**, the pass
	// that builds the whole-program index — so `structOf` (which field is position 0?) and `enumOf` (what tag
	// is `Priority.low`?) have no answer yet. Both are asked at the USE (`emitEnumBackingFieldSelect`). See
	// `EnumBackingField`.
	function appendStructBackedEnumCase(layout EnumLayout, caseName ByteArray, ledger EnumCaseLedger, caseTok Token, payloads PayloadFieldArray) throws SourceParseError
		let typeTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.requireEnumBacking(layout, kind: EnumBacking.structRecord, backingStruct: EnumBackingStruct.declared(typeTok.value), caseTok: caseTok)
		let fields = try self.readEnumBackingFields(typeTok)
		let tag = self.nextAutoEnumTag(ledger)

		// The struct's own name is what `caseRawValues` can carry of a value that is not one — the column's
		// "how the raw value was written" for a backing whose fields ride a column of their own. Nothing reads
		// it back: E3031 and the decl hash both route through `rawValueIdentity`, which answers `incomparable`.
		try self.appendEnumCase(layout, caseName: caseName, tag: tag, rawValue: String.from(typeTok.value), declaresRawValue: true, caseTok: caseTok, payloads: payloads, backingFields: fields, ledger: ledger)
	end 'appendStructBackedEnumCase'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO SPELLINGS OF A CONSTANT STRUCT, AND THEY PRODUCE THE IDENTICAL FIELD LIST.** The cursor
	// sits just past the type name, on `{` or on `.`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **A FACTORY CALL IS PURE SYNTAX HERE — THE CALLEE IS NEVER RESOLVED AND ITS BODY IS NEVER READ —
	// AND THAT IS THE LANGUAGE'S RULE, NOT AN ASSUMPTION THIS COMPILER MAKES.** On
	// `enum TestOp  add = Meta.create(1)  mul = Meta.create(3) end`:
	//
	//   1. a `create` whose body is `return Self{value: other + 20}` — with the PARAMETER named `other`, not
	//      `value` — still answers `op.rawValue.value == 3`, so the body is not consulted;
	//   2. the same written `Meta.make(1)` answers 3, so the member's spelling is not `create`;
	//   3. the same with **no such static declared at all** answers 3, so nothing is looked up.
	//
	// ⇒ **Any member name is admitted**, because restricting to `create` would refuse `Meta.make(1)`.
	//
	// ⇒ What an argument means is its POSITION (or its label), and nothing else: `Meta.create(1)` IS
	// `Meta{<field 0>: 1}`. That is why the two forms share one `EnumBackingFieldArray` and one reader.
	function readEnumBackingFields(typeTok Token) returns immutable EnumBackingFieldArray throws SourceParseError
		var fields = EnumBackingFieldArray.create(0) as isolated EnumBackingFieldArray
		try self.readEnumBackingGroupInto(fields, typeTok: typeTok, group: RootBackingGroup)

		return freeze fields
	end 'readEnumBackingFields'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE DELIMITED LIST READER FOR BOTH SPELLINGS.** `{ name: v, name2: v2 }` and `( v, label: v2 )`
	// are the same loop — open, empty check, `push(label, value)`, `,`, close — and they were written out
	// TWICE for one edit, differing in the opener/closer pair, the label reader, and (accidentally) in what
	// an EMPTY list meant. That divergence is the whole argument for folding them: the factory form refused
	// `Meta.create()` with a paragraph while the brace form silently admitted `Meta{}` on a struct with
	// fields — one condition, two verdicts — and every rule this reader gains from here on would otherwise
	// have to be written into both, where a clause added to one reads as correct at both sites.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE EMPTY LIST IS NOW ONE VERDICT: ADMITTED, and adjudicated by the door.** `Meta{}` and
	// `Meta.create()` both record no fields, and `checkStructBackedCase` — which is the only thing that can
	// see how many fields `Meta` actually declares — reports the arity there, once, for both spellings.
	//
	// ⚠ **`labelled` PICKS THE LABEL READER AND NOTHING ELSE.** A brace literal REQUIRES `<name> :` on every
	// entry (the only struct-literal spelling the language has, and what `parseStructLiteral` reads); a
	// factory argument's label is optional, and an unlabelled one means the field at its own POSITION
	// (`EnumBackingField.name`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS THE SAME READER AT EVERY DEPTH — THE ONE CALL THAT MAKES NESTING WORK**
	// (`/specs/enum-nested-struct-backing.md`). `OpMeta.create(1)` written as an ARGUMENT is the identical
	// construct written as a CASE's backing, so it is read by the identical function; what a nested group
	// adds is only where its entries say they belong. Both spellings mix freely at every level, because the
	// brace/factory question is asked per group rather than once for the case.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CURSOR SITS JUST PAST THE TYPE NAME**, on `{` or on `.`, exactly as `readEnumBackingFields`
	// finds it — which is what lets the caller be either `appendStructBackedEnumCase` or this function's own
	// nested-entry arm.
	function readEnumBackingGroupInto(fields mutable isolated EnumBackingFieldArray, typeTok Token, group EnumBackingGroupId) throws SourceParseError
		let labelled = self.at(TokenKind.leftBrace)

		if not labelled 'factoryCall'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)

			// The member's spelling decides nothing (see `readEnumBackingFields`' header), so it is consumed
			// and dropped rather than bound to a name nothing reads.
			//
			// ⚠ **THROUGH THE SAME DOOR `atEnumStructRawValue` ADMITTED IT.** That predicate claims the form on
			// `tokenCanBeAName`, which admits a KEYWORD-spelled member — so a strict `consume(identifier)` here
			// claimed `Meta.default(1)` and then refused it with a token-level *"Expected identifier"*, a
			// predicate and its consumer disagreeing about one token. That is verbatim the defect
			// `readEnumBackingFieldLabel`'s header records fixing for a field's LABEL, here for the member name one line
			// away — and it is reachable at every nesting depth.
			_ = try self.consumeMemberNameToken()
		end 'factoryCall'

		let closer = TokenKind.rightBrace if labelled else TokenKind.rightParen
		self.advance()

		if self.at(closer) 'noFields'
			try self.consume(closer)
			return
		end 'noFields'

		var position = 0 as FieldIndex

		while true 'fieldLoop'
			let label = try self.readEnumBackingFieldLabel(labelled)
			try self.readEnumBackingEntryInto(fields, typeTok: typeTok, group: group, label: label, position: position)
			position = position + 1

			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endFields'
				break
			end 'endFields'

			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'fieldLoop'

		try self.consume(closer)
	end 'readEnumBackingGroupInto'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE ENTRY: A SCALAR CONSTANT, OR A NESTED STRUCT WHOSE OWN ENTRIES FOLLOW IT.** The shape test is
	// `atEnumStructRawValue` — the very predicate that decided the CASE was struct-backed — so "what a
	// constant struct looks like" is one rule rather than one at the case and a second inside it. Anything
	// else (`1`, `true`, `-1.5`, `Priority.low`) is a leaf and goes to `readEnumBackingFieldValue`, whose
	// refusal names the token the author wrote.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OWNER'S INDEX IS TAKEN AFTER ITS OWN PUSH AND BEFORE THE RECURSION**, which is the whole of
	// the bookkeeping: the nested entries are appended to the SAME array (`EnumBackingField`'s header for
	// why the tree cannot hold them) and say which entry they fill a field of.
	function readEnumBackingEntryInto(fields mutable isolated EnumBackingFieldArray, typeTok Token, group EnumBackingGroupId, label immutable ByteArray, position FieldIndex) throws SourceParseError
		try requireMemberCount(fields.count() + 1, limit: MaxEnumBackingEntries, members: "backing entries", anchor: typeTok)

		if not self.atEnumStructRawValue() 'scalarConstant'
			let scalar = EnumBackingField.create(label, position: position, group: group, value: try self.readEnumBackingFieldValue(typeTok))
			fields.push(scalar)
			return
		end 'scalarConstant'

		let nestedTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let owning = EnumBackingField.create(label, position: position, group: group, value: EnumBackingFieldValue.nestedStruct(nestedTok.value))
		fields.push(owning)
		let owner = fields.count() - 1
		let nestedGroup = nestedBackingGroupOf(owner as EnumBackingEntryIndex)

		try self.readEnumBackingGroupInto(fields, typeTok: nestedTok, group: nestedGroup)
	end 'readEnumBackingEntryInto'

	// The field an entry NAMES, or `EmptySourceText` for a positional factory argument.
	//
	// ⭐ **BOTH SPELLINGS ADMIT A KEYWORD-SPELLED LABEL, AND THAT IS ONE RULE RATHER THAN TWO**, so
	// `Meta{bool: 1}` and `Meta.create(bool: 1)` agree on what a field may be called. `tokenCanBeAName` is
	// the same admission `consumeMemberNameToken` applies.
	function readEnumBackingFieldLabel(labelled bool) returns immutable ByteArray throws SourceParseError
		if labelled 'everyEntryNamesItsField'
			let nameTok = try self.consumeMemberNameToken()
			try self.consume(TokenKind.colon)
			return nameTok.value
		end 'everyEntryNamesItsField'

		if not (tokenCanBeAName(self.current()) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.colon) 'positional'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'positional'

		let labelTok = self.current()
		self.advance()
		try self.consume(TokenKind.colon)
		return labelTok.value
	end 'readEnumBackingFieldLabel'

	// ⭐ **ONE *LEAF* FIELD'S VALUE, AND THE ADMITTED SET IS THE SPEC'S OWN.** `/specs/enum-struct-backing.md`
	// bounds it — *"compile-time integer, float, or boolean constants, or nested struct literals"* — and its
	// cases add the enum-member reference (`Priority.low`, `Mode.fast`, `Cat.bool`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE NESTED STRUCT IS NOT ONE OF THEM AND NEVER REACHES HERE**: it is not a leaf, so
	// `readEnumBackingEntryInto` claims it one branch up and this reader only ever sees the constants at the
	// bottom of the tree. What is refused below is a token that is neither.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NAMED TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT IS REFUSED**, though `MAX` reads like a constant: resolving one needs
	// the whole-program index, and this reader runs inside the sweep that builds it. It would have to ride the column unresolved like the enum-case arm does; no case asks.
	function readEnumBackingFieldValue(typeTok Token) returns EnumBackingFieldValue throws SourceParseError
		// A sign belongs to a numeric LITERAL and to nothing else, so it is claimed only when one follows —
		// a lone `-` then falls to the refusal below, positioned at the `-` the author actually wrote.
		let negated = self.at(TokenKind.minus) and (self.peekKind() == TokenKind.intLiteral or self.peekKind() == TokenKind.floatLiteral)

		if negated 'consumeSign'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.minus)
		end 'consumeSign'

		// A float rides as its IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN, the one spelling every float in this compiler travels in
		// — and a leading `-` is a SIGN FLIP into the literal (`negatedFloatBits`), never `0.0 - x`, which is
		// how `-0.0` keeps its sign (see the float raw value's own arm in `readEnumCaseInto`).
		if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'floatValue'
			let floatTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.floatLiteral)
			let magnitude = try self.floatLiteralBits(floatTok)
			return EnumBackingFieldValue.literal(negatedFloatBits(magnitude) if negated else magnitude, kind: EnumBackingLiteralKind.floatLiteral)
		end 'floatValue'

		if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'intValue'
			let intTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.intLiteral)
			return EnumBackingFieldValue.literal(try self.intLiteralValue(intTok, negated: negated), kind: EnumBackingLiteralKind.integerLiteral)
		end 'intValue'

		// A bool's payload IS 0 or 1 (`TypeRules.BoolTrueValue`), which is what lets it share the `literal`
		// arm's `FullRangeInt` with an int rather than needing a truth column of its own. WHICH literal it was still rides
		// beside the bits, because that is what the door type-checks against the field.
		if self.at(TokenKind.true) or self.at(TokenKind.false) 'boolValue'
			let isTrue = self.at(TokenKind.true)
			self.advance()
			return EnumBackingFieldValue.literal(BoolTrueValue if isTrue else BoolFalseValue, kind: EnumBackingLiteralKind.booleanLiteral)
		end 'boolValue'

		// ⭐ `Priority.low` — and the case name is read by `consumeNameToken(NamePosition.enumCase)`, the very
		// reader the DECLARATION of a case goes through, so a KEYWORD-spelled case (`Cat.bool`, `Cat.int`)
		// resolves here exactly as it declares. A strict `identifier` here is the mis-parse the spec's
		// `struct-backing-keyword-enum-case-field` case exists to catch, and the shape the compiler's own `StdType`
		// enum would write (`CastCategory.bool`).
		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'enumCaseValue'
			let enumTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
			let memberTok = try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.enumCase)
			return EnumBackingFieldValue.enumCase(enumTok.value, caseName: memberTok.value)
		end 'enumCaseValue'

		let tok = self.current()
		throw SourceParseError.unsupported("`{tokenDisplayText(tok)}` as a field value of the `{String.from(typeTok.value)}` backing — a case's backing is a COMPILE-TIME constant, so each field is an integer, float or boolean literal, a `<Enum>.<case>` reference, or a nested constant of the field's own `type` written as a literal or a factory call. A named top-level constant cannot be reached at all: this runs inside the declaration sweep that builds the index one would be resolved through", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'readEnumBackingFieldValue'

	// The codepoint a CHARACTER raw value denotes.
	//
	// ⛔ A multi-codepoint grapheme cluster (a ZWJ family emoji, a flag) is a `Character` and nothing else —
	// it has no integer reading (`CharLiteralCodepoint.cluster`) — so it cannot be an enum tag. Refused here
	// rather than silently stored as its first codepoint, which would make two different clusters one case.
	function enumCharacterRawValue(literalTok Token) returns FullRangeInt throws SourceParseError
		match try self.charLiteralCodepoint(literalTok) 'decoded'
			codepoint(value) then return value
			cluster then throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a character raw value that is a multi-codepoint grapheme cluster ('{String.from(literalTok.value)}') — an enum case's tag is one integer, and a cluster has no integer reading", line: literalTok.line, column: literalTok.column)
		end 'decoded'
	end 'enumCharacterRawValue'

	// Parse a payload field list `(name Type, name2 Type2)` for a `union`/`enum` case, into a
	// `PayloadField` array in slot order. Shared by the real parse and the tolerant sweep, so it
	// captures types WITHOUT rejecting any — see `readEnumCaseInto`'s payload arm
	// for why a rejection here would corrupt the sweep. `parseTypeReference` is the same type reader
	// a struct field and a parameter use, so a payload's type is spelled by one grammar.
	function readPayloadFieldsInto() returns PayloadFieldArray throws SourceParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let fields = PayloadFieldArray.create(0)

		while true 'fieldLoop'
			let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)

			// A `__`-prefixed field is reserved, at the name, exactly as a struct field's is
			// (`readStructFieldInto`). The tolerant sweep swallows the throw and leaves the diagnostic
			// to the real parse.
			try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
			let capabilityTok = self.current()
			let writtenCapability = try self.readParamCapability()
			let typeTok = self.current()

			// ⭐ **THE PAYLOAD's PRE-ERASURE SPELLING** — asked at the cursor, which sits on the
			// type's first token, for the reason a struct field asks it there: `parseTypeReference` on the
			// next line folds `__ManagedMemory` into the very `genericInstance` a `ByteArray` payload
			// resolves to. Captured in this ONE shared reader, so the tolerant sweep's layout — the
			// whole-program one every read door consults — and the real parse's carry the identical word.
			//
			// ⚠ A tuple SLOT's element bit is held clear (`slotElementsFromAliases: false`) for
			// `readStructFieldInto`'s reason: the SWEEP fills this column while the alias table is still
			// being built, so a slot's element bit read there answers *how far the sweep had got*. The
			// ROOT keeps asking, because `ProgramSignatures.payloadSurfaceOf` ORs in the complementary
			// derivation; see `declaredSurfaceAt`.
			let declaredSurface = self.declaredSurfaceAt(self.pos, slotElementsFromAliases: false)
			let declaredBrand = self.typeSpellingAt(self.pos)
			let fieldType = try self.parseTypeReference(BarePrimitiveVoice.asAType)
			try self.requireCapabilityFits(writtenCapability, wordTok: capabilityTok, spelled: fieldType)
			try self.requireStorableDeclaredType(fieldType, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.unionPayload, line: typeTok.line, column: typeTok.column)
			fields.push(PayloadField.create(nameTok.value, declaredType: fieldType, declaredSurface: declaredSurface, declaredBrand: declaredBrand, capability: self.aliasCapabilityOr(writtenCapability, typeTok: typeTok), typeSite: writtenTypeSiteOf(typeTok)))

			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endFields'
				break
			end 'endFields'

			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'fieldLoop'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		return fields
	end 'readPayloadFieldsInto'

	// Read the token that NAMES a case — an identifier, a keyword used as one, or the string/char
	// LITERAL of an implicitly-backed case (`"North"`, `'N'`), which names the case through the value it
	// carries. The literal is returned as it stands and the name derived from it by `enumCaseNameOf`,
	// because the caller needs BOTH halves of it: the name it declares and the raw value it fixes.
	function readEnumCaseNameToken() returns Token throws SourceParseError
		if tokenKindIsEnumLiteralBacking(self.current().kind) 'implicitlyBacked'
			let tok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			return tok
		end 'implicitlyBacked'

		return try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.enumCase)
	end 'readEnumCaseNameToken'

	// ⭐ **THE NAME A CASE'S HEAD TOKEN DECLARES.** An identifier (or a keyword used as one) declares
	// itself; a string/char literal in that position declares the case named by its DECODED CONTENT, so
	// `"North"` is the case `StringBacked.North` and `'N'` is `CharBacked.N`. The decode is the same one
	// the value paths use, so an escape means here exactly what it means in an expression.
	function enumCaseNameOf(nameTok Token) returns immutable ByteArray throws SourceParseError
		if nameTok.kind == TokenKind.stringLiteral 'stringBacked'
			return try self.decodedStringLiteralBytes(nameTok)
		end 'stringBacked'

		if nameTok.kind == TokenKind.charLiteral 'charBacked'
			return try self.charLiteralBytes(nameTok)
		end 'charBacked'

		return nameTok.value
	end 'enumCaseNameOf'

	// Parse an INTEGER raw value (`= 200`, `= -1`) — the same literal reading `parseRangeBound` does,
	// minus the type-qualified bounds a raw value cannot be. Every other backing is claimed before this is
	// reached — the string and char ones by `appendLiteralBackedEnumCase`, a struct by
	// `appendStructBackedEnumCase`, a function reference by `appendFunctionBackedEnumCase` — so what arrives
	// here is a token no raw value may open with at all.
	function readEnumIntRawValue() returns FullRangeInt throws SourceParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.minus) 'negative'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.minus)
			let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.intLiteral)
			return try self.intLiteralValue(tok, negated: true)
		end 'negative'

		if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'literal'
			let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.intLiteral)
			return try self.intLiteralValue(tok, negated: false)
		end 'literal'

		let tok = self.current()
		throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a raw value of kind '{tok.kind.rawValue}' (a raw value is an integer, a float, a string, a character, a function reference, or a constant struct)", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'readEnumIntRawValue'

	// ⭐ **FIX THE LAYOUT'S BACKING TO `kind`, OR RAISE E3032 IF A DIFFERENT ONE WAS ALREADY FIXED.**
	// The backing is settled by the FIRST case and every later one must agree with it, so the rule is
	// one comparison and the message is `expected <established>, got <incoming>`, rendered from
	// `enumBackingNoun` so the four backings cannot be worded differently at different doors.
	// `caseTok` positions the diagnostic at the offending case, not at its raw value.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A STRUCT BACKING IS FIXED BY *TWO* FACTS, AND BOTH ARE SETTLED HERE.** `= Meta{…}` fixes the kind
	// AND the struct type; a later `= Other{…}` agrees on the kind and disagrees on the type, which is the
	// same E3032 in the same words (*"expected Meta, got Other"*), from this one comparison.
	// `structName` is `EmptySourceText` for every other backing, which is exactly what the layout already
	// holds, so the assignment below writes both fields in one act for every kind of case.
	function requireEnumBacking(layout EnumLayout, kind EnumBacking, backingStruct EnumBackingStruct, caseTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		let kindDisagrees = layout.backing != EnumBacking.unresolved and layout.backing != kind
		let structDisagrees = layout.backing == kind and not layout.backingStruct.sameAs(backingStruct)

		if kindDisagrees or structDisagrees 'mismatch'
			throw SourceParseError.enumRawValueTypeMismatch("expected {enumBackingNoun(layout.backing, backingStruct: layout.backingStruct)}, got {enumBackingNoun(kind, backingStruct: backingStruct)}", line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'mismatch'

		layout.backing = kind
		layout.backingStruct = backingStruct
	end 'requireEnumBacking'

	// Append a case after checking E3031 (duplicate raw value) against the cases `ledger` has claimed so
	// far, positioned at the offending case. `rawValue` is how the value is written (the int, the float's
	// source text, the string's or character's decoded content), which is what the message prints and what
	// the ledger compares for a string backing (see `EnumCaseLedger.rawValueIsClaimed`, which owns the
	// compare).
	// ⭐ `declaresRawValue` is spelled by every caller rather than derived here, and `appendAutoTaggedEnumCase`
	// is the ONE that says `false` — it is the only reader that consumes no literal. Deriving it from
	// `rawValue` is not open: an auto-tagged case records `"{tag}"`, which is byte-identical to what
	// `red = 0` records. See `EnumLayout.declaresRawValues` for what the bit decides.
	function appendEnumCase(layout EnumLayout, caseName ByteArray, tag FullRangeInt, rawValue String, declaresRawValue bool, caseTok Token, payloads PayloadFieldArray, backingFields EnumBackingFieldArray, ledger EnumCaseLedger) throws SourceParseError
		try requireMemberCount((layout.caseCount()) + 1, limit: MaxDeclaredMembers, members: "cases", anchor: caseTok)
		try requireMemberCount(payloads.count(), limit: MaxDeclaredMembers, members: "payload fields", anchor: caseTok)

		if ledger.rawValueIsClaimed(layout, tag: tag, rawValue: rawValue) 'duplicateRawValue'
			throw SourceParseError.enumDuplicateRawValue(rawValue, line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'duplicateRawValue'

		layout.addCase(caseName, tag: tag, rawValue: rawValue, declaresRawValue: declaresRawValue, payloads: payloads, backingFields: backingFields)
		ledger.claim(layout, name: caseName, tag: tag, rawValue: rawValue)
	end 'appendEnumCase'

	// One member of a type body that is not a field: `[export] [static] function …`.
	//
	// ⭐ **`static` IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE, AND IT IS ONE BOOLEAN.** A `static function create()`
	// is a free function with a qualified name; a `function area()` is the same thing plus a
	// RECEIVER — `__self` at parameter 0, and every field of the enclosing type bound by name so a
	// bare `width` inside it means `self.width`. Both are built by the same `parseFunction`,
	// because a method IS a function: there is no method table and nothing for a call to dispatch
	// through. `enclosingType` supplies the qualification and is already set.
	//
	// The receiver is passed down as a flag and NOT recorded as one: `parseFunction` turns it into
	// a parameter named `SelfParamName` at position 0, and every later reader asks
	// `FuncSignature.takesReceiver`, which reads that name back. See there — one fact, one home.
	//
	// ⭐ **`static` ALSO PREFIXES A `let`/`var`, WHICH DECLARES MODULE STORAGE RATHER THAN A MEMBER** —
	// see the `staticBinding` arm below. `memberList` is what tells the two bodies that reach this reader
	// apart: a `type` body may declare it, an `extension` body may not.
	function parseTypeMember(memberList MemberList) throws SourceParseError
		let visibility = try self.consumeOptionalVisibility()

		let isStatic = self.at(TokenKind.static)

		if isStatic 'staticMember'
			if self.enclosingTypeIsEnum and self.kindAt(self.pos + 1) == TokenKind.function 'aStaticFunctionOnAnEnum'
				throw self.staticFunctionOnEnumRefusal(self.enumBeingExtended(), tok: self.current())
			end 'aStaticFunctionOnAnEnum'

			self.advance()
		end 'staticMember'

		// ⭐⭐ **`static let MAX = 100` — A TYPE-QUALIFIED TOP-LEVEL BINDING.**
		// It is not a field and takes no slot in the instance layout (`atStructFieldKeyword` never sees it —
		// that predicate looks past a VISIBILITY modifier, not past `static`); it is one copy for the TYPE,
		// which is exactly what a top-level binding already is. So it is READ by the top-level binding reader
		// and REPORTED by the top-level binding's own failure door, under the name `Counter.count` — the whole
		// mechanism, and the reason nothing downstream of the parser learned that static members exist.
		//
		// ⚠ The cursor sits on `let` here — `static` is consumed above — which is precisely where
		// `readTopLevelBinding` expects to begin, so the sweep's arm and this one enter it identically.
		if self.atTopLevelBindingKeyword() 'staticBinding'
			try self.requireMemberListDeclaresStorage(memberList, isStatic: true)
			try self.raiseBindingFailure(try self.readTopLevelBinding(visibility, qualifier: self.enclosingType.clone()))
			return
		end 'staticBinding'

		if self.at(TokenKind.var) and not isStatic 'anExtensionField'
			try self.requireMemberListDeclaresStorage(memberList, isStatic: false)
		end 'anExtensionField'

		// A NESTED (inner) typealias — `export typealias Idx = int(0 to u64.max)`. The
		// `export` (visibility) is already consumed above; a nested typealias takes no `static`. Its
		// visibility is its OWN modifier, as any member's is: unmarked is directory-scoped, and a file outside
		// the subtree naming an unexported inner alias is E3008 (`requireInnerAliasNameable`). An interface's inner alias
		// takes the interface's visibility instead. An extension of an enum or union declares none:
		// the member reaches the `function` check below and is refused there (E2010).
		if self.at(TokenKind.typealias) and not self.enclosingTypeIsEnum 'nestedTypeAlias'
			if isStatic 'staticInnerAlias'
				let tok = self.current()
				throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a nested typealias cannot be `static` — it is a per-type declaration, not a member function", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			end 'staticInnerAlias'

			// The three PER-INSTANCE forms are keyed whole-program by the sweep (`innerAliases`/`genericAliases`
			// /`tupleAliases`) and the real parse needs only the local `enclosingInnerAliases` population
			// `readNestedTypeAlias` performs — so their shapes are discarded here, and the TUPLE form's owner is
			// `unpublished` for the same reason (the sweep already keyed it, from the same tokens). A FUNCTION
			// alias's declared SIGNATURE lives only in the real parse's artifact (`readFunctionTypeAlias`'s
			// `recordSignature` note), so it is filed here in the array a top-level `typealias Fn = function(…)`
			// files into — under BOTH of the names it declares, exactly as the sweep files it
			// (`publishNestedFunctionAlias`). This is the copy every consumer of a PARAMETER type reads, so a
			// qualified key filed by the sweep alone would carry the return type and an empty parameter list.
			let aliasNameTok = self.tokenAt(self.pos + 1)
			var yieldsToTheTypesOwn = false

			match memberList 'whoseMember'
				typeBody then try self.requireNestedAliasDeclaredOnce(aliasNameTok)
				extensionBody then yieldsToTheTypesOwn = try self.extensionAliasYieldsToTheTypesOwn(aliasNameTok)
			end 'whoseMember'

			let nested = try self.readNestedTypeAlias(visibility, recordFunctionSignature: true, tupleOwner: TupleAliasOwner.unpublished)

			if yieldsToTheTypesOwn 'aDefaultTheConformerOverrides'
				return
			end 'aDefaultTheConformerOverrides'

			match nested 'nested'
				ranged or
				genericInstance or
				tupleAlias then return
				functionAlias(alias) then self.publishNestedFunctionAlias(alias, keys: self.nestedAliasKeys(self.extensionInterfaceName), into: self.artifact.functionTypeAliases)
			end 'nested'

			return
		end 'nestedTypeAlias'

		if not self.at(TokenKind.function) 'notFunction'
			let tok = self.current()
			throw SourceParseError.unexpectedToken(TokenKind.function.rawValue, got: tokenDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'notFunction'

		try self.parseFunction(visibility, takesReceiver: not isStatic, form: DeclaredHeadForm.declaredFunction)
	end 'parseTypeMember'

	// ⭐ **ONLY A `type` BODY MAY DECLARE STORAGE.** An `extension` adds BEHAVIOUR to types other
	// declarations named — its members are re-parsed once per conformer — so a binding written there has no
	// declaration to belong to and no single file that owns its slot. This is the refusal the member-list
	// header promises: a `type` body has a `let`/`var` arm, so an extension body reaching this reader
	// has to be refused by a rule rather than by the absence of an arm.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT MUST STAY A REFUSAL RATHER THAN BECOMING A FEATURE HERE, because the SWEEP cannot record
	// it.** `recordScannedExtension` consumes an extension declaration whole and cannot know its conformer
	// list (the conformance index is what that sweep is building), so a binding admitted here would be one
	// the sweep never filed — and `declaredOutcome`'s drift guard PANICS on exactly that, on a program with
	// no error in it. Neither reference declares storage in an extension either.
	//
	// ⚠ **A BARE `let`/`var` (no `static`) REACHES THIS TOO, AND ONLY FROM AN EXTENSION.** In a `type` body
	// `atStructFieldKeyword` claims it as a FIELD one door up, so a member list reaching the `staticBinding`
	// arm at all is either an extension's or carries the `static` the arm is named for. The message names
	// the keyword the author wrote, so both spellings read as the mistake they are.
	function requireMemberListDeclaresStorage(memberList MemberList, isStatic bool) throws SourceParseError
		if memberList == MemberList.typeBody 'aTypeBody'
			return
		end 'aTypeBody'

		let tok = self.current()
		let cure = "declare it in the `type` body as a `static {tok.kind.rawValue}`" if isStatic else "declare it as a field of the `type` body"
		throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a `{tok.kind.rawValue}` declaration in an `extension` body — an extension adds methods to types other declarations named and declares no storage of its own, so there is no type for this binding to belong to; {cure}", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'requireMemberListDeclaresStorage'

	// Parse a function: `[static] function <name>(<params>) [returns <Type>] <body> end
	// [label]`. Parameters are `name type`, comma-separated. There is no `main` qualification, so the declared
	// name is used verbatim for registry keys — `main` stays `main`. The ONE qualification there is:
	// a function declared inside a `type` body is `Point.create` (`declaredMethodName`).
	//
	// `takesReceiver` is set by the two doors that read a MEMBER declaration — `parseTypeMember` for a
	// member with no `static`, and `parseEnumMethod` unconditionally (an `enum`/`union` refuses a
	// `static`, so its every method is an instance one). It adds ONE thing — a leading parameter — and
	// everything else about an instance method follows from that, through the ordinary function machinery:
	// the call is an ordinary direct call, the receiver is an ordinary argument, `slotCallArgs` slots it at
	// position 0 because it is positional, and the register ABI hands it to the callee in argument
	// register 0.
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT READS A `test` DECLARATION TOO (see `DeclaredHeadForm`).** The two forms share everything
	// from the per-function reset block down — value numbering, the parameter binding, the body parse, the
	// close, the four registry commits — and differ in exactly two places, both marked below: the HEAD they
	// consume, and whether the closing `end` label is CHECKED or merely skipped. A second routine for `test`
	// would be a second copy of ~120 lines of per-function column resets, which is the shape
	// `parseClosureExpression`'s own header already names as this file's standing hazard ("this list is
	// written three times ... every miss is silent at the compiler's own build").
	function parseGenericTemplate(visibility Visibility, takesReceiver bool, form DeclaredHeadForm) throws SourceParseError
		let functionIndex = self.pos
		let nameIndex = functionIndex + 1
		let template = try self.readGenericTemplateAt(nameIndex)
		try self.requireGenericNameDeclaredOnce(template, nameTok: self.tokenAt(nameIndex))
		try self.requireEveryTypeParamInferable(template, nameIndex: nameIndex)

		if takesReceiver and not self.enclosingTypeParams.isEmpty() 'aMethodOfAGenericType'
			let nameTok = self.tokenAt(nameIndex)
			throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a method of the generic type '{String.from(self.enclosingTypeName())}' declaring type parameters of its own — declare it as a `static function` or on a type with no `uses` list", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'aMethodOfAGenericType'

		let instanceCount = self.signatures.genericInstanceCountAt(template.siteKey())
		let enclosingRegion = self.enterRegion(AbandonableRegion.none)

		for position in 0 upto instanceCount 'eachInstance'
			self.openGenericInstance(template, position: position)
			self.pos = functionIndex
			try self.parseFunction(visibility, takesReceiver: takesReceiver, form: form)
			self.closeGenericInstance()
		end 'eachInstance'

		self.openRegion = enclosingRegion
		self.pos = functionIndex
		self.skipGenericTemplateDeclaration()

		if instanceCount == 0 'neverInstantiated'
			self.artifact.unbuiltBodies.push(UnbuiltBody.create(functionIndex, limit: self.pos))
		end 'neverInstantiated'
	end 'parseGenericTemplate'

	function requireEveryTypeParamInferable(template GenericFunctionTemplate, nameIndex ElementIndex) throws SourceParseError
		for position in 0 upto template.typeParamNames.count() 'eachTypeParam'
			var inferred = false

			for i in 0 upto template.paramSlots.count() 'eachParam'
				let slot = try template.paramSlots.get(i) otherwise panic("requireEveryTypeParamInferable: paramSlots.get OOB at {i}")
				let bound = try template.paramTypeParams.get(i) otherwise panic("requireEveryTypeParamInferable: paramTypeParams.get OOB at {i}")

				if slot == GenericParamSlot.typeParameter and bound == position 'declaredHere'
					inferred = true
				end 'declaredHere'
			end 'eachParam'

			if not inferred 'uninferable'
				let name = try template.typeParamNames.get(position) otherwise panic("requireEveryTypeParamInferable: typeParamNames.get OOB at {position}")
				let anchor = self.tokenAt(self.usesClauseNameIndex(nameIndex, name: name))
				throw SourceParseError.genericTypeParameterNotInferable(String.from(name), functionName: (bareMethodName(template.key)), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			end 'uninferable'
		end 'eachTypeParam'
	end 'requireEveryTypeParamInferable'

	function usesClauseNameIndex(nameIndex ElementIndex, name ByteArray) returns ElementIndex
		var q = self.parenGroups.closeOf(self.tokens, open: nameIndex + 1) + 2

		while self.kindAt(q) == TokenKind.identifier or self.kindAt(q) == TokenKind.comma 'eachUsesToken'
			if self.kindAt(q) == TokenKind.identifier and self.tokenAt(q).value.equals(name) 'found'
				return q
			end 'found'

			q = q + 1
		end 'eachUsesToken'

		panic("usesClauseNameIndex: '{String.from(name)}' is a type parameter read off this head's `uses` list, so the list spells it")
	end 'usesClauseNameIndex'

	function skipGenericTemplateDeclaration()
		self.skipWhereClauseToLineEnd()
		self.advanceToOwnBlockEnd()
		self.advancePastBlockCloser()
	end 'skipGenericTemplateDeclaration'

	function parseFunction(visibility Visibility, takesReceiver bool, form DeclaredHeadForm) throws SourceParseError
		let confinement = try self.confine(RegionKind.body) otherwise 'unconfined'
			try self.readFunction(visibility, takesReceiver: takesReceiver, form: form)
			return
		end 'unconfined'

		try self.readFunction(visibility, takesReceiver: takesReceiver, form: form) otherwise (refusal) 'refused'
			try self.recoverRegion(refusal, confinement: confinement)

			if self.readsALaterConformer 'earlierConformersReadThisBody'
				self.extensionReplayOwed = true
			end 'earlierConformersReadThisBody'

			try self.readFunction(visibility, takesReceiver: takesReceiver, form: form)
		end 'refused'
	end 'parseFunction'

	function readFunction(visibility Visibility, takesReceiver bool, form DeclaredHeadForm) throws SourceParseError
		let isTest = form == DeclaredHeadForm.testDeclaration

		if not isTest and not self.genericInstanceName.isInstance() and functionDeclaresTypeParamsIn(self.tokens, groups: self.parenGroups, nameIndex: self.pos + 1) 'aGenericTemplate'
			try self.parseGenericTemplate(visibility, takesReceiver: takesReceiver, form: form)
			return
		end 'aGenericTemplate'

		// Parameters are parsed into parallel name/type lists BEFORE the scope reset:
		// binding them needs the fresh per-function scope + value numbering, which is
		// re-seeded at the resulting paramCount just below. Parsing itself only
		// consumes tokens, so it needs no scope.
		let paramNames = NameRoster.create(0)
		var paramTypes = MaxonTypeArray.create(0)

		// Parallel to `paramNames`: each parameter's name-token span, so `bindParameters` can
		// record a ValueOrigin at the DECLARATION for parameter `i` — the source site the
		// E5001 diagnostic points at when a parameter is in a register-pressure blocking set.
		let paramRanges = SourceRangeArray.create(0)

		// Parallel to `paramNames` too: whether the parameter's DECLARED TYPE spelled `__ManagedMemory`
		// — the fact `parseTypeReference` is about to erase, read by `bindParameters` below.
		let paramSurfaces = DeclaredSurfaceArray.create(0)

		// And parallel again: the INDEX of each parameter's declared-type first token — the position the
		// `with` arguments of a parameterized-interface type are still readable at, erased by the same call and
		// read by the same `bindParameters`.
		let paramTypeStarts = TokenIndexArray.create(0)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE HEAD FORK, AND THE THREE FACTS IT PRODUCES.** Whatever a head reads, the body below needs
		// exactly this much from it: the TOKEN every position in this declaration is anchored at, the name the
		// registries are keyed by, and the key the sweep filed this declaration's defaults under. A `test`
		// declares no parameters, so its head leaves the five columns above empty and its whole grammar is
		// `test '<prose>'` followed by a line break.
		let head = try self.readFunctionHead(isTest, takesReceiver: takesReceiver, paramNames: paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges, paramSurfaces: paramSurfaces, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts)
		let nameTok = head.nameTok
		let sourceName = head.sourceName
		let ownTypeParams = head.ownTypeParams

		// ⭐ **A STRUCT PARAMETER OR RETURN TYPE IS RE-TAGGED `structRef` HERE**, in the real parse where
		// the whole-program struct registry is complete — the step `parseTypeReference` could not take in
		// the tolerant sweep (see `resolveNamedStruct`). Both lists are rewritten BEFORE anything reads
		// them: the value-tag seeding below, `bindParameters`, `func.maxonParamTypes`, the recorded
		// signature, and `parseReturnStatement`'s declared-type check all then see a struct as a struct.
		paramTypes = self.resolveNamedStructAll(paramTypes)

		// Captured BEFORE the clause is consumed, for `paramTypeStarts`' reason one column over: the
		// resolved type is exactly where the `with` arguments stop existing, so the token is what survives.
		let returnTypeStart = self.returnTypeTokenIndex()
		let returnSpelling = self.typeSpellingAt(returnTypeStart) if self.at(TokenKind.returns) else EmptySourceText
		let qualifiedReturn = try self.parseOptionalReturnType()
		let returnType = self.resolveNamedStructReturn(qualifiedReturn.returnType)
		let returnBrand = self.returnBrandOf(returnType, returnTypeStart: returnTypeStart)

		// The `throws E` clause, stamped onto the function so the body parse and the lowering can read it
		// off `func`.
		//
		// A `test` declares `throws TestFailure` and nobody writes it: the header is `test '<prose>'`,
		// and the language forbids writing the clause (`docs/LANGUAGE_REFERENCE.md`, "Test
		// Declarations"). The substituted clause is what lets an assertion's `TestFailure` leave the
		// test, whether the body's implied handler takes the throwing call or an explicit bare `try`
		// propagates it.
		//
		// It cannot be a refusal inside `parseOptionalThrowsClause`: that reader is shared with the
		// tolerant declaration sweep, whose `otherwise return` would swallow the judgement, as
		// `requireDeclarationHeaderLineEnd` also argues. Nothing is left to refuse anyway: the line break
		// `readTestDeclarationHead` requires makes a `throws` keyword here E2001.
		//
		// Each arm states its own clause, so a test's clause does not read as the default.
		var throwsClause = ThrowsClause.none

		if isTest 'anImpliedThrowsClause'
			throwsClause = ThrowsClause.throwing(TestImpliedThrowsTypeName)
		end 'anImpliedThrowsClause' else 'aWrittenThrowsClause'
			throwsClause = try self.parseOptionalThrowsClause()
		end 'aWrittenThrowsClause'

		if self.at(TokenKind.where) and self.genericInstanceName.isInstance() 'anInstanceWhereClause'
			self.skipWhereClauseToLineEnd()
		end 'anInstanceWhereClause'

		try self.requireDeclarationHeaderLineEnd()

		let signatureTypeNames = self.writtenSignatureTypeNames(visibility != Visibility.unexported, paramNames: paramNames, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts, returnTypeStart: returnTypeStart, hasReturnType: not returnSpelling.isEmpty(), ownTypeParams: ownTypeParams)

		// After the whole head, so a malformed head is reported by the parse's own positioned error
		// rather than as a compiler bug.
		try self.requireSweptDeclaration(sourceName, nameTok: nameTok)
		try self.requireNoEarlierGenericTemplate(sourceName, nameTok: nameTok)

		// ⭐ **THE NAME EVERY REGISTRY BELOW IS KEYED BY** is the source name: a function is declared once per
		// directory and a method once per type, so no declaration is registered under a variant of it. Two
		// tests of one name are a duplicate, which `requireUnmintedTestName` has already refused above in
		// terms that name both prose spellings.
		let name = head.sourceName

		// Fresh per-function value numbering, seeded at the function's paramCount so
		// parameter ids occupy 0..paramCount-1 by Std-ABI convention (a body value
		// then starts at paramCount — see IrValueId's ValueIdRef contract). The value-TYPE
		// column is seeded in lockstep, with the parameters' declared types, so
		// `valueTypes[i]` is parameter `i`'s type from the very first lookup.
		let userParamCount = paramNames.count()

		let mayCarryALayoutDescriptor = self.signatures.calleeMayCarryALayoutDescriptor(name)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE FIXED-ELEMENT-COUNT SLOT** — reserved off the whole-program count fixpoint under the
		// qualified name, and INDEPENDENTLY of the descriptor: a method that reads only `countof(Self)` needs
		// no dictionary word at all, so making the count imply a descriptor would demand one from every frame
		// that calls it — including the frames that have none.
		var needsCountParam = false

		if self.signatures.anyFunctionReadsFixedElementCount() and self.enclosingType.isType() 'aMethodOfSomeTypeReadsACount'
			needsCountParam = self.signatures.functionReadsFixedElementCount(qualifiedMethodName(self.enclosingTypeName(), methodName: nameTok.value))
		end 'aMethodOfSomeTypeReadsACount'

		// ⭐⭐ **AN INTERFACE RETURN TYPE IS THE SECOND WRITER OF THE ONE SECONDARY RETURN REGISTER
		// (the return ABI).** A non-throwing function hands the witness half back in exactly the
		// register a throwing one spends on the error flag — same register convention, same op shape
		// (`StdOp.errorReturn` / `StdOp.tryCall`), which is `functionUsesSecondaryReturnSlot`'s whole
		// content. There is ONE such register, so a function that is BOTH is refused here: no third slot
		// exists, The alternative — silently skipping the return-witness path and handing back a bare
		// pointer — is not one.
		if throwsClauseIsThrowing(throwsClause) 'theErrorFlagAlreadyOwnsTheSecondRegister'
			match returnType 'declaredReturn'
				void then break
				value(inner) then try self.requireStorableDeclaredType(inner, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.throwingReturnType, line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'declaredReturn'
		end 'theErrorFlagAlreadyOwnsTheSecondRegister'

		// ⭐ **THE ONE DECLARATION SHAPE `self.<field> = …` MEANS ANYTHING IN.** A `static function` of a
		// `type` returning that type is the factory `/specs/field-initialization.md`'s third rule is about:
		// there is no receiver, so the assignment cannot be a store — it names the value the `Self{…}` below
		// it will BE. An `enum`/`union` has no fields to defer, and everywhere else `self` outside an
		// instance method stays E2001.
		let nascentSelfFactory = not takesReceiver and self.enclosingType.isType() and not self.enclosingTypeIsEnum and self.returnsEnclosingType(returnType)

		let countParamCount = 1 if needsCountParam else 0

		// ⭐ **A HIDDEN WITNESS PARAMETER PER `where` CONSTRAINT (the dictionary's dispatch
		// half).** An INSTANCE method of a constrained generic type carries one witness pointer per constraint,
		// just before the layout slot:
		// `[user][existentialWitness][count][witness_0..k][layout]`, index-aligned to
		// `enclosingWhereConstraints`. The reservation is UNCONDITIONAL on instance methods (not gated on
		// whether this method actually dispatches), so the type's instance-method ABI is uniform and
		// every caller passes the witnesses; `witnessReserveFor` says which statics carry them.
		let witnessReserve = self.witnessReserveFor(takesReceiver, registrationName: name)
		let witnessParamCount = witnessReserve.unconditional
		let tailWitnessParamCount = witnessReserve.conditional
		let layoutReserve = layoutReserveFor(mayCarryALayoutDescriptor, conditionalWitnesses: tailWitnessParamCount)

		// ⭐ **AND ONE HIDDEN WITNESS PER INTERFACE-TYPED USER PARAMETER NOT TAKEN BY REFERENCE** — the
		// other half of the fat pointer, travelling as an adjacent ABI parameter rather than as an
		// aggregate. It sits between the user parameters and the layout slot; see
		// `bindExistentialWitnessParams`. It is sized through `existentialWitnessParamCount`, the counter
		// the lowering's SUPPLY side reads: a second spelling here is a callee reading a witness slot the
		// caller never filled.
		let byRef = self.declarationByRefConvention(sourceName)
		let byReference = byReferenceMaskOf(byRef, paramCount: userParamCount as ParamCount)
		let existentialWitnessCount = existentialWitnessParamCount(paramTypes, byReference: byReference)
		let paramCount = userParamCount as ParamCount + existentialWitnessCount + countParamCount as ParamCount + witnessParamCount
		try requireAbiParamCount(paramCount, anchor: nameTok)

		// The global-alias answer is read once here rather than at each `return`: it cannot change while
		// one body is parsed, and its door sits on the promotion path of every returned aggregate. The
		// return and parameter aliases are read here because `resolveTypes` erases both.
		self.beginDeclaredBody((paramCount + layoutReserve) as ValueId, returnSpelling: returnSpelling, returnBrand: returnBrand, returnAlias: self.rangedReturnAlias(returnType), paramSites: try self.rangedParamSites(paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges) otherwise RangeCheckParamSiteArray.create(0), layoutParam: layoutParamOf(mayCarryALayoutDescriptor, paramCount: paramCount + layoutReserve), countParam: fixedElementCountParamOf(needsCountParam, paramCount: paramCount, witnessParamCount: witnessParamCount), nascentSelfFactory: nascentSelfFactory, kind: EnclosingBodyKind.testBody if isTest else EnclosingBodyKind.declaredFunction, witnessParamBase: witnessBlockStartValueId(paramCount + tailWitnessParamCount, witnessParamCount: witnessParamCount + tailWitnessParamCount), witnessParamCount: witnessParamCount + tailWitnessParamCount)

		self.bodyState.returnsAReceiverField = takesReceiver and self.signatures.returnsAReceiverField(name)

		for paramType in paramTypes 'seedParamTypes'
			// ⭐ A FLOAT-ALIAS parameter is a genuine FLOAT value, so its value TAG must be `float`, not the
			// `named` a ranged alias otherwise carries — exactly as the parser resolves a float-returning
			// callee's result type at `mintOwnedCallResult`. Left `named`, arithmetic on the parameter reads
			// it as integral (`operandsAreFloat` is true and `promoteToFloat` is NOT idempotent on it),
			// emitting a spurious `siToFp` on an already-float value that the backend rejects as a
			// cross-file convert. An INT alias stays `named` (its name carries the signedness
			// `valueIsUnsigned` reads); only a proven float alias is lifted (see `resolveFloatAliasType`).
			// `paramTypes` itself is UNCHANGED — the recorded signature, `func.maxonParamTypes` and the
			// parameter bindings keep the declared alias — so this touches ONLY the value columns: the tag
			// the operators dispatch on, and the alias name the float value wears so that a float alias
			// stays nominal (`wearDeclaredFloatAlias`).
			self.recordParamValueType(paramType)
		end 'seedParamTypes'

		// `valueTags` and `valueNameIds` are handed over by reference, already seeded with the parameter
		// types, so every `mintValue` below pushes straight into the function's own type columns. It is
		// safe because each body state creates its own columns, so nothing aliases these after this body.
		let func = IrFunction.create(name, maxonReturnType: returnType, paramCount: paramCount, maxonParamTypes: paramTypes, typeSites: self.declaredTypeSites(paramTypeStarts, returnTypeStart: returnTypeStart), valueTags: self.bodyState.valueTags, valueNameIds: self.bodyState.valueNameIds, valueBrandIds: self.bodyState.valueBrandIds, valueCapabilities: self.bodyState.valueCapabilities, valueMintedSole: self.anchors.functions.mintedSole, valueWitnessIds: self.bodyState.valueWitnessIds, declaredAt: SourcePosition.declaredAt(self.job.path, nameTok: nameTok), placeholders: self.anchors.functions, throwsTypeName: throwsClause)
		func.visibility = visibility
		func.reassignedParamMask = byReference
		self.bodyState.declaredReturnCapability = qualifiedReturn.capability

		self.seedParameterCapabilities(func, head: head, takesReceiver: takesReceiver)
		self.bodyState.consumesSelf = takesReceiver and head.selfCapability == ParamCapability.consuming

		if self.bodyState.consumesSelf 'aConsumingMethod'
			try self.requireTypeDeclaresAFinalizer(nameTok)
		end 'aConsumingMethod'

		// ⭐ **THE FUNCTION IS PUSHED UP FRONT, NOT AT THE END, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES ValueOrigin CORRECT
		// UNDER NESTED PARSING.** A non-capturing closure literal lifts to a top-level function
		// that is parsed and pushed in the MIDDLE of this body (`parseClosureExpression`), so if this
		// function were pushed only at the END, the closure would take this function's index and every origin
		// keyed to `funcCount()` mid-body would name the wrong function. Pushing here reserves this
		// function's final index BEFORE any closure can push after it, and `currentFuncIndex` records it so
		// `recordValueOrigin`/`recordParamOrigin` (below, in `bindParameters` and every body emit) file
		// against the right function. For a body with no closures the module order is unchanged — a function
		// still completes before the next one starts — so this is index-for-index identical to
		// pushing at the end for every closure-free program.
		let funcIndex = self.artifact.module.funcCount()
		self.artifact.module.pushFunc(func)
		self.artifact.currentFuncIndex = funcIndex

		// Every hidden parameter's origin is filed after the stamp above: `recordParamOrigin` files
		// under `artifact.currentFuncIndex`, so filed above it a hidden slot lands under the PREVIOUS
		// function and resolves to no source span here, which `RegisterPressureDiagnostic` aborts on as a
		// compiler-introduced value blocking an allocation. The columns are seeded in ABI order,
		// `[user][existentialWitness][count][constraintWitness][layout]`.
		self.bindExistentialWitnessParams(paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges, userParamCount: userParamCount as ParamCount, byReference: byReference)

		self.seedHiddenWordParam(self.bodyState.fixedElementCountParam, anchor: nameTok)

		let witnessBase = witnessBlockStartValueId(paramCount, witnessParamCount: witnessParamCount)

		for w in 0 upto witnessParamCount 'seedWitnessParam'
			self.seedHiddenIntegerParam(witnessBase + (w as ValueId), anchor: nameTok)
		end 'seedWitnessParam'

		self.seedLayoutReserve(func, firstId: paramCount, reserve: layoutReserve, anchor: nameTok)

		// Create the entry block (registered in func.blockRefs). It also displaces the detached
		// placeholder `currentBlock` was created with — from here on, every op the parser emits goes to a
		// block that belongs to a real function.
		let entry = self.artifact.module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
		self.bodyState.currentBlock = entry

		// ⭐ **THE FIELD ALIASES ARE DECLARED BEFORE THE PARAMETERS, AND THE ORDER IS THE E3006
		// CONTRACT.** Both a parameter and a body local are bound through `declareValueBinding`,
		// which refuses to displace a self-field alias — so declaring the aliases into the
		// just-`reset` scope FIRST is what puts every colliding declaration on the guarded side of
		// the collision, with its own token in hand to anchor the diagnostic on. Reversed, the
		// parameter would be in place first and the ALIAS would be the one refused, leaving the
		// error to be reported against a field declaration that is not what needs to change.
		// ⚠ An `enum`/`union` receiver contributes NO aliases: an enum has no fields, and
		// `declareSelfFieldAliases` would ask `enclosingLayout` for a `StructLayout` no enum declares —
		// which REFUSES with a position.
		// So a bare name in an enum method's body is an ordinary local or an
		// undefined variable, never a field — which is also what makes the E3006 collision contract below
		// vacuous here.
		// ⚠ **A PRIMITIVE RECEIVER IS THE SECOND SUCH DECLARATION, AND IT IS THE SAME SENTENCE FOR A
		// DIFFERENT REASON.** `extension int`'s methods take a receiver and the enclosing type is `int`, which
		// no struct registry holds — a primitive IS its value, so there is nothing for a layout to describe.
		// MEASURED without this clause: every method of `extension int` took the compiler down at
		// `enclosingLayout`, blaming the declaration sweep for a `type int` no program wrote.
		// ⛔⛔ **AN INTERFACE EXTENSION'S BODY IS THE THIRD SUCH DECLARATION, AND IT IS THE ONE WHOSE ABSENCE
		// TOLD A USER TO RENAME A LOCAL THEY DID NOT WRITE.** `extension Iterable` is re-parsed once per
		// CONFORMER (`parseExtensionDeclaration`), with `enclosingType` set to the conformer — so the
		// conformer's FIELDS were installed as bare aliases into a body written in another file, against the
		// INTERFACE, by an author who cannot see them. A `type Bag uses Element implements Iterable` whose
		// field is spelled `item` therefore collided with `map`'s own `for item in self`, and the diagnostic
		// was **`error E3006: stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:201:7: local 'item' shadows self field 'Bag.item'`** — a
		// refusal quoting a library line, naming a library local, on a program whose author's only mistake was
		// choosing a field name. Every local in every `extension Iterable` method was a reserved word for
		// conformers.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE ALIASES ARE WITHHELD RATHER THAN THE COLLISION EXCUSED, AND THAT IS THE NARROWER FIX OF THE
		// TWO.** Permitting the shadow would let the local win and stop the refusal, but it leaves the other
		// half standing: a bare name in an extension body that does NOT collide with a local would still
		// resolve to the conformer's private field, so the body's meaning would depend on storage the interface
		// does not expose and a different conformer does not have. **An interface declares a contract, not
		// storage** — so there is nothing here for a bare name to denote, and `self.<field>` remains the
		// explicit spelling for a body that has some other reason to reach one. It is the enum clause's
		// sentence for a different reason: a bare name in such a body is an ordinary local or an undefined
		// variable, never a field.
		if takesReceiver and not self.enclosingTypeIsEnum and primitiveTagOfTypeName(self.enclosingTypeName()) == ValueTypeTag.unresolved and not self.extensionInterfaceName.isNamed() 'selfFieldAliases'
			try self.declareSelfFieldAliases(nameTok)
		end 'selfFieldAliases'

		// Bind each parameter name to its reserved ValueId (i for parameter i), so a
		// body reference resolves straight to the parameter's SSA value — the same
		// value-substitution model `let`/`var` bindings use. A parameter is a `let` unless it is marked
		// `mutable` (reassigning an unmarked one is E2013). Runs after the push +
		// `currentFuncIndex` stamp above so each parameter's `recordParamOrigin` is filed under this
		// function's real index.
		// `sourceName`, not `name`: the declaration sweep files the parameters' ownership facts under
		// the name the source wrote and this declaration's site (`consumeOfDeclaration`).
		// ⭐ Which of this body's local names need a cell, decided from tokens before the body is parsed
		// (see `cellLocalNamesIn` for why it is one walk per body and not one per declaration).
		try self.requireNoMarkedMessageOrFactoryParameter(func, markerTokens: head.markerTokens)

		self.bodyState.bodyStart = self.pos
		self.bodyState.outerBodyStart = self.pos
		self.seedCellLocalNames(paramNames, extent: BodyExtent.block)
		try self.bindParameters(func, consume: self.signatures.consumeOf(sourceName), paramNames: paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges, paramSurfaces: paramSurfaces, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts)

		self.skipNewlines()

		// ⭐⭐ **A STDLIB BODY NO PATH FROM `main` REACHES IS SKIPPED WHOLE — the ops, and with them every
		// diagnostic that is a property of the ops.** See `ParseJob.buildsBodies` for why the DECLARATION
		// above this line is never skipped.
		let regionKey = positionKey(nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		let abandoned = self.abandonedRegions.contains(regionKey)
		let enclosingRegion = self.openRegion.clone()

		if abandoned 'abandonedBody'
			func.bodyStatus = BodyStatus.abandoned
			self.skipAbandonedBody(entry, nameTok: nameTok)
		end 'abandonedBody' else if not self.job.buildsBodies 'bodyNotReached'
			func.bodyStatus = BodyStatus.skippedAsUnreached
			self.skipUnreachedFunctionBody(entry, nameTok: nameTok)
		end 'bodyNotReached' else 'bodyReached'
			_ = self.enterRegion(AbandonableRegion.body(regionKey))

			// parseStatements threads a "current block" that control-flow statements can
			// move forward (an `if` leaves subsequent statements in its continuation block),
			// so the block it RETURNS — not `entry` — is the one control falls out of.
			try self.declareNascentSelfSlots(entry, nameTok: nameTok)
			let bodyEnd = try self.parseStatements(func, block: entry)
			func.closeLine = self.current().line
			func.closeColumn = self.current().column
			try self.closeFunctionBody(func, bodyEnd: bodyEnd, nameTok: nameTok)
			try self.requireFrozenResultIsMixed()

			self.finishCapabilityFacts(func)

			try self.validateNascentSelfAssignments(func)
			try self.requireNoWitnessCursorElementIsDropped("'{String.from(nameTok.value)}'", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)

			// ⚠ The BARE name off the token, not `sourceName`: the interface-requirement waiver
			// matches against the requirement an `interface` body spells, which is the method name alone.
			// `sourceName` is already qualified for a method (`Silent.greet` — `declaredMethodName`) and would
			// match no contract, silently, so the waiver would simply never fire.
			// E3012 first, E3077 only when it found nothing — the precedence, stated as the order of the two
			// scans over one list. See `reportVarShouldBeLet`.
			if not self.reportUnusedBindings(nameTok.value) 'nothingUnused'
				self.reportVarShouldBeLet()
			end 'nothingUnused'
		end 'bodyReached'

		// ⭐ **THE SECOND OF THE TWO PLACES THE FORMS DIFFER: a `test` MUST write its `end` label.** A test's
		// label IS its name — the one the reporter prints — so an `end` without it names no test at all.
		if not abandoned 'aBodyWasRead'
			try self.consumeBlockEnd(nameTok.value, labelIsRequired: isTest)
		end 'aBodyWasRead'

		self.openRegion = enclosingRegion

		self.publishExistentialInstances(func)
		self.stampLetCastScopes(nameTok.line, lastLine: self.tokenAt(self.pos - 1).line)
		func.valueMintedSole = self.mintedSoleColumn()
		self.publishDebugLocals(func)
		let declaredSignature = FuncSignature.create(paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramAggregateNames: self.paramAggregateNamesOf(paramTypes), paramDeclaredNames: self.paramDeclaredNamesOf(paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts), returnAggregateName: self.erasedAggregateNameOfReturn(returnType), returnBrand: returnBrand, paramExistentialSites: self.paramExistentialSitesOf(paramTypes, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts), returnExistentialSites: self.returnExistentialSitesOf(returnType, returnTypeStart: returnTypeStart), extensionConstraints: self.enclosingExtensionConstraints, writtenTypeNames: signatureTypeNames, selfSpelledParams: self.selfSpelledParamsOf(paramNames, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts), offeredResultAlias: EmptySourceText, byRefParams: byReference, returnCapability: qualifiedReturn.capability, paramCapabilities: head.paramCapabilities, selfCapability: head.selfCapability, writtenParams: head.markedParams)
		self.artifact.recordFuncSignature(name, signature: declaredSignature, returnType: returnType, nameLine: nameTok.line, nameColumn: nameTok.column, sourceFilePath: self.job.path, synthesized: false)
		self.commitRangeChecks(name)
	end 'readFunction'

	function readFunctionHead(isTest bool, takesReceiver bool, paramNames NameRoster, paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray, paramSurfaces DeclaredSurfaceArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexArray) returns FunctionHead throws SourceParseError
		let headStart = self.pos

		if isTest 'aTestHead'
			let nameTok = try self.readTestDeclarationHead()

			// The MANGLED name (`__test_<sanitized>`), which is what every registry, the symbol table and
			// the `.mxdbg` sidecar carry. It goes through the ONE mint so the declaration SWEEP files this
			// declaration under the very bytes the real parse registers it under.
			let sourceName = testFunctionRegistrationName(nameTok.value)
			try self.requireUnmintedTestName(sourceName, nameTok: nameTok)

			// A test takes no parameters, so it can have no defaults and there is no contest to compose: its
			// own key is its own name.
			return FunctionHead.create(nameTok, sourceName: sourceName, parameterListStart: headStart, ownTypeParams: self.emptyTypeParams, paramCapabilities: ParamCapabilityArray.create(0), selfCapability: ParamCapability.unmarked, markedParams: NoParamPositions, markerTokens: TokenIndexArray.create(0))
		end 'aTestHead'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.function)

		// ⭐ A KEYWORD MAY BE THE DECLARED NAME — `export static function from (path String)` is
		// `stdlib/FilePath.maxon`'s `FilePath.from`. Nothing but a name may stand here, so a keyword written here is one;
		// see `consumeNameToken`. The token scans that re-derive block structure learn the same fact through
		// `keywordIsAName`'s declaration-name shape, or a `function while(…)` would read as a block nothing
		// closes.
		let nameTok = try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.declaration)

		// The BARE name only — a method's `Type.` qualification is applied below by
		// `declaredMethodName`, so `Point.create` is checked as `create` and the reserved test never
		// sees the type prefix (`Self` is fine; a `__method` name is not).
		try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
		try self.requireNoRegistryMemberOnAKey(nameTok, takesReceiver: takesReceiver)

		// The fault probe's name, which is reserved HERE and at no other binding site: this is the only door
		// a declaration reaches the free-function name space through.
		try self.requireFreeFunctionNameIsNotTheFaultProbe(nameTok)

		// `Point.create` inside `type Point`, the bare token at file scope. The SAME derivation the
		// declaration sweep records the signature under, so the key an `IrFunction` carries and the
		// key `mintCallResult` types a call from are one name.
		let sourceName = try self.genericInstanceName.instanceName() otherwise self.declaredMethodName(nameTok)

		// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER IS AN ORDINARY PARAMETER, PUSHED FIRST.** That is the entire mechanism:
		// it occupies position 0, so it is ValueId 0 by the same Std-ABI seeding every parameter
		// gets, and no part of the call path, the lowering or the backend learns that methods
		// exist. It is pushed BEFORE `parseFunctionParameters` so the user's own parameters land
		// after it — which also means the receiver is inside the ABI count `requireAbiParamCount`
		// judges, correctly: it occupies an argument slot exactly like a declared parameter.
		//
		// It is typed by `enclosingSelfType` — the same `Self` means here, through the same routine that
		// resolves the `Self` keyword, so a receiver and a `returns Self` cannot come to disagree about
		// which type encloses them, NOR about whether that type is a struct or an enum: a struct's
		// receiver is a `structRef` and an enum's is a `named`, which is what routes each to its own
		// dispatch at the call site.
		//
		// Its span is the METHOD NAME, because no source token declares it. That is the choice
		// `closeFunctionBody` makes for its synthetic `retVoid` and for E3013, and for the same
		// reason: the name is where a reader looks when something about the function's own shape is
		// at fault.
		if takesReceiver 'receiverParam'
			paramNames.push(SelfParamName)
			paramTypes.push(try self.enclosingSelfType(nameTok))
			paramRanges.push(SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))

			// The receiver is typed by `enclosingSelfType`, never by a source spelling, so it can never be
			// the buffer alias — but it occupies position 0 and every column here is indexed by position.
			paramSurfaces.push(plainDeclaredSurface())

			// Nor is it declared at a type any token spells, and its slot must therefore hold an index that
			// names no type BY TOKEN KIND rather than by a claim about what some name means. The cursor has not
			// moved past the signature's `(` yet, so `self.pos` is that `(` — and `declaredExistentialInstanceAt`
			// declines anything but an identifier. (The method NAME would have been the natural choice, matching
			// this parameter's `paramRanges` span, and it is the wrong one: a method named the same as a generic
			// alias would resolve, and an `extension <interface>` receiver IS existential-tagged, so the slot is
			// not unreachable.)
			paramTypeStarts.push(self.pos)
		end 'receiverParam'

		let parameterListStart = self.pos
		let selfCapability = try self.consumeParameterListOpen(takesReceiver)
		let paramCapabilities = ParamCapabilityArray.create(0)
		let paramMarkers = BoolArray.create(0)
		let markerTokens = TokenIndexArray.create(0)
		try self.parseFunctionParameters(paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges, paramSurfaces: paramSurfaces, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts, paramCapabilities: paramCapabilities, paramMarkers: paramMarkers, markerTokens: markerTokens, owner: ParamDefaultOwner.declaredFunction(sourceName, declaredAt: nameTok), paramTypeOffer: noClosureParamOffer)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		var ownTypeParams = self.emptyTypeParams

		if self.at(TokenKind.uses) 'aTypeParameterList'
			ownTypeParams = try self.readFunctionTypeParams()
		end 'aTypeParameterList'

		return FunctionHead.create(nameTok, sourceName: sourceName, parameterListStart: parameterListStart, ownTypeParams: ownTypeParams, paramCapabilities: paramCapabilities, selfCapability: selfCapability, markedParams: markerMaskOf(paramMarkers, firstPosition: 1 if takesReceiver else 0), markerTokens: markerTokens)
	end 'readFunctionHead'

	function consumeParameterListOpen(takesReceiver bool) returns ParamCapability throws SourceParseError
		let receiver = self.receiverCapabilityAt(self.pos)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)

		if receiver == ParamCapability.unmarked 'noReceiverCapability'
			return ParamCapability.unmarked
		end 'noReceiverCapability'

		let wordTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)

		if not takesReceiver 'noReceiver'
			throw notATypeError(wordTok)
		end 'noReceiver'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.self)

		if self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreParameters'
			self.advance()
		end 'moreParameters'

		return receiver
	end 'consumeParameterListOpen'

	function receiverCapabilityAt(parenIndex ElementIndex) returns ParamCapability
		if self.kindAt(parenIndex) != TokenKind.leftParen or not self.qualifiers.qualifiesAt(parenIndex + 1) or self.kindAt(parenIndex + 2) != TokenKind.self 'noReceiverCapability'
			return ParamCapability.unmarked
		end 'noReceiverCapability'

		return try capabilityOfWord(self.tokenAt(parenIndex + 1).value) otherwise panic("receiverCapabilityAt: the classifier marks a word before `self` as a qualifier only when it spells a capability")
	end 'receiverCapabilityAt'

	// The parser's deferral doors read the sweep, so a declaration it lacks has every call to it
	// deferred, and no E3004 follows because the function exists — an `unresolved` value would survive
	// with no error.
	function requireSweptDeclaration(sourceName ByteArray, nameTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		if self.signatures.declaresCallee(sourceName) 'theSweepFiledIt'
			return
		end 'theSweepFiledIt'

		throw SourceParseError.declarationTheSweepMissed(String.from(sourceName), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
	end 'requireSweptDeclaration'

	// ⭐⭐ **READ A `test` DECLARATION'S HEAD — `test '<prose>'` and the line break behind it — and hand back
	// the NAME TOKEN, which is what every position in the declaration is then anchored at.**
	//
	// Three refusals live here and nowhere else, and each is a property of the HEAD rather than of the body:
	// the file it stands in (E2058), an empty name (E2059), and anything at all after the name (E2001).
	// The third is what refuses `test 'x'(v int)` and `test 'x' returns int` — the two shapes the language
	// forbids — and it is the ORDINARY block-label layout rule (`requireLineEndAfter`), not a pair
	// of rules of its own: a test's name IS a block label, closed by an `end` that repeats it, so "the label
	// ends its line" already says everything the two refusals need to say. That is also what keeps a
	// malformed test a PARSE error rather than a test that silently passes.
	function readTestDeclarationHead() returns Token throws SourceParseError
		// `testDeclarationAt` established both tokens at the dispatch, so neither read can fail here: the
		// keyword is an ordinary identifier (see `TestKeywordName`) and the prose name is the `charLiteral`
		// behind it.
		let testTok = self.current()
		self.advance()
		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)

		// Anchored at the KEYWORD, not the name: what is wrong is that the declaration stands here at all.
		let fileName = self.job.path.filename()

		if not isTestSourceName(fileName) 'notATestFile'
			throw SourceParseError.testOutsideTestFile(fileName, testFileName: testSourceNameFor(fileName), line: testTok.line, column: testTok.column)
		end 'notATestFile'

		// The name is the test's whole identity — what the reporter prints, and what `end ''` must repeat —
		// so an empty one names nothing. Refused at the declaration rather than at the report, which is the
		// least useful place to discover an unnamed failure.
		if nameTok.value.isEmpty() 'unnamed'
			throw SourceParseError.testEmptyName(nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'unnamed'

		try self.requireLineEndAfter(LineEndingConstruct.blockLabel)

		return nameTok
	end 'readTestDeclarationHead'

	// ⭐ **E3107 — TWO TESTS IN ONE FILE WHOSE PROSE NAMES SANITIZE ALIKE.** Asked at the mint, so the
	// declaration that collides is the one blamed and the incumbent is only named.
	//
	// ⚠ **A LINEAR SCAN, AND THE REASON IS THE BOUND — NOT THAT NOTHING ELSE FITS.** It is Θ(t²) in the
	// tests of ONE file (`mintedTests` is a `Parser` field, and `Queries.fileReader` builds a fresh `Parser`
	// per path), and t is the tests a human wrote into one file: a handful, where a scan of contiguous bytes
	// beats hashing and the array is the SAME single per-file allocation a map would be.
	//
	// ⛔ **"A MAP CANNOT ANSWER THIS" IS FALSE TWICE OVER, and the reason is recorded because a false reason
	// is what invites the wrong edit later.** The argument is "an allocation per file for a membership
	// question a `Set` cannot answer anyway", but `MintedTestArray` is already that allocation, and the type
	// that fits is `Map with ByteArray,`
	// ByteArray` (symbol ⇒ the earlier test's prose) answers membership AND hands back the prose the
	// message must name, in one O(1) `get`. It exists and this very file uses the shape
	// (`MutableVarPositionByName`). ⇒ **If t ever stops being a handful — a generated
	// test file would do it — that Map is the change, and nothing here argues against it.**
	function requireUnmintedTestName(symbol immutable ByteArray, nameTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		for minted in self.mintedTests 'eachMintedTest'
			if minted.symbol.equals(symbol) 'collides'
				if minted.line > nameTok.line or (minted.line == nameTok.line and minted.column > nameTok.column) 'theMintedOneIsLater'
					throw SourceParseError.duplicateTestName(String.from(nameTok.value), second: String.from(minted.prose), symbol: String.from(symbol), line: minted.line, column: minted.column)
				end 'theMintedOneIsLater'

				throw SourceParseError.duplicateTestName(String.from(minted.prose), second: String.from(nameTok.value), symbol: String.from(symbol), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'collides'
		end 'eachMintedTest'

		self.mintedTests.push(MintedTest.create(symbol, prose: nameTok.value, line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column))
	end 'requireUnmintedTestName'

	// ⭐ **A LABEL WRITTEN AFTER A BLOCK'S `end` MUST REPEAT THE LABEL ITS HEADER OPENED**, on every block the
	// real parse closes: E2008, or E2043 for a `match` (`rule`). A declaration's opening label is its NAME. Only
	// a `test` must write the label, because its label IS the name the reporter prints. Positioned at the `end`.
	//
	// ⚠ The tolerant declaration sweep steps over these same labels with `skipOptionalLabel` and judges
	// nothing; the real parse reaches every one of them and is the one place the refusal is raised.
	//
	// ⛔ **`got` IS HAND-SPELLED, NOT ROUTED THROUGH `tokenDisplayText`**: that helper's fallback rests on "an
	// empty value means the KIND implies the text", which is false of a block label, whose value IS the label
	// and may legitimately be empty. Through the helper, `end ''` would report `got 'character literal'` — a
	// reader hunting for a string that is not in the file.
	function consumeBlockEnd(openingLabel ByteArray, labelIsRequired bool, rule EndLabelRule = EndLabelRule.block) throws SourceParseError
		let endTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.end)

		if not labelIsRequired and not self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'labelOmitted'
			return
		end 'labelOmitted'

		let closeTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)

		if self.closerRepeats(closeTok.value, openingLabel: openingLabel) 'repeatsIt'
			return
		end 'repeatsIt'

		match rule 'rule'
			block then throw SourceParseError.mismatchedEndLabel(String.from(openingLabel), got: String.from(closeTok.value), line: endTok.line, column: endTok.column)
			matchArm then throw SourceParseError.matchMismatchedBlockId(String.from(openingLabel), got: String.from(closeTok.value), line: endTok.line, column: endTok.column)
		end 'rule'
	end 'consumeBlockEnd'

	// A label as the name space this file is parsed in spells it. `shadowRenamedTokens` moves a contested
	// stdlib declaration's NAME token and never a label, so `type Ascii … end 'Ascii'` opens as `__Ascii` and
	// closes as `Ascii`; asked of both sides, a block label that happens to spell a contested name moves on
	// both.
	function closerRepeats(label ByteArray, openingLabel ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.labelInNameSpace(label).equals(self.labelInNameSpace(openingLabel))
	end 'closerRepeats'

	function noteDeclarationCloser(closer ElementIndex, openingLabel ByteArray, bodyStart ElementIndex)
		if self.misclosedBlock.isRecorded() or self.kindAt(closer + 1) != TokenKind.charLiteral 'nothingToCheck'
			return
		end 'nothingToCheck'

		let label = self.tokenAt(closer + 1).value

		if self.closerRepeats(label, openingLabel: openingLabel) 'repeatsIt'
			return
		end 'repeatsIt'

		match self.scanBlockClosers(bodyStart, opening: OpenedBlockLabel.unclaimed(openingLabel)) 'scan'
			misclosed(expected, at) then self.misclosedBlock = MisclosedBlock.recorded(String.from(expected), got: String.from(self.tokenAt(at + 1).value), closer: at, bodyStart: bodyStart)
			closed or
			unterminated then return
		end 'scan'
	end 'noteDeclarationCloser'

	function recordReusedBlockLabels()
		let keyword = pastVisibilityModifiers(self.tokens, index: self.pos)
		let kind = self.kindAt(keyword)

		if kind != TokenKind.function and not tokenKindDeclaresANominalType(kind) and kind != TokenKind.extension and not testDeclarationAt(self.tokens, index: keyword) 'hasNoBody'
			return
		end 'hasNoBody'

		match self.scanBlockClosers(keyword + 2, opening: self.claimedLabelAt(keyword + 1)) 'scan'
			closed(reuses) then self.recordEachReusedBlockLabel(reuses)
			misclosed or
			unterminated then return
		end 'scan'
	end 'recordReusedBlockLabels'

	function recordEachReusedBlockLabel(reuses LabelReuseArray)
		for reuse in reuses 'eachReuse'
			match reuse 'found'
				reused(label, outerAt, innerAt) then self.recordArtifactDiagnostic(ErrorCode.blockLabelReused, message: reusedBlockLabelMessage(String.from(label), outer: self.tokenAt(outerAt)), range: SourceRange.ofToken(self.tokenAt(innerAt)))
			end 'found'
		end 'eachReuse'
	end 'recordEachReusedBlockLabel'

	function scanBlockClosers(bodyStart ElementIndex, opening OpenedBlockLabel) returns BlockCloserScan
		let open = OpenLabels.create()
		open.open(opening)
		var q = bodyStart

		while self.kindAt(q) != TokenKind.eof 'walk'
			// A body missing its `end` must not pair against, or take labels from, the declarations after it.
			if q > bodyStart and self.tokenAt(q).column == DeclarationHeadColumn and declarationOpensIn(self.tokens, index: q) 'nextDeclaration'
				return BlockCloserScan.unterminated
			end 'nextDeclaration'

			if self.functionDeclarationAt(q) 'declaration'
				open.open(self.claimedLabelAt(q + 1))
			end 'declaration' else if self.opensBlockAt(q) or self.armBlockIfAt(q) 'opens'
				open.open(self.blockHeaderLabelAt(q))
			end 'opens' else if self.closesBlockAt(q) 'closes'
				let innermost = open.close()

				if self.kindAt(q + 1) == TokenKind.charLiteral 'labelled'
					let label = self.tokenAt(q + 1).value
					let expected = innermost.expectedAt(label)

					if not self.closerRepeats(label, openingLabel: expected) 'misclosed'
						return BlockCloserScan.misclosed(expected, closer: q)
					end 'misclosed'
				end 'labelled'

				if open.isEmpty() 'outermostClosed'
					return BlockCloserScan.closed(open.foundReuses())
				end 'outermostClosed'
			end 'closes'

			q = q + 1
		end 'walk'

		return BlockCloserScan.unterminated
	end 'scanBlockClosers'

	function claimedLabelAt(index ElementIndex) returns OpenedBlockLabel
		let label = self.tokenAt(index).value
		return OpenedBlockLabel.claimed(label, key: self.labelInNameSpace(label), at: index)
	end 'claimedLabelAt'

	function blockHeaderLabelAt(opener ElementIndex) returns OpenedBlockLabel
		var q = opener + 1

		while not tokenKindEndsALine(self.kindAt(q)) 'header'
			q = q + 1
		end 'header'

		if self.kindAt(q - 1) != TokenKind.charLiteral 'noTrailingLabel'
			return OpenedBlockLabel.unread
		end 'noTrailingLabel'

		return self.claimedLabelAt(q - 1)
	end 'blockHeaderLabelAt'

	export function publishMisclosedBlock()
		match self.misclosedBlock 'misclosed'
			none then return
			recorded(expected, got, closer, bodyStart) then self.signatures.recordMisclosedBlock(MisclosedBlockSite.create(self.job.path.toString(), refusal: SourceParseError.mismatchedEndLabel(expected, got: got, line: self.tokenAt(closer).line, column: self.tokenAt(closer).column), bodyStart: positionKey(self.tokenAt(bodyStart).line, column: self.tokenAt(bodyStart).column), closer: positionKey(self.tokenAt(closer).line, column: self.tokenAt(closer).column), corruptibleNames: self.namesDeclaredFrom(bodyStart)))
		end 'misclosed'
	end 'publishMisclosedBlock'

	function namesDeclaredFrom(start ElementIndex) returns NameRoster
		let names = NameRoster.create(0)
		var q = start

		while self.kindAt(q) != TokenKind.eof 'walk'
			if self.declaresANameOutsideABodyAt(q) 'aDeclaration'
				names.push(self.tokenAt(q + 1).value)
			end 'aDeclaration'

			q = q + 1
		end 'walk'

		return names
	end 'namesDeclaredFrom'

	function declaresANameOutsideABodyAt(index ElementIndex) returns bool
		let kind = self.kindAt(index)
		let declaresAName = kind == TokenKind.function or kind == TokenKind.type or kind == TokenKind.enum or kind == TokenKind.union or kind == TokenKind.interface or kind == TokenKind.typealias or kind == TokenKind.let or kind == TokenKind.var

		if not declaresAName or not tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(index + 1)) or not declarationBeginsStatementIn(self.tokens, index: index) 'notADeclaration'
			return false
		end 'notADeclaration'

		if kind != TokenKind.let and kind != TokenKind.var 'namesADeclarationEverywhere'
			return true
		end 'namesADeclarationEverywhere'

		return self.kindAt(index + 2) == TokenKind.as or (index > 0 and declarationModifierIn(self.tokens, index: index - 1)) or self.tokenAt(index).column == 1
	end 'declaresANameOutsideABodyAt'

	function labelInNameSpace(label immutable ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
		if not self.job.isStdlibSource and self.signatures.hasAuthorMints() 'anAuthorFileBesideMints'
			return self.signatures.authorMintOf(self.job.path, name: label)
		end 'anAuthorFileBesideMints'

		if not self.job.isStdlibSource or not self.signatures.interfaceMap().hasLibraryRenames() 'notRenamed'
			return label
		end 'notRenamed'

		return self.signatures.interfaceMap().libraryNameOf(label)
	end 'labelInNameSpace'

	function requireNoWitnessCursorElementIsDropped(owner String, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws SourceParseError
		if not self.bodyState.droppedAWitnessCursorElement 'nothingOwed'
			return
		end 'nothingOwed'

		throw SourceParseError.unsupported("{owner} owns an opaque type-parameter value it must release on some path: the element of a `for … in` over a value held at a PARAMETERIZED interface, which is `current()`'s result, dispatched through a witness table into a generic conformer's impl — and a witness table carries no layout descriptor for that impl to read, so the element cannot be released. Store the element into an `Array with <type parameter>`, iterate `self`, or iterate in a method of a concrete instantiation", line: line, column: column)
	end 'requireNoWitnessCursorElementIsDropped'

	// Commit this function's accumulated range-check sites to the artifact, keyed by `name`. Files
	// nothing when the function has no site, no ranged parameter, no proven interval, no unsigned mark
	// and no checked call — the common case — so the registry stays proportional to the checks a
	// program actually contains.
	function commitRangeChecks(name ByteArray)
		self.reportBareUnsignedLiterals()

		// Loop intervals ride the same channel and files on the same terms: a function whose only side data is a
		// counted loop's interval still files an entry, because that interval is how this function's own
		// CALL SITES are proved at the Std tier (`InlineLeaves`) — a fact nobody but this function can state.
		let hasProvenRanges = self.functionProvedAnInterval()

		// ⚠ A RANGED PARAMETER IS ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, even for a function with no site of its own: the entry is
		// how this function's CALLERS learn what its parameters were declared as (`paramSites`) and where its own
		// entry guard goes, and the caller's site names it by the name this map is keyed by.
		if self.bodyState.rangeCheckSites.isEmpty() and self.bodyState.rangeCheckArgSites.isEmpty() and self.bodyState.rangeCheckReturnSites.isEmpty() and self.bodyState.rangeCheckParamSites.isEmpty() and not hasProvenRanges and not valueMarksWritten(self.bodyState.unsignedMaxLiterals) and not valueMarksWritten(self.bodyState.unsignedPatternValues) and not valueMarksWritten(self.bodyState.checkedCalls) 'noSideData'
			return
		end 'noSideData'

		self.artifact.recordFunctionRangeChecks(name, checks: FunctionRangeChecks.create(self.job.path, sites: self.bodyState.rangeCheckSites, argSites: self.bodyState.rangeCheckArgSites, returnAlias: self.bodyState.rangeCheckReturnAlias, returnSites: self.bodyState.rangeCheckReturnSites, paramSites: self.bodyState.rangeCheckParamSites, provenValueRanges: self.bodyState.countedValueRanges, provenFloatRanges: self.bodyState.provenFloatRanges, writtenNegativeLiterals: self.bodyState.writtenNegativeLiterals, unsignedMaxLiterals: self.bodyState.unsignedMaxLiterals, unsignedPatternValues: self.bodyState.unsignedPatternValues, checkedCalls: self.bodyState.checkedCalls, wrapOperands: self.bodyState.wrapOperands))
	end 'commitRangeChecks'

	// The function's return type IF it is a ranged alias — integer or float — else `undeclared`
	// (`ExitCode`, a bare primitive, void: none is a user ranged alias). Asked once per function, off
	// the DECLARED return type, before `resolveTypes` collapses the alias away — which is the whole
	// reason the pass reads a side table rather than the resolved return type.
	//
	// ⭐ A FLOAT ALIAS IS INCLUDED: the pass carries its own float arm (`aliasIsFloat` and the
	// predicates it forks), so the domain is decided where the bounds are read rather than where the
	// site is recorded.
	function rangedReturnAlias(returnType MaxonReturnType) returns DeclaredAlias
		match returnType 'r'
			void then return self.anchors.undeclaredAlias
			value(inner) then return self.rangeCheckedDeclaredAlias(inner)
		end 'r'
	end 'rangedReturnAlias'

	function rangeCheckedDeclaredAlias(t MaxonType) returns DeclaredAlias
		let alias = try self.rangeCheckedAliasOf(t) otherwise return self.anchors.undeclaredAlias
		return DeclaredAlias.declared(alias.clone())
	end 'rangeCheckedDeclaredAlias'

	// ⭐ **THE PARAMETER TWIN OF THE LINE ABOVE — one site per RANGED-ALIAS parameter, and a THROW when none
	// of them is.** Asked once per function, off the declared parameter types, at the one point they still
	// carry their names.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT ANSWERS "NONE" WITH A THROW RATHER THAN AN EMPTY ARRAY.**
	// Returning the empty list would mean returning SOMETHING, and the cheapest something is a
	// module-level shared array — which would then have to be laundered through a `var` binding, guarded by an
	// `is`-identity test and privatised by a free function before any `push`, an invariant held by nothing
	// but a comment. See `RangedParamError` for why the absence is an error instead.
	//
	// It exists because a CALL ARGUMENT cannot be checked where it is written. Every other position holds
	// its own declared type; a call argument's type belongs to the callee, which may be declared in a file
	// this one is parsed independently of — the whole-program `ProgramSignatures` sweep carries return
	// types, not parameter types — so the alias has to be PUBLISHED here and read back by the whole-program
	// pass. See `RangeCheckArgSite`; the same record is also the anchor of the entry guard that
	// enforces the range on an argument no constant folds (`RangeCheckParamSite`), which is why the site
	// carries the parameter NAME TOKEN's line and column.
	//
	// ⚠ It walks the DECLARED list, not the reserved one: `paramTypes` holds exactly the user's
	// parameters, while the ValueId space `parseFunction` reserves also carries existential witnesses,
	// the layout descriptor and the witness slots. `argSlotPosition` maps an argument to a position in
	// the USER list, and a user parameter's ValueId IS that position, so indexing anything wider would
	// slide every alias by the count of hidden slots.
	//
	// The array is built lazily — most functions take no ranged parameter at all, and one shared empty
	// array is the difference between that and one allocation per function in the program.
	function rangedParamSites(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray) returns RangeCheckParamSiteArray throws RangedParamError
		if not self.declaresARangedParam(paramTypes) 'noneRanged'
			throw RangedParamError.noRangedParameters
		end 'noneRanged'

		let sites = RangeCheckParamSiteArray.create(0)

		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("rangedParamSites: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			let alias = try self.rangeCheckedAliasOf(paramType) otherwise continue

			let nameRange = try paramRanges.get(i) otherwise panic("rangedParamSites: paramRanges.get OOB at {i} — one range is pushed per parameter name token, so the two lists have the same length")
			sites.push(RangeCheckParamSite.create(i as ParamCount, alias: alias.clone(), line: nameRange.line, column: nameRange.column))
		end 'eachParam'

		return sites
	end 'rangedParamSites'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS PARAMETER LIST DECLARE A RANGED ALIAS AT ALL** — the question `rangedParamSites` must
	// answer BEFORE it builds anything, so that a function with none allocates no list to report it with.
	//
	// It answers a `bool` rather than the first ranged index, and that is the same rule this pair
	// exists to obey: an index would need a *"none"* spelling, and the only ones available are a
	// sentinel past the end or a second error — one sentinel traded for another. The cost is that the
	// fill loop below re-probes from 0; that is `count()` extra `rangeCheckedAliasOf` lookups on the
	// RARE path where a ranged parameter exists, against zero allocations on the common path where
	// none does.
	function declaresARangedParam(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray) returns bool
		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("declaresARangedParam: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")

			if self.declaresARangedAlias(self.declaredAliasOfType(paramType), t: paramType) 'ranged'
				return true
			end 'ranged'
		end 'eachParam'

		return false
	end 'declaresARangedParam'

	function declaresARangedAlias(fileScoped DeclaredAlias, t MaxonType) returns bool
		return match self.fileScopedOrInnerAliasOfType(fileScoped, t: t) 'resolved'
			declared gives true
			undeclared gives false
		end 'resolved'
	end 'declaresARangedAlias'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A `MaxonType` IS ASKED WHETHER IT DENOTES A RANGED ALIAS.** Every reader of that
	// question goes through here — the return path above, and the `as`-cast path's TWO readers (which
	// alias a narrowing check names, and what that alias's range says about the shift fill).
	//
	// It hands back the ALIAS rather than its name so a caller that needs both answers pays for one
	// lookup: `signatures.aliasOf` is a whole-program registry probe that CONSTRUCTS its `DeclaredAlias`,
	// so a second call is a second allocation and not a second field read. `applyCast` is that caller.
	function declaredAliasOfType(t MaxonType) returns DeclaredAlias
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.declaredAliasOfNameId(typeNameIdOf(t))
	end 'declaredAliasOfType'

	// The file-scoped alias, else one declared inside a type or extension body, which
	// `declaredAliasOfType` cannot see: a range check resolved through that alone guards no nested
	// alias at all.
	function rangeCheckedAliasOf(t MaxonType) returns RangedTypeAlias throws MapError
		return try self.rangeCheckedAlias(self.declaredAliasOfType(t), t: t)
	end 'rangeCheckedAliasOf'

	function rangeCheckedAlias(fileScoped DeclaredAlias, t MaxonType) returns RangedTypeAlias throws MapError
		return try requireDeclaredAlias(self.fileScopedOrInnerAliasOfType(fileScoped, t: t))
	end 'rangeCheckedAlias'

	function fileScopedOrInnerAliasOfType(fileScoped DeclaredAlias, t MaxonType) returns DeclaredAlias
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return fileScoped
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.signatures.fileScopedOrInnerDeclaredAlias(fileScoped, name: self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(t)))
	end 'fileScopedOrInnerAliasOfType'

	// The same question asked of a VALUE rather than of a written type — the shape the `try … otherwise`
	// check needs, where what is in hand is the try's result and not the callee's declared return type.
	// A ranged-int alias value carries tag `named` and its alias name in the parallel name column (see
	// `aggregateNameIn`), so the two routes reach the identical registry probe below.
	function declaredAliasOfValue(value ValueId) returns DeclaredAlias
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.declaredAliasOfNameId(self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'declaredAliasOfValue'

	// The registry probe both spellings above end in, written once: a file-local interner id becomes the
	// name, and the name becomes this FILE's answer for it (the alias registry is scoped per declaring
	// file, so the reader's path is not optional).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CANNOT TELL THE FILLER ID FROM A REAL ONE, AND NOTHING HERE CAN.** `UnnamedTypeId` is 0 and
	// `TypeNameInterner` starts empty, so id 0 is the first name a file mentions; a guard here would
	// refuse that name. The obligation is on the MINT — a value tagged `named` carries a real id — and
	// `parseUnary`'s `-` arm records what it cost when one did not.
	function declaredAliasOfNameId(nameId TypeNameId) returns DeclaredAlias
		if let answered = try self.memos.declaredAliases.get(nameId) 'alreadyAsked'
			return answered
		end 'alreadyAsked'

		self.memos.declaredAliases.file(nameId, value: self.signatures.aliasOrInnerAliasOf(self.internedName(nameId), readerFilePath: self.job.path))
		return try self.memos.declaredAliases.get(nameId) otherwise panic("declaredAliasOfNameId: the answer filed for {nameId} one line up is missing")
	end 'declaredAliasOfNameId'

	// ⭐ **THE SAME QUESTION ASKED OF AN INDEX-SPACE TYPE** — one read out of `ProgramSignatures` (a
	// container's element, an instance's substituted argument) rather than minted by this file's parse. Same
	// registry probe, DIFFERENT INTERNER, and the difference is not cosmetic: the id means one name in the
	// index's table and whatever name that NUMBER happens to hold in this file's. `indexSpaceTypeName` carries
	// the general measurement; the concrete one here is that `[10 as Byte, 20 as Byte, 30 as Byte]` panicked
	// `internedName` outright the moment the element door was routed through `declaredAliasOfType` above.
	function declaredAliasOfIndexSpaceType(indexSpaceType MaxonType) returns DeclaredAlias
		if maxonTypeTag(indexSpaceType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'spellsNoAlias'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'spellsNoAlias'

		return self.signatures.aliasOrInnerAliasOf(self.signatures.indexSpaceTypeName(indexSpaceType), readerFilePath: self.job.path)
	end 'declaredAliasOfIndexSpaceType'

	// Close a function body. `bodyEnd` is the block its last statement left control in;
	// when that block has no terminator, control can FALL OFF THE END of the function
	// and the parser must supply what the source did not:
	//
	//   • VOID function — the implicit `return`. This is why `function doNothing()
	//     end 'doNothing'` compiles (specs/empty-block.md), and it is what every void
	//     function needs, not just an empty one: no void body writes a trailing
	//     `return`. Without it the block reaches the backend in `Terminator.unset`.
	//   • VALUE-returning function — an error: control would leave with no value to
	//     return. E3013, positioned at the function's name (specs/missing-return-error.md).
	//
	// An already-terminated `bodyEnd` covers the UNREACHABLE case for free: an `if`
	// whose branches all return leaves its continuation marked `dead`, which counts as
	// terminated — so `main` ending in a fully-returning `if`/`else` is not a missing
	// return. The synthetic `retVoid` is anchored on the function's name for the same
	// reason E3013 is: no source token spawned it, and the name is where a reader looks
	// for "what does this function return?".
	//
	// Reads the return type off `func` rather than taking it as a parameter — the same
	// place `parseReturnStatement` reads it (see there). `nameTok` still rides along: it
	// is the SPAN the synthetic `retVoid` and the E3013 are anchored on, and a span is
	// not something `IrFunction` carries.
	function closeFunctionBody(func IrFunction, bodyEnd IrBlock, nameTok Token) throws SourceParseError
		if bodyEnd.isTerminated() 'terminated'
			return
		end 'terminated'

		if not maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(func.maxonReturnType) 'fallsOffEnd'
			throw SourceParseError.missingReturn(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'fallsOffEnd'

		// ⭐ **THE IMPLICIT RETURN IS A RETURN, AND IT OWES THE SAME DROPS** — this is the THIRD
		// exit path a function has. Hanging `emitFunctionExit` off `parseReturnStatement` alone leaves
		// a void function that simply ENDS —
		//
		//     function helper()
		//         let p = Point.create(1, y: 2)
		//     end 'helper'
		//
		// — never dropping `p`, and it reports exit 101 on a perfectly valid program. It is exactly the
		// case this routine exists for: "no void body writes a trailing `return`" is the sentence
		// three lines up, and it is precisely why the drop cannot live only where `return` is
		// WRITTEN. A VALUE function cannot reach here (E3013 above), so the leak is void-only,
		// which is why no acceptance case sees it.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE DROPS AND THE TERMINATOR GO TO THE SAME BLOCK, AND IT IS `bodyEnd`.** `bodyEnd` is not
		// `self.bodyState.currentBlock`: a body ending in an `if`/`while` leaves `currentBlock` at that construct's BODY,
		// so drops emitted through it land on one path of a branch whose `retVoid` is on another. See
		// `emitScopeDrops`.
		self.emitFunctionExit(bodyEnd, exit: FunctionExit.returnVoid, anchor: nameTok)
	end 'closeFunctionBody'

	// ⭐⭐ **WALK THE CURSOR PAST A BODY THIS PARSE IS NOT BUILDING, leaving it ON the function's own `end`**
	// — exactly where `parseStatements` leaves it, so the caller's `consume(TokenKind.end)` and its optional
	// label are untouched and this is invisible to everything after the call.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A THIRD READER OF THE BLOCK RULE, NOT A THIRD COPY OF IT.** Maxon's block structure is
	// written in `parseStatement`/`parseBlockBody` and re-derived once in `opensBlockAt`/`closesBlockAt`;
	// this asks those predicates, exactly as `ensureSiblingReceivers` does, so a construct added to the
	// grammar is learned here through the same door. The `functionDeclarationAt` term is that walk's
	// documented +1: a NAMED function opens a block `opensBlockAt` does not count (it knows
	// `if`/`while`/`match`/`try`), while a CLOSURE is `function(` — no name, no `end` — so it needs none.
	//
	// ⚠ The entry block still needs a terminator or the module is malformed, and `retVoid` is what it gets
	// whatever the declared return type is. That is sound ONLY because a skipped body is by construction one
	// no path from `main` reaches: `FrontEndRun`'s fixpoint demands every body the reachability walk
	// touches, and `DeadFunctionElimination.requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` turns a disagreement
	// into a panic rather than letting a `retVoid` stand in for a value the program would have read.
	function skipUnreachedFunctionBody(entry IrBlock, nameTok Token)
		let bodyStart = self.pos
		self.advanceToOwnBlockEnd()
		self.artifact.unbuiltBodies.push(UnbuiltBody.create(bodyStart, limit: self.pos))
		self.emitBodyTerminator(entry, op: MaxonOp.retVoid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
	end 'skipUnreachedFunctionBody'

	// The declaration keeps its signature, so its callers are still checked against it. The empty body
	// is never lowered: the refusal that abandoned it stands at the error gate.
	function skipAbandonedBody(entry IrBlock, nameTok Token)
		self.advanceToOwnBlockEnd()
		self.advancePastBlockCloser()
		self.emitBodyTerminator(entry, op: MaxonOp.retVoid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
	end 'skipAbandonedBody'

	function skipAbandonedDeclaration()
		let declarationColumn = self.lineStartColumnAt(self.pos)
		let keyword = pastVisibilityModifiers(self.tokens, index: self.pos)

		if self.functionDeclarationAt(keyword) or testDeclarationAt(self.tokens, index: keyword) 'aBlockDeclaration'
			self.pos = keyword + 1
			self.advanceToOwnBlockEnd()
			self.advancePastBlockCloser()
			return
		end 'aBlockDeclaration'

		// A `typealias` has no body, so no `end` of its own for a closer search to find.
		if typeDeclarationHeaderIn(self.tokens, index: keyword) and self.kindAt(keyword) != TokenKind.typealias 'aTypeDeclaration'
			self.pos = self.typeBodyCloserAfter(keyword, membersHaveBodies: self.kindAt(keyword) != TokenKind.interface)
			self.advancePastBlockCloser()
			return
		end 'aTypeDeclaration'

		self.advance()
		self.advancePastDeclarationEnd(declarationColumn)
	end 'skipAbandonedDeclaration'

	function advancePastBlockCloser()
		self.advance()

		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'aLabel'
			self.advance()
		end 'aLabel'
	end 'advancePastBlockCloser'

	function advancePastDeclarationEnd(declarationColumn ColumnNumber)
		while self.kindAt(self.pos) != TokenKind.eof 'scan'
			if self.beginsALineAt(self.pos) 'aLineStart'
				let column = self.tokenAt(self.pos).column

				if self.at(TokenKind.end) and column == declarationColumn 'itsOwnCloser'
					self.advancePastBlockCloser()
					return
				end 'itsOwnCloser'

				if column < declarationColumn or (column == declarationColumn and self.opensADeclarationAt(self.pos)) 'leavesIt'
					return
				end 'leavesIt'
			end 'aLineStart'

			self.advance()
		end 'scan'
	end 'advancePastDeclarationEnd'

	function beginsALineAt(index ElementIndex) returns bool
		return index > 0 and self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.newline and self.tokenAt(index).line != self.tokenAt(index - 1).line
	end 'beginsALineAt'

	function opensADeclarationAt(index ElementIndex) returns bool
		let keyword = pastVisibilityModifiers(self.tokens, index: index)

		return declarationHeadShapeIn(self.tokens, index: keyword) and not (self.kindAt(keyword) == TokenKind.function and self.keywordIsAName(keyword))
	end 'opensADeclarationAt'

	function lineStartColumnAt(index ElementIndex) returns ColumnNumber
		let line = self.tokenAt(index).line
		var start = index

		while start > 0 and self.tokenAt(start - 1).line == line 'toTheLineStart'
			start = start - 1
		end 'toTheLineStart'

		return self.tokenAt(start).column
	end 'lineStartColumnAt'

	function advanceToOwnBlockEnd()
		var depth = 0 as BlockDepth

		while self.kindAt(self.pos) != TokenKind.eof 'scan'
			if self.functionDeclarationAt(self.pos) or self.opensBlockAt(self.pos) or self.armBlockIfAt(self.pos) 'opens'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'opens' else if self.closesBlockAt(self.pos) 'closes'
				if depth == 0 'ownEnd'
					break
				end 'ownEnd'

				depth = depth - 1
			end 'closes'

			self.advance()
		end 'scan'
	end 'advanceToOwnBlockEnd'

	// A block `if` after `then` is refused (E2049) but still closes with its own `end`, which
	// `opensBlockAt` does not match to an opener; a scan that skips it ends a region one `end` early.
	function armBlockIfAt(index ElementIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.if and index > 0 and self.kindAt(index - 1) == TokenKind.then and not self.keywordIsAName(index)
	end 'armBlockIfAt'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3012 — THE FIRST BINDING THIS FUNCTION DECLARED AND NEVER MENTIONED.** Called at the `end` of
	// a function, once the whole body has been seen, because "never mentioned" is a fact about the WHOLE
	// body and about no smaller part of it. A candidate is a PARAMETER, a `for` binding or a body
	// `var`, which is why the list is walked with a `continue` and its kind is asked at the waiver below
	// rather than once before the loop.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **BODY `var`s ARE IN, AND NO DISCARD RULE BLOCKS THAT.** A value that must be neither bound nor read
	// always has a spelling — `_ = <call>` (`parseAssignment`'s `DiscardBindingName` arm) — so enrolling a body
	// `var` here cannot leave an author with nowhere to put one. E3064 refuses that spelling only for a
	// callee the effect summary proves pure, which is a rule about the CALLEE and not about this candidate.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE UNUSED-`let` HALF COSTS 82 CASES OF THIS SUITE, in EXPRESSIVENESS rather than tidiness.**
	// See `UnusedBindingKind.mutableLocal`, which carries the measurement and says which spec owns it.
	// A body `var` rides because E3077 owes it.
	//
	// ⇒ **it returns a VERDICT, because E3077 rides behind it** (`reportVarShouldBeLet`): "an unused
	// `var` reports as UNUSED, not as should-be-let" is the two scans' ORDER over one list.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **"REFERENCED" IS A FACT ABOUT THE NAME, NOT ABOUT THE BINDING**, so the verdict asks TWO questions
	// and the second is the one a per-binding bit cannot answer: two sequential `for i` loops mint two
	// `VarInfo`s, and the oracle ACCEPTS the pair when only the second reads `i` (its `_referencedVars` is a
	// per-function `HashSet<string>`). See `referencedNames`, which is where that half lives, and
	// `specs/unused-variable-name-scope.md`, which pins it.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE DIAGNOSTIC PER FUNCTION, AND IT IS THE EARLIEST DECLARATION.** `unusedCandidates` is in
	// declaration order — parameters as `bindParameters` mints them, then each `for` binding and each
	// body `var` as the body reaches it — so a forward scan reports the leftmost unused one. The scan
	// stops there because the rest are the same fact about the same edit, and `multiple-unused` (two
	// unused parameters) pins exactly one line.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CANDIDATE LIST IS IN TRUE SOURCE ORDER, WITH NOTHING TO PIN IT.** Pushing every
	// parameter and then every local would make an unused parameter outrank an unused local declared
	// above it. No case in the suite declares an unused parameter and an unused local in one function,
	// and source order is the rule the header above already states.
	//
	// ⚠ **A FUNCTION WHOSE BODY FAILED TO PARSE NEVER REACHES HERE**: a `SourceParseError` unwinds past this
	// call, and the parse that follows skips the abandoned body whole, so a body with a real error
	// never also reports its unused names as cascade noise. The suppression is structural and needs no
	// rule of its own.
	function reportUnusedBindings(methodName ByteArray) returns bool
		for candidateIndex in 0 upto self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.count() 'eachCandidate'
			let candidate = try self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.get(candidateIndex) otherwise panic("reportUnusedBindings: candidateIndex {candidateIndex} is below self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.count()")

			if self.bindingNameWasMentioned(candidate.binding) 'wasMentioned'
				continue
			end 'wasMentioned'

			// ⭐ **THE WAIVER IS ABOUT PARAMETERS AND MUST NOT REACH A LOOP BINDING OR A LOCAL.** MEASURED on
			// the oracle: an unread `for i in 0 upto limit 'l'` inside an interface-dictated method is still
			// `error E3012: unused variable: 'i'`, and so is an unread body `let`. The oracle draws the same
			// line structurally — its `skipParamCheck` skips the parameter loop and never the locals loop.
			//
			// A `continue` rather than a `return`: with three candidate kinds in one list, a
			// waived parameter must not suppress a later declaration the contract has no say over. Asked
			// here rather than before the loop because it walks two registries, and the overwhelming majority
			// of functions never reach it; `and` short-circuits, so a non-parameter never pays for it.
			let waivable = match candidate.kind 'candidateKind'
				parameter gives true
				loopVariable or
				mutableLocal or
				immutableLocal or

				// A catching form's `(e)` and a `match` arm's payload binder are the author's own and optional
				// — no contract can force either to be declared, so there is nothing for the interface waiver
				// to excuse.
				caughtError or
				payloadBinding gives false
			end 'candidateKind'

			if waivable and self.parametersAreInterfaceDictated(methodName) 'contractDictatedTheList'
				continue
			end 'contractDictatedTheList'

			self.recordArtifactDiagnostic(ErrorCode.semanticUnusedVariable, message: "unused variable: '{String.from((try self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.get(candidateIndex) otherwise panic("reportUnusedBindings: candidateIndex {candidateIndex} is below self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.count()")).binding.name)}'", range: (try self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.get(candidateIndex) otherwise panic("reportUnusedBindings: candidateIndex {candidateIndex} is below self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.count()")).nameRange)
			return true
		end 'eachCandidate'

		return false
	end 'reportUnusedBindings'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3077 — THE FIRST `var` THIS FUNCTION DECLARED AND NEVER NEEDED WRITABLE.** The twin of the
	// scan above, over the SAME candidate list, run only when that one reported nothing — which is the
	// whole of "E3012 takes precedence" (`unused-takes-precedence`: `var x = 10` + `return 0` is the
	// unused diagnostic, not this one).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CANDIDATE TEST IS THE KIND `mutableLocal`, WHICH IS EXACTLY "THE AUTHOR WROTE `var`".** The
	// binding's own `isMutable()` is NOT that question and cannot be made into it: a `match` arm's payload
	// binder takes its mutability from the SCRUTINEE (`declarePayloadBindings`), so a writable `value(n)` the
	// arm never reassigns would be told to "use `let`" for a `var` its author never wrote.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `var` NOTHING MENTIONED IS SKIPPED RATHER THAN REPORTED, and that is not the same as the
	// precedence above.** The precedence covers one function reaching both rules; this covers the
	// SECOND unused declaration, which E3012 deliberately did not report (one diagnostic per
	// function). Reporting it here would say "use `let`" about a binding whose real fix is deletion.
	function reportVarShouldBeLet()
		for candidateIndex in 0 upto self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.count() 'eachCandidate'
			if not self.isVarShouldBeLetCandidate(candidateIndex) 'notACandidate'
				continue
			end 'notACandidate'

			let candidate = try self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.get(candidateIndex) otherwise panic("reportVarShouldBeLet: candidateIndex {candidateIndex} is below self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.count()")
			self.recordArtifactDiagnostic(ErrorCode.semanticVarShouldBeLet, message: varShouldBeLetMessage(String.from(candidate.binding.name), boundFromContainerLiteral: candidate.boundFromContainerLiteral), range: candidate.nameRange)
		end 'eachCandidate'
	end 'reportVarShouldBeLet'

	function isVarShouldBeLetCandidate(candidateIndex ElementIndex) returns bool
		let candidate = try self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.get(candidateIndex) otherwise panic("isVarShouldBeLetCandidate: candidateIndex {candidateIndex} is below self.bodyState.unusedCandidates.count()")

		if candidate.kind != UnusedBindingKind.mutableLocal or not self.bindingNameWasMentioned(candidate.binding) 'notAVarOrUnusedIsE3012sToReport'
			return false
		end 'notAVarOrUnusedIsE3012sToReport'

		return not candidate.binding.mutabilityExercised and not self.nameIsListed(self.bodyState.mutabilityExercisedNames, name: candidate.binding.name)
	end 'isVarShouldBeLetCandidate'

	// Was the NAME this binding declared mentioned anywhere in the function being parsed? The per-binding
	// bit first, then the name-scoped half — see `referencedNames` for why the second question exists and
	// why a per-binding bit structurally cannot answer it. The scan is reached only by a declaration one of
	// the two scans above is about to report or excuse, so a function whose bindings are all used never
	// walks the list at all.
	function bindingNameWasMentioned(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		return binding.referenced or self.nameIsListed(self.bodyState.referencedNames, name: binding.name)
	end 'bindingNameWasMentioned'

	// ⭐ **THE SINGLE WRITER OF E3012'S EVIDENCE** — the per-binding bit and the per-function name list,
	// together. Three doors call it (`requireBindingLive`, `parseAssignment`, `emitCaptureRead`); see
	// `VarInfo.referenced` for why a name can be mentioned in exactly three places none of the others sees.
	//
	// The bit is the DEDUP: a name reaches the list once per binding rather than once per mention, so a hot
	// read path pays one already-set test and nothing else.
	function markBindingReferenced(binding VarInfo)
		if binding.referenced 'alreadyRecorded'
			return
		end 'alreadyRecorded'

		binding.referenced = true
		self.bodyState.referencedNames.push(binding.name)
	end 'markBindingReferenced'

	// ⭐ **THE SINGLE WRITER OF E3077'S EVIDENCE**, the exact twin of `markBindingReferenced` above and
	// deduped the same way. Its doors are the ones that REQUIRE a binding to be writable and then let the
	// program through — see `VarInfo.mutabilityExercised` for the list and for why a receiver and an
	// argument are marked without asking whether the callee actually writes.
	function markMutabilityExercised(binding VarInfo)
		if binding.mutabilityExercised 'alreadyRecorded'
			return
		end 'alreadyRecorded'

		binding.mutabilityExercised = true
		self.bodyState.mutabilityExercisedNames.push(binding.name)
	end 'markMutabilityExercised'

	// The same mark reached by NAME, for the doors that hold a name rather than the binding — a call
	// ARGUMENT and a method RECEIVER, both of which carry the storage's name because E3207 needs it.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE GUARD IS THE SCOPE, NOT `mutableVars`.** `scope.contains` plus `binding.isMutable()` is the
	// question itself — only a `var` in scope can ever be an E3077 candidate, so a name that is not one (a
	// `let`, a parameter, a global, an undeclared token the sweep happened to walk past) has nothing to mark.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BINDING IT MARKS MAY BE A `let` THAT SHADOWS THAT `var`, AND THAT IS CORRECT.** The verdict
	// is name-scoped (see `mutabilityExercisedNames`), so what matters is that the NAME lands in the list —
	// which excuses the outer `var`.
	//
	// ⛔ `innermostMutableVar` — membership in the loop-phi tracking list — is a cheap stand-in for
	// "is this name a `var`?" and answers NO for a CELL-RESIDENT one: such a `var` is deliberately never
	// pushed into that list, because its pointer is loop-invariant and must mint no phi (see
	// `declareCellLocal`). That is E3077 *"variable 'n' is never reassigned; use 'let' instead of 'var'"* on
	// `var n = 0` + `setTo99(n)` — exactly the bindings pass-by-reference exists to serve. The scope answers
	// for both representations and agrees with the stand-in wherever the stand-in is right: a `let`, a
	// parameter and a self-field alias are all `BindingKind.let`.
	function markNameMutabilityExercised(name ByteArray)
		if not self.bodyState.scope.contains(name) 'namesNoLocalBinding'
			return
		end 'namesNoLocalBinding'

		let binding = self.bodyState.scope.getInfo(name)

		if not binding.isMutable() 'namesNoVarInScope'
			return
		end 'namesNoVarInScope'

		self.markMutabilityExercised(binding)
	end 'markNameMutabilityExercised'

	// Does `names` hold this name? A linear scan because these lists are short (one entry per binding that
	// recorded the fact) and each is consulted only for a declaration E3012 or E3077 is about to report or
	// excuse — a map would cost a hash of every name on the write path to save a walk on a path that runs a
	// handful of times per compile.
	//
	// ONE scan for the two lists rather than one function each: they answer different questions
	// (`referencedNames`, `mutabilityExercisedNames`) and are read through the two predicates that name
	// those questions, but "is this name in that list" is a single fact and a second copy of the walk would
	// be a second place for the name-scoped rule to be got wrong.
	function nameIsListed(names NameRoster, name ByteArray) returns bool
		for listed in names 'eachListed'
			if listed.equals(name) 'sameName'
				return true
			end 'sameName'
		end 'eachListed'

		return false
	end 'nameIsListed'

	// ⭐ **DOES AN INTERFACE THIS TYPE IMPLEMENTS REQUIRE A METHOD OF THIS NAME?** — the E3012 parameter
	// exemption. An implementer is forced to declare every parameter the contract names, even one this
	// particular implementation has no use for, so the unused check may not be applied to a method that
	// satisfies a requirement.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE WAIVER IS PER METHOD, NOT PER TYPE.** A sibling `function helper(unused Integer)` on the same
	// conforming type is nobody's requirement and is still checked — the two cases sit one beside the other
	// in `specs/interface-conformance.md`. And it waives the PARAMETERS only: a `for` binding inside an
	// interface method is the author's own, so it stays checked.
	//
	// ⚠ **"WAIVES THE PARAMETERS ONLY" IS A CONDITION SOMETHING HAS TO ASK.** `unusedCandidates` carries two kinds,
	// so the caller tests `candidate.kind` and `continue`s: a waived parameter must not end the scan before a
	// loop binding the contract has no say over. `interface-conformance.md`'s
	// `interface-method-loop-variable-still-errors` is red both ways round.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS ANSWERED AT PARSE TIME, AND NOT RE-EVALUATED WHOLE-PROGRAM.** A parse-time answer
	// would be a false negative if the implemented interface could be parsed after the conformer; the
	// declaration sweep completes before any body is parsed, so `signatures.interfaceMap()` is already
	// the WHOLE program here — the same store `requireInterfaceForParse` resolves a `where` constraint
	// through — and file order cannot move the answer. Its contribution to the parse memo's key is
	// already carried: `recordInterfaceDeclaration` mixes every requirement's name into the signature
	// hash.
	//
	// Requirements are collected TRANSITIVELY through `extends` (`witnessSlotsOf`, the memoized walk the
	// witness dispatch already uses), so a method satisfying an inherited requirement is waived too.
	function parametersAreInterfaceDictated(methodName ByteArray) returns bool
		let typeName = try self.enclosingType.typeName() otherwise return false

		for declIndex in 0 upto self.artifact.conformances.count() 'eachDecl'
			let conformance = try self.artifact.conformances.get(declIndex) otherwise panic("parametersAreInterfaceDictated: declIndex {declIndex} is below self.artifact.conformances.count()")

			if not conformance.typeName.equals(typeName) 'anotherType'
				continue
			end 'anotherType'

			// ⚠ **THE INNER WALK IS A HELPER, AND THAT IS THE SAME RULE ONE LEVEL DOWN.** Left inline it read
			// `conformance.interfaces`, which keeps the OUTER element's borrow live across
			// `requirementSlotsOf` — a write of `artifact`. Handing the conformance to a function makes it a
			// PARAMETER there, which carries no borrow of `artifact` at all, so the walk is free to memoize.
			if self.conformanceRequiresMethod(try self.artifact.conformances.get(declIndex) otherwise panic("parametersAreInterfaceDictated: declIndex {declIndex} is below self.artifact.conformances.count()"), methodName: methodName) 'thisMethodIsRequired'
				return true
			end 'thisMethodIsRequired'
		end 'eachDecl'

		return false
	end 'parametersAreInterfaceDictated'

	// Does any interface this conformance declares REQUIRE `methodName`? Split out of the walk above for the
	// reason its call site records: the conformance arrives as a parameter, so the memoizing
	// `requirementSlotsOf` is not a write standing between an element borrow and its use.
	function conformanceRequiresMethod(conformance ConformanceDecl, methodName ByteArray) returns bool
		for interfaceIndex in 0 upto conformance.interfaces.count() 'eachInterface'
			if witnessSlotsRequireMethod(self.requirementSlotsOf((try conformance.interfaces.get(interfaceIndex) otherwise panic("conformanceRequiresMethod: interfaceIndex {interfaceIndex} is below conformance.interfaces.count()")).interfaceName), methodName: methodName) 'required'
				return true
			end 'required'
		end 'eachInterface'

		return false
	end 'conformanceRequiresMethod'

	// The TRANSITIVE requirement list of `interfaceName` — the memoized walk a witness dispatch already
	// takes (`witnessSlotsOf`), so a method satisfying a requirement inherited through `extends` is seen.
	//
	// An `implements` naming something no file declares requires nothing, and that name is E3015's to
	// report (`ConformanceCheck`), not this reader's to guess about — so a miss is the SHARED empty list
	// rather than an allocation or a refusal.
	function requirementSlotsOf(interfaceName ByteArray) returns CollectedInterfaceMethodArray
		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: interfaceName) 'resolve'
			found(iface) then return self.witnessSlotsOf(iface)
			notFound then return CollectedInterfaceMethodArray.create(0)
		end 'resolve'
	end 'requirementSlotsOf'

	// Parse a comma-separated parameter list `name type[, name type]*` into the
	// parallel `paramNames`/`paramTypes` lists (empty when the list is empty). Each
	// parameter is `name type` — a bare identifier followed by a type reference. A
	// `= <default>` after the type is captured for `owner` (see `ParamDefaultOwner`).
	// The caller has already consumed the `(` and consumes the closing `)`.
	//
	// ⭐ **`paramSurfaces` IS THE PRE-ERASURE SPELLING COLUMN**, the shape
	// `FuncSignature.paramAggregateNames` already established for a union parameter's name: a fact the
	// declaration holds and `parseTypeReference` is about to destroy, captured at the last moment it is
	// readable and carried alongside the columns it describes. It needs no signature-index entry, because the
	// only reader is `bindParameters` — and both live inside one `parseFunction`.
	function parseFunctionParameters(paramNames NameRoster, paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray, paramSurfaces DeclaredSurfaceArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexArray, paramCapabilities ParamCapabilityArray, paramMarkers BoolArray, markerTokens TokenIndexArray, owner ParamDefaultOwner, paramTypeOffer ClosureParamOffer) throws SourceParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.rightParen) 'noParams'
			return
		end 'noParams'

		while true 'paramLoop'
			// ⭐ A KEYWORD MAY BE A PARAMETER'S NAME — `function identity(type Integer)`, the shape
			// `keyword-parameter-names.md` is written for. A parameter list admits nothing but `name type` in
			// this position, so the keyword is a name; `keywordIsAName` learns the same fact for the token
			// scans, or a `function f(end Integer)` would read as a block CLOSE that never opened.

			let nameTok = try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.declaration)
			try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
			let marked = self.mutableMarkerAt(self.pos)

			if marked 'aMarkedParameter'
				markerTokens.push(self.pos)
				self.advance()
			end 'aMarkedParameter'

			let capabilityTok = self.current()
			let writtenCapability = try self.readParamCapability()

			// Asked at the cursor, which sits on the type's first token — the last instant the spelling
			// `__ManagedMemory` still exists, at any depth (`declaredSurfaceAt`). `slotElementsFromAliases:
			// true`: the tolerant sweep skips a parameter list wholesale (`skipBalancedParens`), so this door
			// is the REAL parse's alone, the whole-program alias registry is complete, and the answer is
			// consumed by `bindParameters` in this same pass rather than stored for another one.
			let declaredSurface = self.declaredSurfaceAt(self.pos, slotElementsFromAliases: true)

			// ⭐ **AND THE TYPE'S OWN FIRST TOKEN, FOR THE SAME REASON ONE LINE UP.** A parameter declared at a
			// PARAMETERIZED interface type is resolved to the bare `interfaceRef(Base)` by `instanceDenotedType`,
			// which is where its `with` arguments stop existing — so the position that still spells them is captured
			// here and read by `bindParameters`, exactly as `paramSurfaces` is. An index rather than the resolved
			// instance because an OMITTED type resolves nothing at all and there is no absent-instance to invent.
			let paramTypeStart = self.pos
			let paramType = try self.parameterTypeOrOffered(paramTypeOffer, paramIndex: paramNames.count() as ParamCount, nameTok: nameTok)

			if writtenCapability != ParamCapability.mutable 'notTheByReferenceWord'
				try self.requireCapabilityFits(writtenCapability, wordTok: capabilityTok, spelled: paramType)
			end 'notTheByReferenceWord'

			let capability = ParamCapability.mutable if marked and writtenCapability == ParamCapability.unmarked else self.aliasCapabilityOr(writtenCapability, typeTok: self.tokenAt(paramTypeStart))

			// A default value is captured, not parsed here: its expression becomes the whole body of a
			// synthesized nullary helper, parsed at the end of the file by `parseDefaultHelpers`, and the
			// call site emits a call to that helper for every argument it omits (see
			// `SignatureIndex.ParamDefaultInfo` for the mechanism and why it is a function).
			//
			// It is deferred to end-of-file because a helper is a function: parsed mid-declaration, it would
			// need the enclosing body's state swapped out and back, as `parseClosureExpression` does.
			// Drained between top-level declarations no body state is live, so the helper begins its own and
			// nothing has to be put back. That also gives the default the scope it needs: the helper's body
			// cannot see the callee's parameters or locals, nor its enclosing `type`, because a default is
			// evaluated at the call site and belongs to neither frame.
			// ⭐ **`_` IS EXEMPT, AND IT IS THE ONLY EXEMPTION.** Two `_` parameters cannot collide:
			// `bindParameters` binds neither, so no body can read one (E2004). A signature needs one `_` per
			// parameter it does not want, and `function make(_ Integer = 1, _ String = "n", item T)` is the
			// ordinary spelling of "two defaults ahead of the one that matters". Refusing it
			// `E2014 duplicate parameter name '_'` would reject a legal declaration.
			//
			// Keyed on the name being EXACTLY `_`: an underscore PREFIX is an ordinary identifier, so two `_x`
			// parameters are still E2014, as is any repeat of a real name.
			//
			// A call reaches each `_` slot separately; see `argSlotPosition`.
			if not nameTok.value.equals(DiscardBindingName) and paramNames.contains(nameTok.value) 'duplicate'
				throw SourceParseError.duplicateParameter(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'duplicate'

			// After the duplicate check, so a declaration already refused captures nothing — and BEFORE the
			// pushes below, because `paramNames.count()` is this parameter's own CALL-ALIGNED position only
			// while its own name is still absent from the list.
			if self.at(TokenKind.equals) 'hasDefault'
				try self.captureParamDefault(owner, paramName: nameTok.value, paramIndex: paramNames.count() as ParamCount, paramType: paramType)
			end 'hasDefault'

			paramNames.push(nameTok.value)
			paramTypes.push(paramType)
			paramRanges.push(SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
			paramSurfaces.push(declaredSurface)
			paramTypeStarts.push(paramTypeStart)
			paramCapabilities.push(capability)
			paramMarkers.push(marked)

			// ⚠ NO COUNT CHECK HERE. Testing `paramNames.count()` would be a SECOND, and WEAKER, spelling of the
			// ceiling: it counts only the parameters the source writes, while the ABI also passes a witness
			// per interface-typed parameter, a layout descriptor and one witness per constraint. The
			// ceiling is asked exactly once, of the ABI count, by `requireAbiParamCount`; both callers of this
			// routine (`parseFunction`, `parseClosureExpression`) ask it after they know their own full count.
			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endParams'
				break
			end 'endParams'

			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'paramLoop'
	end 'parseFunctionParameters'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `Base with Args` INSTANCE THE DECLARED TYPE AT `index` NAMES** — the fact
	// `ProgramSignatures.instanceDenotedType` destroys when it collapses an interface instance to the bare
	// `interfaceRef(Base)` the value is typed with, recovered from the one thing that survives: the tokens.
	// `MapError.keyNotFound` for a type that names no generic alias, which is every ordinary declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO NAME SHAPES ARE `parseTypeReference`'s TWO ALIAS ARMS, IN ITS ORDER, AND THEY MUST STAY
	// THAT ORDER.** An INNER alias resolves under its base-qualified name (`Array.ElementIterable`) and a
	// file-scope one under its bare name, and the inner one wins — a nested `typealias` shadows a top-level
	// one of the same spelling. Asking bare-first would silently type a shared generic body's iteration
	// against some other file's instantiation. This is the only restatement of that rule; the JOIN it reaches
	// (`genericAliasDenotedType`'s two calls) is shared rather than spelled again.
	//
	// ⚠ It declines a non-identifier by TOKEN KIND, which is what lets the receiver's slot hold the
	// signature's `(` rather than an index whose meaning depends on what a name happens to denote.
	function declaredExistentialInstanceAt(index ElementIndex) returns ExistentialClaim throws AbsentError
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.identifier 'namesNoTypeAtAll'
			throw AbsentError.absent
		end 'namesNoTypeAtAll'

		let name = self.tokenAt(index).value

		if self.namesInnerAliasHere(name) 'innerAliasOfTheEnclosingType'
			return try self.existentialClaimOf(self.innerAliasKey(name), readerFilePath: self.job.path)
		end 'innerAliasOfTheEnclosingType'

		return try self.existentialClaimOf(name, readerFilePath: self.job.path)
	end 'declaredExistentialInstanceAt'

	function existentialClaimOf(aliasName ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ExistentialClaim throws AbsentError
		if not self.signatures.isAliasOfFormFor(aliasName, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, wanted: FormAliasKind.generic) 'namesNoInstance'
			throw AbsentError.absent
		end 'namesNoInstance'

		match self.signatures.writtenGenericAliasDecl(aliasName, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'declared'
			undeclared then panic("existentialClaimOf: '{String.from(aliasName)}' is a generic alias as '{readerFilePath.toString()}' reads it, yet no declaration of it is held — `isAliasOfFormFor` and `scopedGenericAliasDecl` read one registry")
			declared(decl) then return ExistentialClaim.create(self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(aliasName, readerFilePath: readerFilePath), scopeFilePath: decl.filePath)
		end 'declared'
	end 'existentialClaimOf'

	function declaredReturnExistentialClaim(callee ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns ExistentialClaim throws AbsentError
		let aliasName = try self.signatures.returnAliasNameOf(callee)

		let written = try self.signatures.returnInnerAliasOf(callee, aliasName: aliasName) otherwise aliasName

		return try self.existentialClaimOf(written, readerFilePath: declFilePath)
	end 'declaredReturnExistentialClaim'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SIGNATURE'S HALF OF THE SAME FACT** — which PARAMETERS were declared at a parameterized
	// existential, and at which instance. `bindParameters` records this per VALUE, for the callee's own body;
	// the caller asking *"may this concrete argument widen into that parameter?"* stands in a different
	// function and a different SSA space, so it reads the SIGNATURE. Both derive it from the same
	// `paramTypeStarts` column through the same `declaredExistentialInstanceAt`, which is what keeps one
	// parameter from being read two ways.
	//
	// The `interfaceRef` gate is the same one `bindParameters` applies and for the same reason: only an
	// existential can have had `with` arguments to lose, and a generic alias over a STRUCT base keeps its
	// arguments on the type where every other door already reads them.
	//
	// ⚠ The walk's list is allocated up front and COLLAPSED to the shared empty when nothing claimed, rather
	// than materialized copy-on-write. Copy-on-write would mean reassigning the walk's own binding from
	// inside a branch, which is the measured `E9001 … does not dominate` shape — see
	// `materializedPendingDefaultHelpers`, the one helper still built around it — and the claim here is
	// discovered inside an `if let` that no `continue` guard can flatten without asking
	// `declaredExistentialInstanceAt` twice. The cost is one empty array per parsed function, which is
	// strictly less than the full-length column `paramAggregateNamesOf` builds beside it.
	function paramExistentialSitesOf(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexArray) returns ParamExistentialSiteArray
		let sites = ParamExistentialSiteArray.create(0)

		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("paramExistentialSitesOf: paramTypes.get OOB at {i} below the count just read from the same array")

			if maxonTypeTag(paramType) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
				continue
			end 'notAnExistential'

			let start = paramTypeStartAt(paramTypeStarts, position: i)

			if let claim = try self.declaredExistentialInstanceAt(start) 'namesAnInstantiation'
				sites.push(ParamExistentialSite.create(i as ParamCount, instance: claim.instance))
			end 'namesAnInstantiation'
		end 'eachParam'

		return sites
	end 'paramExistentialSitesOf'

	// The RETURN slot's half — at most one instance, so an EMPTY column is "this function's return names no
	// instantiation" and there is no absent-value to invent. A `void` return and a non-existential one leave it
	// empty through the same predicate, exactly as a non-existential parameter is skipped above.

	function returnExistentialSitesOf(returnType MaxonReturnType, returnTypeStart ElementIndex) returns GenericInstanceIdArray
		if maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'returnsNothing'
			return GenericInstanceIdArray.create(0)
		end 'returnsNothing'

		if maxonReturnTypeTag(returnType) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
			return GenericInstanceIdArray.create(0)
		end 'notAnExistential'

		let sites = GenericInstanceIdArray.create(0)

		if let claim = try self.declaredExistentialInstanceAt(returnTypeStart) 'namesAnInstantiation'
			sites.push(claim.instance)
		end 'namesAnInstantiation'

		return sites
	end 'returnExistentialSitesOf'

	// ⭐ **THE TOKEN INDEX OF THE DECLARED RETURN TYPE'S OWN NAME** — `self.pos` is the `returns` keyword and
	// the type follows it, past a result capability word (`mutable`, `isolated` or `immutable`) when one is written. Spelled once
	// because two readers want it: `returnDeclaredSurface` reads the buffer spelling off it and
	// `returnExistentialSitesOf` reads the `with` arguments, and a drift between the two would have them
	// describing different tokens of one declaration.
	//
	// With NO return clause it answers `self.pos` itself, which is the newline or the `throws` keyword — a slot
	// that names no type BY TOKEN KIND, which is the same guarantee the receiver's slot rests on (see
	// `parseFunction`'s `receiverParam`) rather than a claim about what some identifier means.
	function returnTypeTokenIndex() returns ElementIndex
		if not self.at(TokenKind.returns) 'noReturnClause'
			return self.pos
		end 'noReturnClause'

		return self.pos + 2 if self.paramCapabilityAt(self.pos + 1) != ParamCapability.unmarked else self.pos + 1
	end 'returnTypeTokenIndex'

	// ⭐ **THE DECLARED TYPE, OR THE ONE THE CALL ARGUMENT OFFERS AT `paramIndex` (`function(p) gives …`).** An
	// OMITTED type is recognized by the parameter list's own punctuation standing where a type would begin — the
	// `,` before the next parameter or the `)` that closes the list — and needs no lookahead: no type reference
	// starts with either token.
	//
	// ⭐ The two refusals are different mistakes and say so. An offer too short for `paramIndex` means a
	// position DID supply types and this parameter is past them — E2003. `none` means nothing supplies a type
	// here, including every NAMED function's parameter list, and the sentence names the construct that does.
	function parameterTypeOrOffered(offer ClosureParamOffer, paramIndex ParamCount, nameTok Token) returns MaxonType throws SourceParseError
		if not (self.at(TokenKind.comma) or self.at(TokenKind.rightParen)) 'declaredType'
			return try self.parseTypeReference(BarePrimitiveVoice.asAType)
		end 'declaredType'

		match offer 'offer'
			offered(paramTypes, _, aliasName) then return try paramTypes.get(paramIndex) otherwise throw SourceParseError.uninferrableClosureParam(String.from(nameTok.value), aliasName: String.from(aliasName), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			deferred then return MaxonType.unresolved
			none then break
		end 'offer'

		throw SourceParseError.unsupported("parameter '{String.from(nameTok.value)}' with no type — the compiler infers an omitted parameter type only for a closure literal passed where the parameter is declared with a function type; every other parameter declares its type", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
	end 'parameterTypeOrOffered'

	// Capture one parameter's `= <default>` for its owning declaration and leave the cursor on the token
	// that ENDS it (the `,` before the next parameter, or the list's `)`), so the parameter loop resumes
	// exactly as it would have without a default. The cursor arrives ON the `=`.
	//
	// The two non-function owners are refused here rather than in their own callers, because the refusal is
	// a property of the PARAMETER LIST's owner and this is the one routine that reads a parameter list —
	// stated in either caller it would be a rule about a construct written somewhere other than where the
	// construct is parsed, and the third caller would silently not have it.
	function captureParamDefault(owner ParamDefaultOwner, paramName ByteArray, paramIndex ParamCount, paramType MaxonType) throws SourceParseError
		let eqTok = self.current()

		match owner 'owner'
			closureLiteral then throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a default value on closure parameter '{String.from(paramName)}' — a closure is called INDIRECTLY, through a function value, so no call site has a callee name to look the default up by. Give the closure's caller the value explicitly", line: eqTok.line, column: eqTok.column)
			interfaceRequirement then throw SourceParseError.unsupported("a default value on interface requirement parameter '{String.from(paramName)}' — a witness dispatch slots its arguments against the INTERFACE's formals, which are in no signature registry, so no dispatch site can read a default from the requirement. Declare the default on each conforming type's method", line: eqTok.line, column: eqTok.column)
			declaredFunction(name, declaredAt) then try self.requireDeclarationIsTheSweepsKept(name, declaredAt: declaredAt)
		end 'owner'

		let ownerName = paramDefaultOwnerName(owner)

		// ⚠ **THE HELPER'S NAME IS NOT MINTED HERE — IT IS READ BACK FROM THE SWEEP AT THE DRAIN**
		// (`parseDefaultHelper`). The sweep is the PARAMETER mint's one home, because it is the mint a CALL
		// SITE in another file must be able to see; a second one here would be the same name derived twice,
		// and the one input the two could disagree on is the parameter's index, which the sweep and the real
		// parse count from different starting points. Reading it turns the agreement into a lookup that
		// either finds the sweep's answer or says so. (A FIELD default's name IS minted at its capture, and
		// safely — see `DefaultHelperOwner` for the property a field name has that a position does not.)
		let exprEnd = self.expressionEndFrom(self.pos + 1)

		// ⭐⭐ **A CALLER-LOCATION DEFAULT SYNTHESIZES NOTHING, SO IT CAPTURES NOTHING.** There is no
		// expression for a helper to return: the sweep published `callerLine`/`callerFile` at this slot from
		// this same classifier, and `fillArgsFromParamDefaults` emits the constant at each call. Pushing a
		// pending helper here would declare a function under a name the sweep never minted — the exact
		// disagreement `requireSweptHelperName` panics on.
		if isCallerLocationDefault(self.callerLocationDefaultAt(self.pos, exprEnd: exprEnd)) 'materializedAtEachCallSite'
			self.pos = exprEnd
			return
		end 'materializedAtEachCallSite'

		try self.requireNoEmbeddedCallerLocation(self.pos, exprEnd: exprEnd)
		self.pendingDefaultHelpers = materializedPendingDefaultHelpers(self.pendingDefaultHelpers, anchor: self.anchors.pendingDefaultHelpers)
		self.pendingDefaultHelpers.push(PendingDefaultHelper.create(DefaultHelperOwner.parameter(ownerName, paramIndex: paramIndex), declaredType: paramType, exprStart: self.pos + 1, exprEnd: exprEnd, enclosingType: self.enclosingType.clone()))
