	// ⭐⭐ **RE-OPEN THE DECLARATION SCOPE A CAPTURED DEFAULT WAS WRITTEN INSIDE** — the drain runs at
	// the end of `parseModule`, where the type that declared the default closed long ago, so every scope
	// `enterTypeScope`/`openTypeParamScope` install has to be put back or the expression is re-parsed as if
	// it had been written at top level.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONLY THE NAME TRAVELS; EVERYTHING ELSE IS READ BACK OFF THE INDEX.** A type's `uses` parameters
	// and its `where` constraints are ON its `StructLayout`, whole-program and complete by the drain, so
	// capturing copies of them at the declaration would be two records of one fact.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ONE SCOPE THAT CANNOT BE READ BACK IS `enclosingInnerAliases`** — it is built by the BODY WALK
	// and is not a column of anything — which is why `innerAliasesFromIndex` exists: inside a drained default
	// the same question is answered from the three whole-program alias registries instead
	// (`namesInnerAliasHere`, the one home of it). That is not a widening: `declaresInnerAlias` is keyed
	// `<Type>.<member>`, so it answers about exactly the declarations the body walk would have recorded.
	//
	// ⚠ An INTERFACE EXTENSION's associated-type substitution is deliberately NOT restored — it is a
	// property of the (extension, conformer) PAIR rather than of the conformer, and neither the pending
	// record nor the layout names the extension. A default expression inside an extension method that writes
	// the interface's associated type therefore reports `E3011 Unknown type`, which is a refusal with a
	// position and not a wrong type.
