		self.enclosingTypeParams = layout.typeParamNames
		self.enclosingWhereConstraints = layout.whereConstraints

		// A TYPE's own declaration carries no extension clause; the extension half is the extension's alone.
		self.enclosingExtensionConstraints = WhereConstraintArray.create(0)
	end 'installDeclaringTypeScope'

	// ⭐ **THE NAME AND THE TIER — the ONLY two things a parameter default's helper and a field default's
	// helper differ in.** Everything else about them is one function (`parseDefaultHelperBody`), which is
	// what "a default value is a synthesized nullary function" means when it is said once instead of twice.
	function parseDefaultHelper(pending PendingDefaultHelper) throws SourceParseError
		match pending.owner 'owner'
			// ⭐ GLOBAL: a defaulted parameter of an `export function` is omitted by call sites in OTHER
			// files, and each of them emits a call to this helper by name.
			parameter(sourceName, paramIndex) then try self.parseDefaultHelperBody(self.sweptParamDefaultHelperName(sourceName, paramIndex: paramIndex), visibility: Visibility.global, pending: pending)

			// ⭐ FILE, and the difference from the parameter tier is a real fact rather than caution: a struct
			// literal may only be written inside the type's OWN body (`requireConstructible`'s E3076), so
			// every call to a field default's helper is in the file that declares the type. A lifted closure
			// is published the same way for the same reason.
			field(helperName, _, _) then try self.parseDefaultHelperBody(helperName, visibility: Visibility.unexported, pending: pending)
		end 'owner'
	end 'parseDefaultHelper'

	// One helper: `function <the minted name>() returns <the declared type>` whose entire body is
	// `return <the default expression>`.
	//
	// The body state begins afresh (`beginDeclaredBody`), as at every body's entry. Nothing is saved:
	// the caller restores only the cursor, because between top-level declarations no other body state
	// is live.
	//
	// The body goes through `parseCheckedValueReturn`, so the default meets every rule a `return`
	// meets — which is what makes `name String = 42` an ordinary return-type mismatch and
	// `factor Number = 2` an ordinary widening, with no rule written twice. That is exactly as true of
	// a FIELD's `var m = Maker.build()` whose factory returns another type, which is why the field
	// door needed no type rule of its own (`returnMismatchError` words it).
	function parseDefaultHelperBody(helperName ByteArray, visibility Visibility, pending PendingDefaultHelper) throws SourceParseError
		self.pos = pending.exprStart
		let anchor = self.current()

		// The declaration scope the default was written inside, re-opened before a token of it is read
		// (see `PendingDefaultHelper.enclosingType`).
		self.restoreDeclaringTypeScope(pending.enclosingType)

		// Resolved HERE rather than at the capture: a `named` declared type is re-tagged `structRef` only
		// once the whole-program struct registry is complete, which at capture time — mid-declaration, and
		// possibly above the `type` being named — it is not.
